window.CURRICULUM_10 = [{"id":"realnumbers10","index":0,"name":"Real Numbers","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 1","emoji":"🔢","palette":{"from":"#60a5fa","to":"#1e3a8a"},"lessons":[{"id":"rn1","title":"The Remainder Riddle","icon":"➗","kind":"lesson","story":"You step through the gates of Numeria and find an ancient stone door that will only open for a traveller who understands remainders. A carved inscription hints that any treasure pile 'a' can be split into equal groups of size 'b', always leaving a remainder smaller than the group size. Solve the riddle to pass.","teach":[{"title":"Divide and Conquer","body":"For any positive integer a divided by another positive integer b, you always get a remainder r that is smaller than b. This is exactly the long-division process you have used for years — nothing new, just a name for a familiar idea.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"The Remainder Rule","body":"The remainder r must satisfy 0 ≤ r < b. For example, dividing 17 by 5 gives quotient 3 and remainder 2, since 17 = 5×3 + 2, and indeed 2 is smaller than 5.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a positive integer a is divided by a positive integer b, the remainder r always satisfies:","options":["0 ≤ r < b","r is always greater than b","r always equals b","r is always 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"The remainder of a division is always smaller than the divisor — that's what makes the division complete."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"When 17 is divided by 5, what is the remainder?","answer":2,"explain":"17 = 5×3 + 2, so the remainder is 2, and 2 < 5 as required."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"When 29 is divided by 6, what is the quotient?","answer":4,"explain":"29 = 6×4 + 5, so the quotient is 4 and the remainder is 5."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"This 'divide and leave a smaller remainder' idea is essentially the process you already know as:","options":["long division","addition","the factor tree method","squaring numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is simply a formal way of describing the everyday long division process."}]},{"id":"rn2","title":"The Prime Factor Tree","icon":"🌳","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Numeria ruins stands a gate shaped like a great tree. To pass, you must split each number into its prime 'roots' by repeatedly breaking composite branches — a factor tree — until only primes remain.","teach":[{"title":"Building Blocks of Numbers","body":"Every natural number greater than 1 can be broken down into prime factors using a factor tree — keep splitting composite branches until only primes are left. For instance, 2 = 2, 4 = 2×2, and 253 = 11×23.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"A Giant Example","body":"Factorising 32760 with a factor tree gives 2×2×2×3×3×5×7×13, which we write compactly as 2³×3²×5×7×13 — a product of powers of primes.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Even Huge Numbers Split","body":"The number 123456789 factorises as 3²×3803×3607, where 3803 and 3607 are themselves prime. No matter how large the number, this splitting process always works.","visual":"🔍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the factorisation 32760 = 2³×3²×5×7×13, what power of 2 appears?","answer":3,"explain":"32760 = 2×2×2×3×3×5×7×13 = 2³×3²×5×7×13, so 2 appears to the power 3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"253 factorises into primes as:","options":["11×23","10×25","11×22","13×19"],"answer":0,"explain":"253 = 11×23, and both 11 and 23 are prime."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"123456789 = 3² × 3803 × 3607. For this to be a complete prime factorisation, what must be true of 3803 and 3607?","options":["Both must be prime numbers","Both must be even","Both must be multiples of 3","One of them must be 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"A prime factorisation is only complete once every factor listed is itself prime."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"4 = 2×2. Written as 2 raised to a power, what is that power?","answer":2,"explain":"4 = 2² since 2×2 = 4."}]},{"id":"rn3","title":"The Unique Code","icon":"🏆","kind":"lesson","story":"The tree-gate's guardian reveals the vault's deepest secret: every composite number has exactly one true prime 'code'. Two travellers may write the factors in different orders, but no number can ever have two genuinely different sets of prime factors.","teach":[{"title":"Theorem 1.1 — The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic","body":"Every composite number can be expressed as a product of primes, and this factorisation is unique, apart from the order in which the prime factors occur. So 2×3×5×7 is regarded as the same factorisation as 3×5×7×2.","visual":"🏆"},{"title":"Why 4ⁿ Never Ends in 0","body":"For a number to end in the digit 0, it must be divisible by 5, so 5 must appear in its prime factorisation. But 4ⁿ = (2²)ⁿ = 2^(2n), so the only prime factor of 4ⁿ is 2. By the uniqueness part of the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, 5 can never appear — so 4ⁿ never ends in 0.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"The Same Logic for 6ⁿ","body":"6ⁿ = (2×3)ⁿ = 2ⁿ×3ⁿ, so its only prime factors are 2 and 3 — never 5. By the same uniqueness argument, 6ⁿ can never end in the digit 0 either, for any natural number n.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, the prime factorisation of a composite number is unique:","options":["apart from the order of the prime factors","only for even numbers","only if the number is less than 100","apart from which primes are used"],"answer":0,"explain":"The set of prime factors and their powers is fixed; only the order in which we write them can differ."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can 4ⁿ never end in the digit 0?","options":["Because 4ⁿ = 2^(2n) has no prime factor 5, but ending in 0 requires a factor of 5","Because 4 is an even number","Because n must be irrational","Because 4ⁿ is always odd"],"answer":0,"explain":"A number ending in 0 must be divisible by 5, but 4ⁿ's only prime factor is 2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By the same reasoning as 4ⁿ, which prime never appears in the factorisation of 6ⁿ?","options":["5","2","3","6"],"answer":0,"explain":"6ⁿ = 2ⁿ×3ⁿ, so its only prime factors are 2 and 3 — 5 never appears, so 6ⁿ never ends in 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"4² written as 2^k — what is the value of k?","answer":4,"explain":"4² = (2²)² = 2⁴, so k = 4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"7×11×13+13 can be written as 13×(7×11+1). What does this tell us about the number?","options":["It is composite, since it has a factor other than 1 and itself (13)","It is prime","It is irrational","It equals 13 exactly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Any number that can be written as 13 times another integer greater than 1 has 13 as a factor, so it is composite."}]},{"id":"rn4","title":"HCF & LCM Vault","icon":"🔑","kind":"lesson","story":"Two paths of light converge on a vault door that only opens when you supply both the greatest common factor and the smallest common multiple of the numbers etched above it. Time to put the prime factor trees to work.","teach":[{"title":"HCF and LCM from Prime Powers","body":"Once two numbers are written as products of prime powers, the HCF is the product of the smallest power of each common prime, and the LCM is the product of the greatest power of each prime involved. For 6=2¹×3¹ and 20=2²×5¹, HCF(6,20)=2 and LCM(6,20)=2²×3×5=60.","visual":"🔑"},{"title":"A Powerful Shortcut","body":"For any two positive integers a and b: HCF(a,b) × LCM(a,b) = a × b. Check: HCF(6,20)×LCM(6,20) = 2×60 = 120 = 6×20.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"A Bigger Example","body":"For 96 = 2⁵×3 and 404 = 2²×101, the HCF is 2² = 4. Using the shortcut, LCM(96,404) = (96×404)/4 = 9696.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"6 = 2¹×3¹ and 20 = 2²×5¹. What is HCF(6, 20)?","answer":2,"explain":"The only common prime is 2, and its smallest power in either number is 2¹."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using HCF(6,20) = 2, what is LCM(6, 20)?","answer":60,"explain":"LCM(6,20) = 2²×3×5 = 60, and indeed 2×60 = 120 = 6×20."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"96 = 2⁵×3 and 404 = 2²×101. What is HCF(96, 404)?","answer":4,"explain":"The only common prime is 2, and its smallest power is 2², giving HCF = 4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using HCF(96,404) = 4, find LCM(96, 404) = (96×404)/4.","answer":9696,"explain":"96×404 = 38784, and 38784/4 = 9696."}]},{"id":"rn5","title":"Three-Number Trial","icon":"🧱","kind":"lesson","story":"The vault guardian raises the stakes: three numbers now glow on the wall, and the handy a×b=HCF×LCM trick you just learned seems to misbehave. A circular racetrack outside the ruins hides another test of timing.","teach":[{"title":"HCF and LCM of Three Numbers","body":"The same idea extends to three numbers: 6=2×3, 72=2³×3², 120=2³×3×5. The HCF takes the smallest power of each common prime: HCF(6,72,120)=2¹×3¹=6. The LCM takes the greatest power of each prime present: LCM(6,72,120)=2³×3²×5=360.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"The Product Rule Breaks for Three","body":"Notice that 6×72×120 = 51840, which is NOT equal to HCF(6,72,120)×LCM(6,72,120) = 6×360 = 2160. The neat a×b = HCF×LCM shortcut only works for pairs of numbers, not three or more.","visual":"⚠️"},{"title":"Racing Around the Track","body":"Sonia takes 18 minutes and Ravi takes 12 minutes to complete one round of a circular field, starting together from the same point and going the same direction. They will next meet at the starting point after LCM(18,12) minutes.","visual":"🏃"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"6=2×3, 72=2³×3², 120=2³×3×5. What is HCF(6, 72, 120)?","answer":6,"explain":"The smallest common powers are 2¹ and 3¹, giving HCF = 2×3 = 6."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is LCM(6, 72, 120) = 2³×3²×5?","answer":360,"explain":"2³×3²×5 = 8×9×5 = 360."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For three numbers 6, 72 and 120, does HCF×LCM equal the product of the three numbers?","options":["No — that shortcut only holds for two numbers, not three","Yes, always","Only when all three numbers are prime","Only when the numbers are equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"6×72×120 = 51840, but HCF(6,72,120)×LCM(6,72,120) = 6×360 = 2160 — they are not equal."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Sonia takes 18 minutes and Ravi takes 12 minutes for one round of a circular track, starting together. After how many minutes will they next meet at the starting point? (Find LCM(18,12))","answer":36,"explain":"18=2×3², 12=2²×3, so LCM(18,12)=2²×3²=36 minutes."}]},{"id":"rn6","title":"The Irrational Frontier","icon":"♾️","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the vault lies the Irrational Frontier, where the guardian challenges you to tame √2 into a simple fraction. Every attempt seems to collapse into contradiction — and that collapse turns out to be the proof itself.","teach":[{"title":"Theorem 1.2 — A Key Divisibility Fact","body":"If p is a prime number and p divides a², then p must divide a itself, where a is a positive integer. This follows from the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic: the only primes appearing in a²'s factorisation are the same primes that appear in a's factorisation.","visual":"🗝️"},{"title":"Proving √2 is Irrational","body":"Assume, to the contrary, that √2 = a/b where a and b are coprime integers. Squaring gives a² = 2b², so 2 divides a², and by Theorem 1.2, 2 divides a. Writing a=2c leads to b²=2c², so 2 divides b too — but then a and b share the factor 2, contradicting that they are coprime. So √2 is irrational.","visual":"♾️"},{"title":"Contradiction is the Weapon","body":"This method is called 'proof by contradiction': assume the opposite of what you want to prove, then show it leads to something impossible. This exact technique proves Theorem 1.3, that √2 is irrational.","visual":"🛡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Theorem 1.2 states: if a prime p divides a², then:","options":["p divides a","p divides a+1","a² is prime","p equals a"],"answer":0,"explain":"This divisibility fact is the key tool used to prove square roots of primes are irrational."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the proof that √2 is irrational, we assume √2 = a/b where a and b are:","options":["coprime (no common factor other than 1)","both even","both prime","both equal to 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Starting with a and b coprime is what makes the later contradiction (both sharing factor 2) meaningful."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the proof, once a=2c, we find b²=2c², meaning 2 divides b. This contradicts the assumption that:","options":["a and b are coprime","b is positive","a is even","2 is prime"],"answer":0,"explain":"If both a and b are divisible by 2, they cannot be coprime, which was our starting assumption."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The proof technique used to show √2 is irrational is called:","options":["proof by contradiction","proof by induction","direct proof","proof by factor tree"],"answer":0,"explain":"We assume √2 is rational and show this assumption leads to an impossible conclusion."}]},{"id":"rn7","title":"Irrational Combinations","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"One last trial guards the road to the boss chamber: prove that other numbers built from √2 and √3 also refuse to become simple fractions, no matter how you combine them with rationals.","teach":[{"title":"√3 is Irrational Too","body":"Using the same contradiction method: assume √3=a/b with a,b coprime. Squaring gives a²=3b², so 3 divides a², and by Theorem 1.2, 3 divides a. Writing a=3c gives b²=3c², so 3 divides b too — contradicting that a,b are coprime. So √3 is irrational.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Rational + Irrational = Irrational","body":"If 5-√3 were rational (say a/b), rearranging gives √3 = 5 - a/b, which would be rational since 5, a and b are integers. But √3 is irrational — a contradiction. So 5-√3 must be irrational.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"Rational × Irrational = Irrational","body":"If 3√2 were rational (say a/b), rearranging gives √2 = a/(3b), a ratio of integers, making √2 rational. But √2 is irrational — a contradiction. So 3√2 is irrational.","visual":"✖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To prove √3 is irrational, assuming √3=a/b (coprime) leads to a²=3b². By Theorem 1.2, this means:","options":["3 divides a","3 divides b only","a is even","b is prime"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since 3 divides a², Theorem 1.2 with p=3 tells us 3 must divide a."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"5 - √3 is irrational because if it were rational, rearranging would show that ___ is also rational, which is false.","options":["√3","5","the number 8","the number 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rearranging 5-√3=a/b gives √3 = 5-a/b, which would make √3 rational — a contradiction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"3√2 is irrational because if it were rational (=a/b), then √2 would equal:","options":["a/(3b), a ratio of integers","3a/b","a+3b","b/(3a)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rearranging 3√2=a/b gives √2=a/(3b), which would make √2 rational — a contradiction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which general strategy proves both '5-√3' and '3√2' are irrational?","options":["Rearrange to isolate a known irrational number, then show it would have to be rational — a contradiction","Compute their decimal expansion directly","Use the factor tree method","Show they are both negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both proofs rearrange the assumed rational expression to isolate √3 or √2 and contradict its known irrationality."}]},{"id":"rnboss","title":"Guardian of Real Numbers","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of Numeria, the Guardian of Real Numbers rises — a shifting figure made of factor trees, remainders, and endless irrational decimals. To pass into the next World, you must prove mastery over every trial you've faced: division, prime factorisation, HCF and LCM, and the irrationality proofs.","boss":{"name":"The Guardian of Real Numbers","taunt":"Prove your mastery, traveller — factor my defences, tame my irrationals, or be lost forever in an endless, non-terminating decimal!"},"teach":[{"title":"Recap Before the Final Trial","body":"Remember: HCF and LCM come from prime factorisations (smallest/greatest powers), HCF(a,b)×LCM(a,b)=a×b for pairs of numbers, the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic guarantees unique prime factorisations, and √2, √3 and their combinations are irrational by contradiction using Theorem 1.2.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"140 = 2²×5×7. What is the sum of its distinct prime factors (2+5+7)?","answer":14,"explain":"The distinct primes in 140's factorisation are 2, 5 and 7, and 2+5+7=14."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"26 = 2×13 and 91 = 7×13. What is HCF(26, 91)?","answer":13,"explain":"The only common prime factor of 26 and 91 is 13."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using HCF(26,91) = 13, find LCM(26, 91) = (26×91)/13.","answer":182,"explain":"26×91 = 2366, and 2366/13 = 182."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Given HCF(306, 657) = 9, find LCM(306, 657) using LCM = (306×657)/9.","answer":22338,"explain":"306×657 = 201042, and 201042/9 = 22338."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is guaranteed, by the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, to never end in the digit 0?","options":["6ⁿ for any natural number n","10ⁿ for any natural number n","20ⁿ for any natural number n","30ⁿ for any natural number n"],"answer":0,"explain":"6ⁿ = 2ⁿ×3ⁿ never contains the prime factor 5, so it can never end in 0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which proof technique is used to show that √2, √3, 5-√3 and 3√2 are all irrational?","options":["Proof by contradiction, using Theorem 1.2 (p divides a² implies p divides a)","Proof by induction","Direct computation of decimal digits","The factor tree method alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each proof assumes the number is rational and derives an impossible contradiction using Theorem 1.2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Sonia (18 min/round) and Ravi (12 min/round) start together on a circular track. After how many minutes do they next meet at the start? (LCM of 18 and 12)","answer":36,"explain":"18=2×3², 12=2²×3, so LCM(18,12) = 2²×3² = 36 minutes."}]}]},{"id":"polynomials10","index":1,"name":"Polynomials","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 2","emoji":"📈","palette":{"from":"#34d399","to":"#065f46"},"lessons":[{"id":"pl1","title":"The Degree Oracle","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Scroll Library of Study Atlas, where every expression is guarded by the Degree Oracle. She will only let you pass if you can name a polynomial's degree and evaluate it at any point she chooses.","teach":[{"title":"Degree and Type","body":"The degree of a polynomial p(x) is the highest power of x appearing in it. Degree 1 gives a linear polynomial (like 4x + 2), degree 2 gives a quadratic polynomial (like 2y² − 3y + 4), and degree 3 gives a cubic polynomial (like 5x³ − 4x² + x − 2).","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Evaluating a Polynomial","body":"If p(x) is a polynomial and k is any real number, the value obtained by replacing x with k is called p(k). For p(x) = x² − 3x − 4, p(2) = 2² − 3×2 − 4 = −6, and p(0) = −4.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"The Zero of a Polynomial","body":"A real number k is called a zero of p(x) if p(k) = 0. For p(x) = x² − 3x − 4, both p(−1) = 0 and p(4) = 0, so −1 and 4 are zeroes. For a linear polynomial ax + b, the zero is always −b/a.","visual":"0️⃣"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these expressions is NOT a polynomial?","options":["1/x","x² − 3x − 4","5x³ − 4x² + x − 2","3z + 4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Polynomials cannot have a variable in the denominator, so 1/x is not a polynomial."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For p(x) = x² − 3x − 4, what is p(2)?","answer":-6,"explain":"p(2) = 2² − (3×2) − 4 = 4 − 6 − 4 = −6."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the zero of the linear polynomial p(x) = 2x + 3?","answer":-1.5,"explain":"Setting 2x + 3 = 0 gives x = −3/2 = −1.5, matching the rule zero = −b/a."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A polynomial of degree 3 is called a ___ polynomial.","options":["cubic","quadratic","linear","quartic"],"answer":0,"explain":"Degree 1 is linear, degree 2 is quadratic, and degree 3 is cubic."}]},{"id":"pl2","title":"Where the Curve Meets the Ground","icon":"🏔️","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the library lies the Graph Canyon, where every polynomial casts a curved shadow on the ground below. The path forward is marked only where each curve touches the canyon floor — the x-axis.","teach":[{"title":"Linear Graphs Cross Once","body":"The graph of y = ax + b (a ≠ 0) is a straight line that crosses the x-axis at exactly one point, (−b/a, 0). For y = 2x + 3, the line passes through (−2, −1) and (2, 7), and crosses the x-axis at x = −3/2 — exactly the zero of the polynomial.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Quadratic Graphs Are Parabolas","body":"The graph of y = ax² + bx + c is a parabola, opening upward if a > 0 and downward if a < 0. For y = x² − 3x − 4, the curve crosses the x-axis at x = −1 and x = 4, which are exactly its zeroes.","visual":"🌙"},{"title":"Three Cases for a Quadratic","body":"A parabola can cross the x-axis at two distinct points (two zeroes), touch it at one coincident point (one repeated zero), or miss it completely (no real zero). So a quadratic polynomial has at most two zeroes.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The graph of y = x² − 3x − 4 crosses the x-axis at x = −1 and x = 4. What are the zeroes of this polynomial?","options":["−1 and 4","0 and −4","1 and −4","3 and −4"],"answer":0,"explain":"The zeroes of a polynomial are exactly the x-coordinates where its graph meets the x-axis."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At most how many zeroes can a quadratic polynomial have?","answer":2,"explain":"A parabola can cross the x-axis at most twice, so a quadratic has at most 2 zeroes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the parabola for y = ax² + bx + c lies entirely above the x-axis, how many real zeroes does the polynomial have?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"answer":0,"explain":"A parabola that never touches the x-axis represents a quadratic with no real zeroes."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The line y = 2x + 3 crosses the x-axis at what x-value?","answer":-1.5,"explain":"Setting 2x + 3 = 0 gives x = −1.5, matching the line's single x-axis crossing."}]},{"id":"pl3","title":"Cubic Currents","icon":"🌊","kind":"lesson","story":"The canyon opens into the Cubic Currents, a river whose surface twists through the ground up to three times. Riding it safely means knowing exactly how many times a cubic curve can touch the shore.","teach":[{"title":"A Cubic Can Cross Three Times","body":"For y = x³ − 4x, plotting the curve shows it crossing the x-axis at x = −2, 0, and 2 — the three zeroes of the cubic polynomial x³ − 4x.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Repeated and Fewer Zeroes","body":"The cubic polynomial x³ has only one zero, x = 0, where its graph touches the x-axis. Since x³ − x² factors as x²(x − 1), its only zeroes are 0 and 1.","visual":"🔱"},{"title":"Degree n Means at Most n Zeroes","body":"In general, the graph of y = p(x) for a polynomial of degree n meets the x-axis at atmost n points. So a polynomial of degree n has at most n zeroes.","visual":"♾️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The cubic polynomial x³ − 4x has zeroes at −2, 0, and 2. How many zeroes does it have in total?","answer":3,"explain":"The graph of y = x³ − 4x crosses the x-axis at exactly three points: −2, 0, and 2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Since x³ − x² factors as x²(x − 1), what are its zeroes?","options":["0 and 1","0 and −1","1 and −1","0, 1 and −1"],"answer":0,"explain":"x²(x − 1) = 0 when x = 0 or x = 1, so those are the only zeroes."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A polynomial of degree 5 has at most how many zeroes?","answer":5,"explain":"A polynomial of degree n has at most n zeroes, so degree 5 gives at most 5 zeroes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the only zero of the cubic polynomial x³?","options":["0","1","−1","3"],"answer":0,"explain":"x³ = 0 only when x = 0, so its graph touches the x-axis at a single coincident point."}]},{"id":"pl4","title":"The Coefficient Code: Quadratics","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"A locked gate blocks the trail, etched with a and b and c. The Keeper of Coefficients says the gate opens only for travelers who can read the secret bond between a quadratic's zeroes and its coefficients.","teach":[{"title":"Sum and Product of Zeroes","body":"If α and β are the zeroes of p(x) = ax² + bx + c, then α + β = −b/a and αβ = c/a. For 2x² − 8x + 6 = 2(x − 1)(x − 3), the zeroes are 1 and 3: their sum is 4 = −(−8)/2 and their product is 3 = 6/2.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"A Second Check","body":"For 3x² + 5x − 2 = (3x − 1)(x + 2), the zeroes are 1/3 and −2. Sum = 1/3 + (−2) = −5/3 = −(coefficient of x)/(coefficient of x²), and product = 1/3 × (−2) = −2/3 = (constant term)/(coefficient of x²).","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Building a Polynomial Backwards","body":"Given a desired sum and product of zeroes, you can build the quadratic ax² + bx + c directly, since sum = −b/a and product = c/a. If the sum of zeroes is −3 and the product is 2, taking a = 1 gives x² + 3x + 2.","visual":"🏗️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The zeroes of x² + 7x + 10 are −2 and −5. What is their sum?","answer":-7,"explain":"−2 + (−5) = −7, which equals −(coefficient of x)/(coefficient of x²) = −7/1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the product of the zeroes of x² + 7x + 10?","answer":10,"explain":"(−2) × (−5) = 10, which equals the constant term divided by the coefficient of x²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The zeroes of x² − 3 are √3 and −√3. What is their product?","options":["−3","3","0","√3"],"answer":0,"explain":"√3 × (−√3) = −3, matching (constant term)/(coefficient of x²) = −3/1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Find a quadratic polynomial whose zeroes have sum −3 and product 2.","options":["x² + 3x + 2","x² − 3x + 2","x² + 3x − 2","x² − 2x + 3"],"answer":0,"explain":"With a = 1, b = −(sum) = 3 and c = product = 2, giving x² + 3x + 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 2x² − 8x + 6, the sum of the zeroes equals −b/a. What is this sum?","answer":4,"explain":"−b/a = −(−8)/2 = 4, matching the zeroes 1 and 3 which sum to 4."}]},{"id":"pl5","title":"The Coefficient Code: Cubics","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"Past the gate stands a three-sided obelisk representing the cubic realm. Three zeroes, three relationships — the Keeper insists you master all three before the final chamber will open.","teach":[{"title":"Three Relationships","body":"If α, β, γ are the zeroes of p(x) = ax³ + bx² + cx + d, then α + β + γ = −b/a, αβ + βγ + γα = c/a, and αβγ = −d/a.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Checking with a Cubic","body":"For 2x³ − 5x² − 14x + 8, the zeroes are 4, −2 and 1/2. Their sum is 4 + (−2) + 1/2 = 5/2 = −(−5)/2, and their product is 4 × (−2) × 1/2 = −4 = −8/2.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"A Full Verification","body":"For 3x³ − 5x² − 11x − 3, substituting shows p(3) = 0, p(−1) = 0, and p(−1/3) = 0, so 3, −1 and −1/3 are its zeroes. These satisfy all three sum/product relationships with a = 3, b = −5, c = −11, d = −3.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The zeroes of 2x³ − 5x² − 14x + 8 are 4, −2, and 1/2. What is their sum?","answer":2.5,"explain":"4 + (−2) + 0.5 = 2.5, matching −b/a = −(−5)/2 = 2.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the product of the zeroes 4, −2, and 1/2?","answer":-4,"explain":"4 × (−2) × 0.5 = −4, matching −d/a = −8/2 = −4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a cubic ax³ + bx² + cx + d with zeroes α, β, γ, which formula gives their product αβγ?","options":["−d/a","d/a","c/a","−b/a"],"answer":0,"explain":"The product of all three zeroes of a cubic equals −d/a."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The zeroes of 3x³ − 5x² − 11x − 3 are 3, −1, and −1/3. What is their product?","answer":1,"explain":"3 × (−1) × (−1/3) = 1, matching −d/a = −(−3)/3 = 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a cubic ax³ + bx² + cx + d with zeroes α, β, γ, the sum of products taken two at a time (αβ + βγ + γα) equals which ratio?","options":["c/a","b/a","−d/a","−b/a"],"answer":0,"explain":"αβ + βγ + γα equals c/a, the coefficient of x divided by the coefficient of x³."}]},{"id":"pl6","title":"Chapter Boss: The Root Warden","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the canyon waits the Root Warden, a guardian built from every zero it has ever swallowed. Only a traveler who has mastered degrees, graphs, and coefficient bonds can make it yield.","boss":{"name":"The Root Warden","taunt":"Every curve I have ever crossed lives inside me. Name my roots, or become one!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything You've Learned","body":"Zeroes are where a polynomial's graph meets the x-axis, a degree-n polynomial has at most n zeroes, and the zeroes of a quadratic or cubic are locked to its coefficients through sum and product formulas.","visual":"🗝️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For p(x) = x² − 3x − 4, what is p(4)?","answer":0,"explain":"p(4) = 4² − (3×4) − 4 = 16 − 12 − 4 = 0, confirming 4 is a zero."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At most how many zeroes can a polynomial of degree 4 have?","options":["4","3","5","2"],"answer":0,"explain":"A polynomial of degree n has at most n zeroes, so degree 4 gives at most 4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"x² − 2x − 8 factors as (x − 4)(x + 2). Using −b/a, what is the sum of its zeroes?","answer":2,"explain":"−b/a = −(−2)/1 = 2, matching 4 + (−2) = 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the product of the zeroes of x² − 2x − 8?","answer":-8,"explain":"c/a = −8/1 = −8, matching 4 × (−2) = −8."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A parabola touches the x-axis at exactly one coincident point. What does this tell you about the quadratic's zeroes?","options":["It has two equal (repeated) zeroes","It has two distinct zeroes","It has no real zeroes","It has three zeroes"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single touching point means the two zero locations have coincided into one repeated zero."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 2x³ − 5x² − 14x + 8, zeroes are 4, −2, and 1/2. Using −d/a, what is their product?","answer":-4,"explain":"−d/a = −8/2 = −4, matching 4 × (−2) × 0.5 = −4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The zeroes of a polynomial p(x) correspond to which feature of the graph y = p(x)?","options":["The x-coordinates where the graph crosses the x-axis","The y-coordinates where the graph crosses the y-axis","The highest point of the graph","The slope of the graph"],"answer":0,"explain":"A zero k satisfies p(k) = 0, which is exactly where the graph crosses the x-axis."}]}]},{"id":"linearequations10","index":2,"name":"Pair of Linear Equations","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 3","emoji":"📐","palette":{"from":"#f472b6","to":"#831843"},"lessons":[{"id":"le1","title":"The Fair at Riverbend","icon":"🎡","kind":"lesson","story":"You step through a shimmering gate into a village fair, where a girl named Akhila is counting coins for Giant Wheel rides and Hoopla games. She needs help figuring out exactly how many of each she can afford -- can you turn her puzzle into equations?","teach":[{"title":"Two Equations, Two Unknowns","body":"A pair of linear equations in two variables uses two unknowns (like x and y) linked by two separate equations that must both be true at once. Akhila's rides (x) and Hoopla games (y) are connected because she played Hoopla half as many times as she rode the Giant Wheel: y = x/2.","visual":"✏️"},{"title":"Turning Words into Equations","body":"Each ride costs ₹3 and each Hoopla game costs ₹4, and Akhila spent ₹20 in total. This gives the second equation: 3x + 4y = 20. Together, y = x/2 and 3x + 4y = 20 form a pair of linear equations describing her day at the fair.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"The General Form","body":"Any linear equation in two variables can be written as ax + by + c = 0, where a, b, c are real numbers and a, b are not both zero. A pair of such equations, taken together, is what this whole chapter is about solving.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Akhila's fair problem, x is the number of Giant Wheel rides and y is the number of Hoopla games. Which equation correctly shows she played Hoopla half as many times as she rode the Giant Wheel?","options":["y = x/2","x = y/2","x + y = 2","2x = 2y"],"answer":0,"explain":"The problem states the number of Hoopla games is half the number of rides, so y = x/2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If Akhila had x = 4 rides on the Giant Wheel, how many times y did she play Hoopla (using y = x/2)?","answer":2,"explain":"y = x/2 = 4/2 = 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Each ride costs ₹3 and each Hoopla game costs ₹4. If x = 4 and y = 2, how much did Akhila spend in total (3x + 4y)?","answer":20,"explain":"3(4) + 4(2) = 12 + 8 = 20, matching the ₹20 she spent."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is the general form of a linear equation in two variables x and y?","options":["ax + by + c = 0","ax^2 + by + c = 0","a/x + b/y = c","ax + by = c^2"],"answer":0,"explain":"A linear equation in two variables is written as ax + by + c = 0, with a and b not both zero."}]},{"id":"le2","title":"The Cartographer's Map Room","icon":"🗺️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in an ancient map room, two roads are drawn for every puzzle -- and how they cross tells you everything. Learn to read intersecting, parallel, and overlapping roads to find whether a pair of equations has a solution.","teach":[{"title":"Three Ways Lines Can Meet","body":"When you graph a pair of linear equations, the two lines can intersect at exactly one point (unique solution, consistent), be parallel and never meet (no solution, inconsistent), or be coincident and lie exactly on top of each other (infinitely many solutions, dependent and consistent).","visual":"📍"},{"title":"Worked Example: Intersecting Lines","body":"For x + 3y = 6 and 2x - 3y = 12, plotting points gives lines that cross at B(6, 0). So x = 6, y = 0 is the unique solution, and the pair is consistent.","visual":"✖️"},{"title":"Worked Example: Coincident Lines","body":"For 5x - 8y + 1 = 0 and 3x - (24/5)y + 3/5 = 0, multiplying the second equation by 5/3 turns it into exactly 5x - 8y + 1 = 0 -- the same line. Since both equations describe the identical line, there are infinitely many common solutions.","visual":"🟰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the graphs of two linear equations intersect at exactly one point, the pair of equations is called:","options":["consistent, with a unique solution","inconsistent, with no solution","dependent, with infinite solutions","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Intersecting lines meet at exactly one point, giving a unique solution -- the pair is consistent."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The lines x + 3y = 6 and 2x - 3y = 12 intersect at point B. What is the x-coordinate of B?","answer":6,"explain":"Plotting both lines shows they meet at B(6, 0), so x = 6."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same intersection point B for x + 3y = 6 and 2x - 3y = 12, what is the y-coordinate?","answer":0,"explain":"The lines meet at B(6, 0), so y = 0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"5x - 8y + 1 = 0 and 3x - (24/5)y + 3/5 = 0 turn out to be the same line when simplified. What does this mean for the pair of equations?","options":["They have infinitely many solutions (coincident lines)","They have no solution (parallel lines)","They have exactly one solution","They cannot be graphed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since both equations represent the identical line, every point on that line is a common solution -- infinitely many solutions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A pair of parallel lines (that never intersect) represents a pair of equations that is:","options":["inconsistent, with no solution","consistent, with a unique solution","dependent, with infinite solutions","always false"],"answer":0,"explain":"Parallel lines never meet, so there is no common solution -- the pair is inconsistent."}]},{"id":"le3","title":"The Ratio Oracle","icon":"🔮","kind":"lesson","story":"An oracle offers a shortcut: no graphing needed. Just compare the coefficients of x, y, and the constants in two equations, and she'll tell you instantly whether their lines cross, run parallel, or lie on top of each other.","teach":[{"title":"The Three Ratio Tests","body":"For a1x + b1y + c1 = 0 and a2x + b2y + c2 = 0: if a1/a2 ≠ b1/b2, the lines intersect (unique solution). If a1/a2 = b1/b2 ≠ c1/c2, the lines are parallel (no solution). If a1/a2 = b1/b2 = c1/c2, the lines are coincident (infinitely many solutions).","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Testing the Oracle's Examples","body":"For x - 2y = 0 and 3x + 4y - 20 = 0: a1/a2 = 1/3 and b1/b2 = -2/4 = -1/2, which are unequal, so the lines intersect. For 2x + 3y - 9 = 0 and 4x + 6y - 18 = 0: a1/a2 = b1/b2 = c1/c2 = 1/2, so the lines are coincident.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Spotting a Parallel Pair","body":"For x + 2y - 4 = 0 and 2x + 4y - 12 = 0: a1/a2 = 1/2 and b1/b2 = 2/4 = 1/2 are equal, but c1/c2 = -4/-12 = 1/3 is different. Since the first two ratios match but the third doesn't, the lines are parallel with no solution.","visual":"🚧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the pair x - 2y = 0 and 3x + 4y - 20 = 0, a1/a2 = 1/3 and b1/b2 = -1/2, which are not equal. What does this tell you?","options":["The lines intersect at exactly one point","The lines are parallel","The lines are coincident","No conclusion is possible"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a1/a2 ≠ b1/b2, the lines intersect, giving a unique solution."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For 2x + 3y - 9 = 0 and 4x + 6y - 18 = 0, all three ratios a1/a2, b1/b2, and c1/c2 equal 1/2. What kind of pair is this?","options":["Coincident lines, infinitely many solutions","Intersecting lines, unique solution","Parallel lines, no solution","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a1/a2 = b1/b2 = c1/c2, the two equations describe the same line -- infinitely many solutions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For x + 2y - 4 = 0 and 2x + 4y - 12 = 0, a1/a2 = b1/b2 = 1/2 but c1/c2 = 1/3. What does this pair represent?","options":["Parallel lines with no solution","Coincident lines with infinite solutions","Intersecting lines with a unique solution","An inconsistent equation with infinite solutions"],"answer":0,"explain":"When a1/a2 = b1/b2 but c1/c2 is different, the lines are parallel and the pair has no solution."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Check the pair 3x + 2y = 5 and 2x - 3y = 7 by comparing a1/a2 and b1/b2. Since 3/2 is not equal to 2/(-3), the pair is:","options":["consistent","inconsistent","dependent","impossible to solve"],"answer":0,"explain":"a1/a2 ≠ b1/b2 means the lines intersect, so the pair is consistent."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Check the pair 2x - 3y = 8 and 4x - 6y = 9. Here a1/a2 = b1/b2 = 1/2, but c1/c2 = 8/9. What is this pair?","options":["inconsistent","consistent with a unique solution","dependent with infinite solutions","undetermined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal coefficient ratios but unequal constant ratio means the lines are parallel -- inconsistent, no solution."}]},{"id":"le4","title":"The Swap Shop","icon":"🔄","kind":"lesson","story":"A clever merchant in the Swap Shop trades one variable for an expression in the other -- teaching you the substitution method. Master her trick to solve equations that graphing can't handle precisely.","teach":[{"title":"How Substitution Works","body":"Step 1: solve one equation for one variable in terms of the other. Step 2: substitute that expression into the other equation to get one equation in one variable, and solve it. Step 3: substitute that value back to find the other variable.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Aftab and His Daughter","body":"Aftab tells his daughter: 'Seven years ago I was seven times as old as you were then; three years from now I'll be three times as old as you'll be.' With s and t as their present ages, the equations are s - 7t + 42 = 0 and s - 3t = 6. Substituting s = 3t + 6 into the first equation gives 4t = 48, so t = 12 and s = 42.","visual":"👨‍👧"},{"title":"When Substitution Reveals No Unique Answer","body":"For 2x + 3y = 9 and 4x + 6y = 18, substituting x = (9 - 3y)/2 into the second equation gives 18 = 18 -- always true, no matter the value of y. This means the equations are really the same line, so there are infinitely many solutions.","visual":"♾️"},{"title":"When Substitution Reveals No Solution","body":"For the 'two rails' x + 2y - 4 = 0 and 2x + 4y - 12 = 0, substituting x = 4 - 2y into the second equation gives -4 = 0, which is false. A false statement with no variables left means the pair has no solution -- the rails never cross.","visual":"🚫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using substitution on Aftab's age puzzle (s - 7t + 42 = 0 and s - 3t = 6), what is his daughter's present age t?","answer":12,"explain":"Substituting s = 3t + 6 into the first equation gives 4t = 48, so t = 12."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Following on from the previous question, what is Aftab's present age s?","answer":42,"explain":"s = 3t + 6 = 3(12) + 6 = 42."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Substituting into 2x + 3y = 9 and 4x + 6y = 18 leads to the statement 18 = 18, which is always true. What does this mean?","options":["The pair has infinitely many solutions","The pair has no solution","x must equal 9 and y must equal 6","The equations are inconsistent"],"answer":0,"explain":"A true statement with no variables means the two equations are equivalent (the same line), giving infinitely many solutions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Substituting into x + 2y - 4 = 0 and 2x + 4y - 12 = 0 leads to -4 = 0, a false statement. What does this tell you about the pair?","options":["It has no solution -- the lines are parallel","It has infinitely many solutions","It has exactly one solution","The substitution was done incorrectly"],"answer":0,"explain":"A false statement with no variables left means the pair of equations is inconsistent -- no solution."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Solve x + y = 14 and x - y = 4 by substitution (write y = 14 - x, then substitute). What is x?","answer":9,"explain":"Substituting x = 14 - y into x - y = 4 gives (14 - y) - y = 4, so 14 - 2y = 4, y = 5, and x = 14 - 5 = 9."}]},{"id":"le5","title":"The Balance Beam Trials","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"A pair of stone balance beams challenges you to make coefficients match, then add or subtract equations to make a variable vanish entirely. This is the elimination method -- often faster than substitution.","teach":[{"title":"How Elimination Works","body":"Step 1: multiply the equations by suitable constants so that the coefficients of one variable become numerically equal. Step 2: add or subtract the equations to eliminate that variable. Step 3: solve the resulting one-variable equation, then Step 4: substitute back to find the other variable.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Incomes and Expenditures","body":"Two people have incomes in ratio 9:7 and expenditures in ratio 4:3, each saving ₹2000 a month: 9x - 4y = 2000 and 7x - 3y = 2000. Multiplying by 3 and 4 respectively and subtracting eliminates y, giving x = 2000, so y = 4000 -- incomes of ₹18,000 and ₹14,000.","visual":"💰"},{"title":"When Elimination Shows No Solution","body":"For 2x + 3y = 8 and 4x + 6y = 7, multiplying the first equation by 2 gives 4x + 6y = 16, which contradicts 4x + 6y = 7. Subtracting gives 0 = 9, a false statement -- so this pair has no solution.","visual":"❌"},{"title":"The Two-Digit Number Puzzle","body":"A two-digit number plus its digit-reversal equals 66, and its digits differ by 2. Writing the number as 10x + y gives x + y = 6, and the digit condition gives x - y = 2 or y - x = 2. Elimination gives (x, y) = (4, 2) or (2, 4), so the number is 42 or 24.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the incomes problem (9x - 4y = 2000 and 7x - 3y = 2000, solved by elimination), what is the monthly income of the person earning 9x, in rupees?","answer":18000,"explain":"x = 2000, so 9x = 9(2000) = 18000."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the monthly income of the other person, earning 7x, in rupees?","answer":14000,"explain":"x = 2000, so 7x = 7(2000) = 14000."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Eliminating x from 2x + 3y = 8 and 4x + 6y = 7 leads to the statement 0 = 9. What does this mean?","options":["The pair of equations has no solution","The pair has infinitely many solutions","x = 0 and y = 9","The elimination was done incorrectly"],"answer":0,"explain":"A false statement like 0 = 9 with no variables means the pair is inconsistent -- no solution."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the two-digit number puzzle (digits sum condition x + y = 6, and digits differ by 2), one valid answer is the number 42. What is the other valid two-digit number?","answer":24,"explain":"Swapping which digit is larger gives x = 2, y = 4, so the number 24 also satisfies both conditions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the elimination method, what is the correct first step?","options":["Multiply the equations by suitable constants so one variable's coefficients match","Immediately add the two equations together","Guess values for x and y","Graph both equations first"],"answer":0,"explain":"Elimination starts by making the coefficients of one variable numerically equal, so that variable cancels when the equations are added or subtracted."}]},{"id":"le6","title":"Market Day Puzzles","icon":"🛒","kind":"lesson","story":"The village market is bustling with shopkeepers, a cricket coach, and a rectangular garden waiting to be measured. Put substitution and elimination to work solving real purchases and puzzles.","teach":[{"title":"Pens and Pencils","body":"5 pencils and 7 pens together cost ₹50, while 7 pencils and 5 pens together cost ₹46. Writing this as 5x + 7y = 50 and 7x + 5y = 46 and eliminating y gives x = 3 (pencil) and y = 5 (pen).","visual":"✏️"},{"title":"Bats and Balls","body":"A cricket coach buys 7 bats and 6 balls for ₹3800, then 3 bats and 5 balls for ₹1750. Setting up 7x + 6y = 3800 and 3x + 5y = 1750 and eliminating y gives the cost of one bat as ₹500 and one ball as ₹50.","visual":"🏏"},{"title":"Taxi Fares","body":"A taxi charge has a fixed part plus a per-kilometre part. A 10 km trip costs ₹105 and a 15 km trip costs ₹155. Solving x + 10y = 105 and x + 15y = 155 gives a fixed charge x = ₹5 and a per-km charge y = ₹10, so a 25 km trip costs 5 + 25(10) = ₹255.","visual":"🚕"},{"title":"The Rectangular Garden","body":"A rectangular garden's length is 4 m more than its width, and half its perimeter is 36 m. Writing l = w + 4 and l + w = 36 gives w = 16 m and l = 20 m.","visual":"🌳"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"5 pencils and 7 pens cost ₹50 together; 7 pencils and 5 pens cost ₹46 together. What is the cost of one pencil, in rupees?","answer":3,"explain":"Solving 5x + 7y = 50 and 7x + 5y = 46 by elimination gives x = 3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cricket coach buys 7 bats and 6 balls for ₹3800, and 3 bats and 5 balls for ₹1750. What is the cost of one bat, in rupees?","answer":500,"explain":"Solving 7x + 6y = 3800 and 3x + 5y = 1750 by elimination gives x = 500."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A taxi's fixed charge is ₹5 and its per-km charge is ₹10 (found from the 10 km and 15 km fares). How much would a 25 km ride cost, in rupees?","answer":255,"explain":"Total charge = fixed + per-km × distance = 5 + 10(25) = 255."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A rectangular garden's length is 4 m more than its width, and half its perimeter is 36 m. What are its dimensions?","options":["20 m by 16 m","18 m by 14 m","22 m by 18 m","24 m by 20 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"l + w = 36 and l = w + 4 give w = 16 and l = 20."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"5 pencils and 7 pens cost ₹50 together; 7 pencils and 5 pens cost ₹46 together. What is the cost of one pen, in rupees?","answer":5,"explain":"Substituting x = 3 back into 5x + 7y = 50 gives 15 + 7y = 50, so y = 5."}]},{"id":"le7","title":"The Equation Sentinel","icon":"🛡️","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the map room stands the Equation Sentinel, guardian of every trick you've learned -- graphing, ratio tests, substitution, and elimination. Defeat it by solving its toughest real-world puzzles.","boss":{"name":"The Equation Sentinel","taunt":"Two lines, two unknowns, one truth -- prove you can find it without a single mistake!"},"teach":[{"title":"Your Full Toolkit","body":"You now have four ways to attack a pair of linear equations: graphing (visual, but imprecise for fractional answers), the ratio test (a1/a2, b1/b2, c1/c2, for a quick classification), substitution, and elimination. Choose whichever is fastest for the numbers in front of you.","visual":"🧰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A solution like x = 49/29, y = 19/29 has non-integer coordinates, making it hard to read off a graph accurately. Which method is best suited for finding such a solution precisely?","options":["An algebraic method (substitution or elimination)","The graphical method only","Guessing and checking","There is no way to find it"],"answer":0,"explain":"The graphical method struggles with non-integer coordinates, so algebraic methods like substitution or elimination give exact answers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Eliminating x from 2x + 3y = 8 and 4x + 6y = 7 leads to 0 = 9. What kind of pair is this?","options":["Inconsistent, with no solution","Consistent, with a unique solution","Dependent, with infinite solutions","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"A false numberless statement like 0 = 9 means the pair of equations has no solution."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A two-digit number plus its digit-reversal is 66, and its digits differ by 2. One answer is 42 -- what is the other valid two-digit number?","answer":24,"explain":"Swapping which digit is larger (y - x = 2 instead of x - y = 2) gives the number 24."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Five years ago, Nuri was thrice as old as Sonu. Ten years later, Nuri will be twice as old as Sonu. What is Nuri's present age?","answer":50,"explain":"Solving x - 3y = -10 and x - 2y = 10 (from the two age conditions) gives y = 20 and x = 50."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A fraction becomes 9/11 when 2 is added to both numerator and denominator, and becomes 5/6 when 3 is added to both. What is the numerator of the original fraction?","answer":7,"explain":"Solving 11x - 9y = -4 and 6x - 5y = -3 gives x = 7 (and y = 9), so the fraction is 7/9."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a1x + b1y + c1 = 0 and a2x + b2y + c2 = 0, which condition means the pair has infinitely many solutions?","options":["a1/a2 = b1/b2 = c1/c2","a1/a2 ≠ b1/b2","a1/a2 = b1/b2 ≠ c1/c2","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"When all three ratios are equal, the two equations represent the same line, giving infinitely many solutions."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Meena withdrew ₹2000 from a bank in ₹50 and ₹100 notes only, receiving 25 notes in total. How many ₹100 notes did she receive?","answer":15,"explain":"Solving x + y = 25 and 50x + 100y = 2000 gives y = 15 (₹100 notes) and x = 10 (₹50 notes)."}]}]},{"id":"quadraticequations10","index":3,"name":"Quadratic Equations","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 4","emoji":"🧮","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"qe1","title":"The Prayer Hall Blueprint","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"You've entered the ruins of an ancient prayer hall, once commissioned to cover exactly 300 square metres. The architect's cracked tablet is covered in equations. Decode the standard form of a quadratic equation to begin your quest.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Quadratic Equation?","body":"An equation of the form ax² + bx + c = 0, where a, b, c are real numbers and a ≠ 0, is a quadratic equation. Any equation p(x) = 0 where p(x) is a degree-2 polynomial is quadratic; writing its terms in descending order of degree gives the standard form.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Building One From a Story","body":"The prayer hall has breadth x metres and length (2x + 1) metres, with area 300 m². Since area = length × breadth, (2x + 1)·x = 300, which simplifies to the standard quadratic equation 2x² + x − 300 = 0.","visual":"🕌"},{"title":"Not Everything That Looks Quadratic Is One","body":"Always simplify first. x(x + 1) + 8 = (x + 2)(x − 2) simplifies to x + 12 = 0 — a linear equation, not quadratic, even though it looked like one. But (x + 2)³ = x³ − 4 simplifies to x² + 2x + 2 = 0, which IS quadratic despite looking cubic.","visual":"🔍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the standard form of a quadratic equation?","options":["ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0)","ax + b = 0","ax³ + bx² + c = 0","a/x + b = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"A quadratic equation must have a degree-2 term with a≠0, written as ax²+bx+c=0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"John and Jivanti together have 45 marbles. Both lose 5 marbles each, and the product of their remaining marbles is 124. This is modeled by x² − 45x + k = 0. What is k?","answer":324,"explain":"(x-5)(40-x)=124 gives -x²+45x-200=124, i.e. x²-45x+324=0, so k=324."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A cottage industry's cost per toy is (55 − x) rupees when x toys are made in a day, and that day's total cost is ₹750. Which quadratic equation models this?","options":["x² − 55x + 750 = 0","x² + 55x − 750 = 0","55x² − x + 750 = 0","x² − 750x + 55 = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"x(55-x)=750 rearranges to x²-55x+750=0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which equation below simplifies to a genuine quadratic equation?","options":["(x+2)³ = x³ − 4","x(x+1)+8=(x+2)(x-2)","x² = x² + 5","2x + 3 = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"(x+2)³=x³-4 simplifies to x²+2x+2=0, a genuine quadratic, unlike the others."}]},{"id":"qe2","title":"Roots of the Ancients","icon":"🗝️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the vault, a stone tablet inscribed by Babylonian scribes challenges you: prove which numbers unlock the equation. A number that makes ax²+bx+c=0 true is called a root — find the correct key.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Root?","body":"A real number α is a root of ax² + bx + c = 0 if aα² + bα + c = 0 — substituting α makes the equation true. Saying 'x = α is a solution' or 'α satisfies the equation' means the same thing.","visual":"🔑"},{"title":"Roots Are Zeroes of the Polynomial","body":"The roots of ax²+bx+c=0 are exactly the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial ax²+bx+c. Since a quadratic polynomial has at most two zeroes, a quadratic equation has at most two roots.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Checking a Candidate Root","body":"For 2x² − 3x + 1 = 0, substituting x = 1 gives (2×1²) − (3×1) + 1 = 2 − 3 + 1 = 0, matching the RHS. So x = 1 is a root of this equation.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Substitute x=1 into 2x² − 3x + 1. What value do you get?","answer":0,"explain":"2(1)²-3(1)+1=0, confirming x=1 is a root."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A quadratic equation can have at most how many roots?","options":["2","1","3","infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"A quadratic polynomial has at most two zeroes, so its equation has at most two roots."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For ax²+bx+c=0, α is called a root if:","options":["aα²+bα+c=0","aα+b=0","α=0","a+b+c=α"],"answer":0,"explain":"By definition, α is a root when substituting it makes the equation equal zero."}]},{"id":"qe3","title":"Splitting the Middle Term","icon":"⚔️","kind":"lesson","story":"The vault's gears are jammed. An old scholar's notes reveal a trick passed down since Class IX: splitting the middle term to prise a quadratic apart into two linear factors. Master it to unlock the next chamber.","teach":[{"title":"The Splitting Method","body":"To solve ax²+bx+c=0 by factorisation, split the middle term bx into two parts whose product equals ac·x², then group and factor. For 2x²−5x+3=0, split −5x as −2x−3x since (−2x)(−3x)=6x²=(2x²)(3): 2x²−2x−3x+3 = 2x(x−1)−3(x−1) = (2x−3)(x−1).","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"From Factors to Roots","body":"Once ax²+bx+c=(px+q)(rx+s), the equation becomes (px+q)(rx+s)=0, true when either factor is zero. For (2x−3)(x−1)=0, either 2x−3=0 giving x=3/2, or x−1=0 giving x=1. So the roots of 2x²−5x+3=0 are 1 and 3/2.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Another Worked Example","body":"For 6x²−x−2=0, split −x as 3x−4x: 6x²+3x−4x−2 = 3x(2x+1)−2(2x+1) = (3x−2)(2x+1). Setting each factor to zero gives x=2/3 or x=−1/2, the two roots.","visual":"🗡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Solve 2x² − 5x + 3 = 0 by factorisation. Give the larger root.","answer":1.5,"explain":"Factorising gives (2x-3)(x-1)=0, so roots are 1 and 3/2 — the larger is 1.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the negative root of 6x² − x − 2 = 0.","answer":-0.5,"explain":"Factorising gives (3x-2)(2x+1)=0, so roots are 2/3 and -1/2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Factorising x² − 3x − 10 = 0 gives which factor pair?","options":["(x-5)(x+2)","(x-2)(x+5)","(x-10)(x+1)","(x+10)(x-1)"],"answer":0,"explain":"-5 and 2 multiply to -10 and add to -3, so x²-3x-10=(x-5)(x+2)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the factorisation (x-5)(x+2)=0 for x²-3x-10=0, what is the positive root?","answer":5,"explain":"Setting x-5=0 gives x=5."}]},{"id":"qe4","title":"The Blueprint Restored","icon":"🏗️","kind":"lesson","story":"With factorisation mastered, you return to the prayer hall's foundation stone. Its exact dimensions — and several other ancient puzzles involving numbers and integers — are waiting to be measured out with the same technique.","teach":[{"title":"Solving the Prayer Hall","body":"The breadth x of the prayer hall satisfies 2x²+x−300=0. Splitting the middle term: 2x²−24x+25x−300=0 → 2x(x−12)+25(x−12)=0 → (x−12)(2x+25)=0, giving x=12 or x=−12.5. Since breadth can't be negative, the breadth is 12 m and the length (2x+1) is 25 m.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Real Roots, Real-World Sense","body":"Factorisation often gives two mathematical roots, but only one may make sense in context — negative lengths, negative counts, or negative ages are usually rejected.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"Two Numbers, One Product","body":"To find two numbers with sum 27 and product 182, let one number be x; the other is 27−x. Then x(27−x)=182 leads to x²−27x+182=0, which factorises to give the numbers 13 and 14.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The prayer hall's breadth satisfies (x-12)(2x+25)=0 with x>0. What is the breadth in metres?","answer":12,"explain":"x=12 is the only positive root; x=-12.5 is rejected since breadth can't be negative."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Given the hall's breadth is 12 m and its length is (2x+1) m, what is the length in metres?","answer":25,"explain":"Length = 2(12)+1 = 25 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two consecutive positive integers have squares summing to 365 (n²+(n+1)²=365 leads to n²+n-182=0, which factorises as (n-13)(n+14)=0). What is the larger integer?","answer":14,"explain":"n=13 is the positive root, so the consecutive integers are 13 and 14; the larger is 14."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two numbers have sum 27 and product 182. If one number is x, which quadratic equation models this?","options":["x² − 27x + 182 = 0","x² + 27x − 182 = 0","27x² − x + 182 = 0","x² − 182x + 27 = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"The other number is 27-x, so x(27-x)=182 gives x²-27x+182=0."}]},{"id":"qe5","title":"The Sridharacharya Formula","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"Legend says the mathematician Sridharacharya once completed the square to forge a universal key — a formula that opens ANY quadratic equation, whether or not it splits neatly. Retrieve the quadratic formula from his scroll.","teach":[{"title":"The Universal Formula","body":"The roots of ax²+bx+c=0 are given by x = [−b ± √(b²−4ac)] / 2a, provided b²−4ac ≥ 0. This formula — credited to ancient Indian mathematicians including Sridharacharya, who derived it by completing the square — solves any quadratic equation directly from its coefficients a, b, c.","visual":"🔮"},{"title":"The Discriminant","body":"The quantity b²−4ac inside the square root is called the discriminant, because it determines the nature of the roots: whether they are real and distinct, real and equal, or not real at all.","visual":"🔱"},{"title":"Applying the Formula","body":"A pole is placed on the boundary of a circular park (diameter 13 m) so its distance x from gate B satisfies x²+7x−60=0. Here a=1, b=7, c=−60, so the discriminant is 7²−4(1)(−60)=289. Since 289>0, real roots exist: x=(−7±√289)/2=(−7±17)/2, giving x=5 or x=−12. Since x is a distance, x=−12 is rejected, so x=5.","visual":"🎪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the equation x² + 7x − 60 = 0, compute the discriminant b² − 4ac.","answer":289,"explain":"7² - 4(1)(-60) = 49+240 = 289."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the quadratic formula on x²+7x-60=0, what is the positive root?","answer":5,"explain":"x = (-7±√289)/2 = (-7±17)/2, giving x=5 or x=-12; only x=5 is a valid distance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The quadratic formula for ax²+bx+c=0 is:","options":["x = [−b ± √(b²−4ac)] / 2a","x = [−b ± √(b²+4ac)] / 2a","x = [b ± √(b²−4ac)] / a","x = [−b² ± 4ac] / 2a"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the standard quadratic formula, valid when b²-4ac≥0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the name given to the quantity b² − 4ac?","options":["the discriminant","the coefficient sum","the root product","the leading term"],"answer":0,"explain":"b²-4ac is called the discriminant because it tells us the nature of the roots."}]},{"id":"qe6","title":"The Discriminant Oracle","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"At the heart of the ruins sits an oracle that speaks only in discriminants. Ask it about any quadratic equation, and it will reveal whether real roots exist at all — two distinct, two equal, or none.","teach":[{"title":"Three Fates of a Quadratic","body":"For ax²+bx+c=0: if b²−4ac>0, there are two distinct real roots; if b²−4ac=0, there are two equal real roots; if b²−4ac<0, there are no real roots, since no real number squares to a negative value.","visual":"🌗"},{"title":"No Real Roots Example","body":"For 2x²−4x+3=0, a=2, b=−4, c=3, so the discriminant is (−4)²−4(2)(3)=16−24=−8, which is less than 0. So this equation has no real roots.","visual":"❌"},{"title":"Equal Roots Example","body":"For 3x²−2x+1/3=0, the discriminant is (−2)²−4(3)(1/3)=4−4=0. So the equation has two equal real roots, both equal to −b/2a = 2/6 = 1/3.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the discriminant of 2x² − 4x + 3 = 0.","answer":-8,"explain":"(-4)²-4(2)(3)=16-24=-8."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An equation with discriminant −8 has:","options":["no real roots","two distinct real roots","two equal real roots","exactly one root"],"answer":0,"explain":"A negative discriminant means no real number satisfies the equation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the discriminant of 3x² − 2x + 1/3 = 0.","answer":0,"explain":"(-2)²-4(3)(1/3)=4-4=0, so the roots are equal."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A rectangular park has perimeter 80 m and area 400 m². Solving gives x² − 40x + 400 = 0, whose discriminant is 0. What does this tell you about the park's shape?","options":["It must be a square (length = breadth = 20 m)","It's impossible to build","It has two different valid shapes","It must be a triangle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Discriminant 0 gives a repeated root x=20, so length=breadth=20 m — a square."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For a mango grove: length is twice the breadth, and area is 800 m². If breadth = x, solving 2x²=800 gives x²=400. What is the breadth in metres?","answer":20,"explain":"x²=400 gives x=20 (rejecting the negative root, since breadth can't be negative)."}]},{"id":"qeboss","title":"Al-Khwarizmi's Trial","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"The final chamber opens onto a guardian assembled from every scroll you've studied — Babylonian tablets, Brahmagupta's formula, Sridharacharya's scroll. Solve its trial of quadratic equations to claim the vault's treasure.","teach":[{"title":"Your Full Toolkit","body":"You now know three ways to solve ax²+bx+c=0: factorisation (splitting the middle term), the quadratic formula x=[−b±√(b²−4ac)]/2a, and the discriminant b²−4ac to predict how many real roots exist before you even solve.","visual":"🧰"}],"boss":{"name":"Al-Khwarizmi's Shade","taunt":"Every equation is a locked door, mortal. Prove you can open them all — by factor, by formula, or by discriminant — or remain trapped in these ruins forever."},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which value of k makes kx² + 2x − 3 = 0 NOT a quadratic equation?","options":["k=0","k=1","k=-1","k=5"],"answer":0,"explain":"A quadratic equation needs a≠0; k=0 removes the x² term entirely."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Solve 2x² + x − 6 = 0 by factorisation. What is the positive root?","answer":1.5,"explain":"Splitting gives 2x(x+2)-3(x+2)=(2x-3)(x+2)=0, so roots are 3/2 and -2; the positive root is 1.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the discriminant of 2x² − 6x + 3 = 0.","answer":12,"explain":"(-6)²-4(2)(3)=36-24=12."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An equation with discriminant 12 has:","options":["two distinct real roots","two equal real roots","no real roots","no solution method"],"answer":0,"explain":"A positive discriminant (12>0) always gives two distinct real roots."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cottage industry's daily cost per pottery article is (2n+3) rupees for n articles, and the total cost is ₹90, giving 2n²+3n−90=0, which factorises as (2n+15)(n-6)=0. How many articles were produced?","answer":6,"explain":"n=6 is the positive root (n=-7.5 is rejected), so 6 articles were produced."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A right triangle has base x and altitude (x−7), with hypotenuse 13 cm. This gives x²−7x−60=0, factorising as (x-12)(x+5)=0. What is the base in cm?","answer":12,"explain":"x=12 is the positive root, giving a 5-12-13 right triangle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A quadratic equation ax²+bx+c=0 has two equal real roots exactly when:","options":["b²−4ac=0","b²−4ac>0","b²−4ac<0","a=0"],"answer":0,"explain":"A zero discriminant means the two roots coincide."}]}]},{"id":"arithmeticprogressions10","index":4,"name":"Arithmetic Progressions","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 5","emoji":"🔁","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"ap1","title":"What Is an AP?","icon":"🪜","kind":"lesson","story":"Reena just got a job offer: ₹8000 a month, with a ₹500 raise every year. Climbing a ladder whose rungs shrink by a steady 2 cm as you go up feels the same way — a hidden fixed step links every number to the next.","teach":[{"title":"A list with a fixed step","body":"An arithmetic progression (AP) is a list of numbers in which each term is obtained by adding a fixed number to the preceding term, except the first term. That fixed number is called the common difference (d), and it can be positive, negative, or zero. Reena's salary 8000, 8500, 9000, ... is an AP with d = 500; a ladder's rungs 45, 43, 41, 39, ... (cm) is an AP with d = -2.","visual":"🪜"},{"title":"The general form","body":"If the first term is a and the common difference is d, the AP looks like a, a+d, a+2d, a+3d, .... If a = 6 and d = 3, the AP is 6, 9, 12, 15, .... If a = 6 and d = -3, the AP is 6, 3, 0, -3, .... If d = 0, every term stays the same, like 2, 2, 2, 2, ....","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Finite and infinite APs","body":"An AP with a definite last term, like the heights of students in a queue (147, 148, ..., 157 cm), is called a finite AP. An AP that goes on forever, like 1, 2, 3, 4, ..., is called an infinite AP and has no last term.","visual":"♾️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these lists is an arithmetic progression (AP)?","options":["-3, -2, -1, 0, ...","1, 2, 4, 8, ...","1, 3, 9, 27, ...","1, 4, 9, 16, ..."],"answer":0,"explain":"Each term in -3, -2, -1, 0, ... is exactly 1 more than the one before it, a fixed common difference — the other lists don't add a fixed number each time."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A ladder's bottom rung is 45 cm long, and each rung above is 2 cm shorter than the one below it. How long (in cm) is the 3rd rung?","answer":41,"explain":"The rungs form the AP 45, 43, 41, ... — so the 3rd rung is 45 - 2 - 2 = 41 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For an AP with first term a = 6 and common difference d = -3, what are the first four terms?","options":["6, 3, 0, -3","6, 9, 12, 15","6, 3, -3, -9","6, -3, 6, -3"],"answer":0,"explain":"Starting at 6 and repeatedly subtracting 3 gives 6, 3, 0, -3 — a negative common difference means the AP decreases."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Reena's starting monthly salary is ₹8000, with an annual increment of ₹500. What is her monthly salary (in ₹) in the 2nd year?","answer":8500,"explain":"Adding the common difference once to the first term: 8000 + 500 = 8500."}]},{"id":"ap2","title":"Spotting an AP: Finding a and d","icon":"🔍","kind":"lesson","story":"Not every list that looks orderly is actually an AP. Before you can use any AP formula, you first need to play detective and check whether the gap between consecutive terms truly stays the same.","teach":[{"title":"The consecutive-difference test","body":"For a list a1, a2, a3, ..., it is an AP only if ak+1 - ak is the same value for every k. You only need to check this once you trust the pattern — but to be sure, compute it for a few pairs. Always subtract the earlier term from the later one, even if the later term is smaller.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Worked checks","body":"For 4, 10, 16, 22, ...: differences are 6, 6, 6 — it's an AP with d = 6, so the next two terms are 28 and 34. For 1, -1, -3, -5, ...: differences are -2, -2, -2 — an AP with d = -2, next terms -7 and -9. For -2, 2, -2, 2, -2, ...: differences are 4, -4, 4 — not the same, so it is NOT an AP.","visual":"✅"},{"title":"Fractions work the same way","body":"For the AP 3/2, 1/2, -1/2, -3/2, ..., the first term is a = 3/2, and d = 1/2 - 3/2 = -1. The common difference can always be found using any two consecutive terms once you know the list is an AP.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these lists is NOT an AP?","options":["-2, 2, -2, 2, -2, ...","4, 10, 16, 22, ...","1, -1, -3, -5, ...","-3, -2, -1, 0, ..."],"answer":0,"explain":"Its consecutive differences alternate between 4 and -4 instead of staying fixed, so it fails the AP test."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the AP 4, 10, 16, 22, ..., what is the term right after 22?","answer":28,"explain":"The common difference is 6, so the next term is 22 + 6 = 28."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the common difference d of the AP 1, -1, -3, -5, ...?","answer":-2,"explain":"Each term is 2 less than the one before it: -1 - 1 = -2, -3 - (-1) = -2, and so on."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the AP 3/2, 1/2, -1/2, -3/2, ..., what is the common difference d?","answer":-1,"explain":"d = 1/2 - 3/2 = -1, confirmed by -1/2 - 1/2 = -1 as well."}]},{"id":"ap3","title":"The nth Term Formula","icon":"🚀","kind":"lesson","story":"Adding ₹500 again and again to find Reena's salary in her 25th year would take forever. There's a shortcut hiding in the pattern — one formula that jumps straight to any term you want.","teach":[{"title":"Deriving the formula","body":"For an AP with first term a and common difference d: a2 = a + d = a + (2-1)d, a3 = a2 + d = a + 2d = a + (3-1)d, a4 = a + 3d = a + (4-1)d. The pattern continues, giving the nth term (or general term): an = a + (n-1)d.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Using the formula","body":"For the AP 2, 7, 12, ..., a = 2 and d = 5. The 10th term is a10 = a + (10-1)d = 2 + 9 x 5 = 2 + 45 = 47. If there are m terms in an AP, the last term am is often written as l.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the AP 2, 7, 12, ..., find the 10th term using an = a + (n-1)d.","answer":47,"explain":"a = 2, d = 5, so a10 = 2 + (10-1) x 5 = 2 + 45 = 47."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Reena's salary AP has a = 8000 and d = 500. Using an = a + (n-1)d, what is her monthly salary (in ₹) in the 15th year?","answer":15000,"explain":"a15 = 8000 + (15-1) x 500 = 8000 + 7000 = 15000."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same salary AP (a = 8000, d = 500), what would Reena's monthly salary (in ₹) be in the 25th year?","answer":20000,"explain":"a25 = 8000 + (25-1) x 500 = 8000 + 12000 = 20000."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct formula for the nth term of an AP with first term a and common difference d?","options":["an = a + (n-1)d","an = a + nd","an = a x d^(n-1)","an = n x a + d"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nth term formula is an = a + (n-1)d, derived by adding d exactly (n-1) times to the first term."}]},{"id":"ap4","title":"Which Term? Is It Even There?","icon":"🕵️","kind":"lesson","story":"A mysterious number shows up — is it hiding somewhere inside a given AP, and if so, which position does it occupy? The nth term formula can be turned around to answer exactly that.","teach":[{"title":"Solving for n","body":"For the AP 21, 18, 15, ... (a = 21, d = -3), to check if -81 is a term, set an = -81: -81 = 21 + (n-1)(-3), which gives n = 35. So -81 is the 35th term. Checking if any term is 0: 21 + (n-1)(-3) = 0 gives n = 8, so the 8th term is 0.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"When a number is NOT a term","body":"For the list 5, 11, 17, 23, ... (a = 5, d = 6), testing whether 301 is a term: 301 = 5 + (n-1) x 6 gives n = 50.33..., which is not a positive integer. Since n must be a positive whole number, 301 is NOT a term of this AP.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"Counting terms in a range","body":"The two-digit numbers divisible by 3 form the AP 12, 15, 18, ..., 99, with a = 12, d = 3, and last term 99. Solving 99 = 12 + (n-1) x 3 gives n = 30 — so there are 30 such numbers.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Which term of the AP 21, 18, 15, ... is -81?","answer":35,"explain":"Solving -81 = 21 + (n-1)(-3) gives n = 35."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Is 301 a term of the list 5, 11, 17, 23, ...?","options":["No, because n would not come out as a positive integer","Yes, it is the 30th term","Yes, it is the 50th term","Yes, it is the 51st term"],"answer":0,"explain":"Solving 301 = 5 + (n-1) x 6 gives n = 50.33..., not a whole number, so 301 never appears in this AP."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many two-digit numbers are divisible by 3?","answer":30,"explain":"They form the AP 12, 15, ..., 99 with a=12, d=3; solving 99 = 12 + (n-1) x 3 gives n = 30."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the AP 21, 18, 15, ..., which term number equals 0?","answer":8,"explain":"Solving 21 + (n-1)(-3) = 0 gives n = 8, so the 8th term is 0."}]},{"id":"ap5","title":"Rebuilding an AP & Counting From the End","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Sometimes you're only told two scattered terms of an AP and must reconstruct the whole sequence. Other times you must count backwards from the very last rung of a ladder instead of the first.","teach":[{"title":"Finding a and d from two terms","body":"If the 3rd term of an AP is 5 and the 7th term is 9, write both using an = a+(n-1)d: a + 2d = 5 and a + 6d = 9. Solving these two equations together gives d = 1 and a = 3, so the AP is 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, ....","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Counting terms from the last term","body":"For the AP 10, 7, 4, ..., -62 (a=10, d=-3, last term l=-62), first find the total number of terms using l = a+(n-1)d, which gives n = 25. The 11th term from the last term is then the 15th term from the start: a15 = 10 + 14 x (-3) = -32.","visual":"⏪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An AP has 3rd term = 5 and 7th term = 9. What is its common difference d?","answer":1,"explain":"Subtracting a+2d=5 from a+6d=9 gives 4d=4, so d=1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same AP (3rd term 5, 7th term 9), what is the first term a?","answer":3,"explain":"Since a + 2d = 5 and d = 1, a = 5 - 2 = 3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many terms are there in total in the AP 10, 7, 4, ..., -62?","answer":25,"explain":"Solving -62 = 10 + (n-1)(-3) gives n = 25."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the AP 10, 7, 4, ..., -62, what is the 11th term counted from the last term?","answer":-32,"explain":"The 11th term from the last is the 15th term from the start: a15 = 10 + 14 x (-3) = -32."}]},{"id":"ap6","title":"Sum of First n Terms — The Gauss Trick","icon":"➕","kind":"lesson","story":"A young Carl Gauss was asked to add every number from 1 to 100 — and found a shortcut in seconds by pairing numbers from both ends. That same trick unlocks a formula for summing any AP instantly.","teach":[{"title":"Pairing from both ends","body":"Gauss wrote S = 1+2+...+100, then reversed it as S = 100+99+...+1, and added the two: each pair (1+100), (2+99), ... equals 101, and there are 100 such pairs, so 2S = 100 x 101, giving S = 5050.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"The general sum formula","body":"Using the same pairing trick on a general AP gives: S = n/2 [2a + (n-1)d]. Since a + (n-1)d = an, this can also be written as S = n/2 (a + an), or S = n/2 (a + l) when l is the last term.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Applying the formula","body":"Shakila puts ₹100 in a money box on birthday 1, then increases the amount by ₹50 each year (a=100, d=50). To find the total on the 21st birthday, use S = n/2 [2a+(n-1)d] with n=21: S21 = 21/2 [200 + 20x50] = 21/2 x 1200 = ₹12600.","visual":"💰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Gauss's pairing trick, what is the sum of the first 100 positive integers (1+2+...+100)?","answer":5050,"explain":"Pairing gives 100 pairs that each sum to 101: 2S = 100 x 101, so S = 5050."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Shakila puts ₹100 in a box in year 1 and adds ₹50 more each year (a=100, d=50). Using S = n/2[2a+(n-1)d] with n=21, how much money (in ₹) is in the box by her 21st birthday?","answer":12600,"explain":"S21 = 21/2 [2x100 + 20x50] = 21/2 x 1200 = 12600."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula correctly gives the sum of the first n terms of an AP?","options":["S = n/2 [2a + (n-1)d]","S = n [a + (n-1)d]","S = n/2 [a - (n-1)d]","S = (n-1)/2 [2a + d]"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the standard AP sum formula, derived by pairing terms from both ends of the list."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the sum of the first 22 terms of the AP 8, 3, -2, ... (a=8, d=-5).","answer":-979,"explain":"S22 = 22/2 [2x8 + 21x(-5)] = 11 x (16 - 105) = 11 x (-89) = -979."}]},{"id":"ap7","title":"Sum Formula in Action","icon":"🏗️","kind":"lesson","story":"A TV factory's yearly output, a contractor's daily penalty, a flower bed's shrinking rows — real problems rarely hand you a and d directly. The sum formula lets you dig them out from partial clues.","teach":[{"title":"Finding d and a term from a known sum","body":"If S14 = 1050 and a = 10, use Sn = n/2[2a+(n-1)d]: 1050 = 14/2[20+13d] = 140+91d, giving d = 10. Then a20 = 10 + 19x10 = 200.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Finding n from a known sum","body":"For the AP 24, 21, 18, ... (a=24, d=-3), to find how many terms sum to 78, solving the resulting quadratic n^2 - 17n + 52 = 0 gives n = 4 or n = 13. Both are valid answers — because a is positive and d is negative, the terms from position 5 to 13 add up to exactly zero.","visual":"🎲"},{"title":"A real production problem","body":"A TV manufacturer made 600 sets in year 3 and 700 in year 7, increasing uniformly each year. Setting a+2d=600 and a+6d=700 gives d=25 and a=550. So production in year 1 is 550, year 10 is a10 = 550+9x25 = 775, and total production over the first 7 years is S7 = 7/2[2x550+6x25] = 4375.","visual":"📺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An AP has first term a=10, and the sum of its first 14 terms is S14=1050. What is the common difference d?","answer":10,"explain":"1050 = 14/2[20+13d] = 140+91d, so 91d = 910 and d = 10."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many terms of the AP 24, 21, 18, ... must be taken so that their sum is 78?","options":["4 or 13","Only 4","Only 13","6"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both n=4 and n=13 give a sum of 78, since the terms from the 5th to the 13th cancel out to zero."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A TV manufacturer produced 600 sets in year 3 and 700 sets in year 7, with uniform yearly increase. What was the production in year 1?","answer":550,"explain":"Solving a+2d=600 and a+6d=700 gives d=25 and a=550."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same manufacturer data (a=550, d=25), what is the total production over the first 7 years?","answer":4375,"explain":"S7 = 7/2 [2x550 + 6x25] = 7/2 x 1250 = 4375."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same manufacturer data (a=550, d=25), what is the production in year 10?","answer":775,"explain":"a10 = 550 + 9x25 = 775."}]},{"id":"ap-boss","title":"The Progression Overseer","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Progression Overseer","taunt":"Terms marching forward forever by a fixed step — spot the pattern, jump to any term, and sum them all before I catch up!"},"story":"One final challenge across everything in this world — recognising APs, finding terms, and summing them using every trick you've collected: from ladders and salaries to money boxes and TV factories.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an AP, what is added to each term to get the next term?","options":["A fixed number called the common difference","The term's position number","A fixed ratio","Nothing, terms are random"],"answer":0,"explain":"By definition, an AP adds the same fixed common difference to move from one term to the next."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the AP 5, 11, 17, 23, ... (a=5, d=6), what is the 12th term?","answer":71,"explain":"a12 = 5 + (12-1) x 6 = 5 + 66 = 71."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Reena's salary AP has a=8000 and d=500. What is her monthly salary (in ₹) in the 10th year?","answer":12500,"explain":"a10 = 8000 + (10-1) x 500 = 8000 + 4500 = 12500."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The formula S = n/2 (a + l) is most useful when you know...","options":["The first term a and the last term l, but not necessarily d","Only the common difference d","Nothing about the AP at all","Only the number of negative terms"],"answer":0,"explain":"This form of the sum formula avoids needing d directly, as long as a, l, and n are known."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A flower bed has 23 rose plants in row 1, 21 in row 2, 19 in row 3, and so on, decreasing by 2 each row, down to 5 plants in the last row. How many rows are there?","answer":10,"explain":"Solving 5 = 23 + (n-1)(-2) gives n = 10."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"₹1000 is invested at 8% simple interest per year, so the interest earned each year forms an AP with a=80 and d=80. What is the interest (in ₹) at the end of 30 years?","answer":2400,"explain":"a30 = 80 + (30-1) x 80 = 80 + 2320 = 2400."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ladder's rungs decrease uniformly from 45 cm at the bottom by 2 cm per rung going up. What is the length of the 4th rung?","options":["39 cm","43 cm","37 cm","41 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rungs are 45, 43, 41, 39, ... so the 4th rung is 39 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A list of numbers has nth term an = 3 + 2n. Find the sum of its first 24 terms.","answer":672,"explain":"The list is 5, 7, 9, ... (a=5, d=2); S24 = 24/2 [2x5 + 23x2] = 12 x 56 = 672."}]}]},{"id":"triangles10","index":5,"name":"Triangles","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 6","emoji":"📏","palette":{"from":"#facc15","to":"#713f12"},"lessons":[{"id":"tr1","title":"Two Photographs, One Monument","icon":"🖼️","kind":"lesson","story":"The guide holds up three photos of the Taj Mahal — postcard, stamp, and poster size. 'Same monument, three sizes,' she says. 'Prove you understand why they're all the same shape before we go further into the ruins.'","teach":[{"title":"Similar vs Congruent","body":"Congruent figures have the same shape AND the same size. Similar figures have the same shape but not necessarily the same size — every circle is similar to every other circle, and every square is similar to every other square.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"The Two Conditions","body":"Two polygons with the same number of sides are similar only if (i) all corresponding angles are equal AND (ii) all corresponding sides are in the same ratio (proportion). Both conditions together define similarity.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Scale Factor","body":"The common ratio of corresponding sides between two similar figures is called the scale factor, or representative fraction — the same idea used for maps and building blueprints.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"One Condition Isn't Enough","body":"A square and a rectangle can have all equal angles (90° each) but different side ratios — not similar. A square and a rhombus can have proportional sides but unequal angles — also not similar. Both conditions are needed together.","visual":"🔲"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two figures that have the same shape but not necessarily the same size are called:","options":["similar figures","congruent figures","identical figures","equal figures"],"answer":0,"explain":"Similar figures share shape; congruent figures share shape AND size."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fill in the blank: All circles are ___.","options":["similar","congruent","identical in size","unrelated"],"answer":0,"explain":"Any two circles have the same shape regardless of radius, so all circles are similar."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fill in the blank: All ___ triangles are similar.","options":["equilateral","isosceles","right-angled","scalene"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every equilateral triangle has three 60° angles, so any two equilateral triangles are automatically similar."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A square and a rectangle have all corresponding angles equal (90°) but their sides are not in the same ratio. Are they similar?","options":["No","Yes","Only if they're the same size","Yes, angles alone decide similarity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal angles alone are not enough for polygons — the sides must also be proportional."}]},{"id":"tr2","title":"The Thales Trail","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the ruins, a rope bridge runs parallel to a fallen support beam, slicing both walls of the canyon. The old stonemason's puzzle: prove the rope cuts both walls in the same ratio — or the bridge collapses.","teach":[{"title":"Basic Proportionality Theorem","body":"If a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle so that it intersects the other two sides at distinct points, it divides those two sides in the same ratio. This is Theorem 6.1, also called Thales' Theorem.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Seeing It In Numbers","body":"Mark five equal segments on ray AX up to point B, with D as the third division point, so AD:DB = 3:2. A line through D parallel to BC meets AC at E — measuring shows AE:EC is also 3:2, exactly matching AD:DB.","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"A Useful Rearrangement","body":"If DE is parallel to BC in triangle ABC, meeting AB at D and AC at E, then AD/DB = AE/EC can be rearranged (adding 1 to both sides) to give the equally useful form AD/AB = AE/AC.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In triangle ABC, DE || BC with D on AB and E on AC. If AD:DB = 3:2, what is the ratio AE:EC (as a decimal)?","answer":1.5,"explain":"By the Basic Proportionality Theorem, AD/DB = AE/EC = 3/2 = 1.5."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Theorem 6.1 (Basic Proportionality Theorem) states that a line parallel to one side of a triangle, intersecting the other two sides:","options":["divides those two sides in the same ratio","bisects the triangle's area","makes the triangle isosceles","is always equal in length to that side"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is exactly the statement of the Basic Proportionality (Thales) Theorem."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In triangle ABC, a line DE || BC meets AB at D and AC at E. Which relation is proved in Example 1 of the chapter?","options":["AD/AB = AE/AC","AD/AB = EC/AC","AB/AD = AE/EC","AD × AB = AE × AC"],"answer":0,"explain":"Starting from AD/DB = AE/EC and adding 1 to both sides gives AD/AB = AE/AC."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a triangle, E and F lie on sides PQ and PR with PE = 4 cm, QE = 4.5 cm, PF = 8 cm, RF = 9 cm. Is EF parallel to QR?","options":["Yes, because PE/QE = PF/RF (both 8/9)","No, the ratios are different","Cannot be determined","Only if the triangle is equilateral"],"answer":0,"explain":"PE/QE = 4/4.5 = 8/9 and PF/RF = 8/9 — equal ratios mean EF || QR by the converse of the BPT."}]},{"id":"tr3","title":"Turning the Theorem Around","icon":"🔄","kind":"lesson","story":"Two scouts mark equal-length beads on separate ropes stretching from the same peg. If the beads line up in matching ratios on both ropes, the string between corresponding beads must run perfectly parallel to the base wall — the converse of yesterday's puzzle.","teach":[{"title":"The Converse (Theorem 6.2)","body":"If a line divides any two sides of a triangle in the same ratio, then the line is parallel to the third side. This is the converse of the Basic Proportionality Theorem, proved by assuming otherwise and reaching a contradiction.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Trapezium Puzzle (Example 2)","body":"In trapezium ABCD with AB || DC, points E and F lie on the non-parallel sides AD and BC with EF || AB. Joining diagonal AC and applying Theorem 6.1 twice (in triangles ADC and CAB) proves AE/ED = BF/FC.","visual":"🔷"},{"title":"Proving Isosceles Triangles (Example 3)","body":"If PS/SQ = PT/TR in triangle PQR, then ST || QR by the converse theorem, making angle PST = angle PQR (corresponding angles). Combined with the given angle PST = angle PRQ, this forces angle PQR = angle PRQ, so PQ = PR — triangle PQR is isosceles.","visual":"🔺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The converse of the Basic Proportionality Theorem states that if a line divides two sides of a triangle in the same ratio, then the line is:","options":["parallel to the third side","perpendicular to the third side","equal in length to the third side","the angle bisector of the opposite angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is Theorem 6.2, the converse of the Basic Proportionality Theorem."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On ray AX, points are marked so that AB4:B4B = 4:1. On ray AY, matching points give AC4:C4C in the same ratio for B4C4 to be parallel to BC. What is AC4:C4C (as a single number)?","answer":4,"explain":"For B4C4 to be parallel to BC (as shown in Activity 3), AC4/C4C must equal AB4/B4B = 4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In trapezium ABCD (AB || DC), E and F lie on AD and BC with EF || AB. What does Example 2 prove?","options":["AE/ED = BF/FC","AE/ED = FC/BF","AE × ED = BF × FC","AE + ED = BF + FC"],"answer":0,"explain":"Joining AC and applying the Basic Proportionality Theorem in two triangles gives AE/ED = BF/FC."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In triangle PQR, PS/SQ = PT/TR and angle PST = angle PRQ. What does Example 3 conclude about triangle PQR?","options":["It is isosceles (PQ = PR)","It is equilateral","It is right-angled at P","It is scalene"],"answer":0,"explain":"ST || QR gives angle PST = angle PQR; combined with the given angle PST = angle PRQ, this forces PQ = PR."}]},{"id":"tr4","title":"The Angle-Only Proof","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"A surveyor's sketch shows two triangular plots with matching angles at every corner but wildly different sizes. 'Prove the plots are similar without measuring a single side,' the surveyor challenges.","teach":[{"title":"AAA Similarity (Theorem 6.3)","body":"If corresponding angles of two triangles are equal, their corresponding sides are automatically in the same ratio, so the triangles are similar. This is the AAA (Angle-Angle-Angle) criterion.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"The AA Shortcut","body":"Since the angles of a triangle always sum to 180°, if two angles of one triangle equal two angles of another, the third pair must match too — so only two angle equalities need to be checked. This is the AA similarity criterion.","visual":"✌️"},{"title":"Correspondence Matters (Example 4)","body":"When PQ || RS in a figure where diagonals cross at O, angle P = angle S (alternate angles), angle Q = angle R (alternate angles), and angle POQ = angle SOR (vertically opposite) — so triangle POQ ~ triangle SOR by AAA, written in that exact vertex order.","visual":"✖️"},{"title":"Finding a Missing Angle (Example 5)","body":"For triangles ABC and RQP, if AB/RQ = BC/QP = CA/PR (all equal to 1/2), then ABC ~ RQP by SSS similarity, so angle C = angle P. With angle A = 80° and angle B = 60°, the angle sum property gives angle C = 180° − 80° − 60° = 40°, so angle P = 40°.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If two angles of one triangle are respectively equal to two angles of another triangle, the triangles are similar by the:","options":["AA similarity criterion","SSS congruence criterion","RHS congruence criterion","area criterion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal angle pairs force the third angle pair to match too, giving AA similarity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Example 5, triangle ABC ~ triangle RQP with angle A = 80° and angle B = 60°. What is the measure of angle P (in degrees)?","answer":40,"explain":"Angle C = 180 - 80 - 60 = 40°, and angle P = angle C since C and P are corresponding angles of similar triangles."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Fig. 6.22, vertex A corresponds to D, B to E, and C to F. Which notation is correct for the similarity?","options":["Triangle ABC ~ Triangle DEF","Triangle ABC ~ Triangle EDF","Triangle ABC ~ Triangle FED","Any order is acceptable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Similarity notation must preserve vertex correspondence, just like congruence notation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Example 4, PQ || RS and the lines cross at O. Which criterion proves triangle POQ ~ triangle SOR?","options":["AAA similarity (using alternate angles and vertically opposite angles)","SSS similarity","SAS similarity","RHS similarity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Angle P = angle S and angle Q = angle R (alternate angles), and angle POQ = angle SOR (vertically opposite) — three equal angle pairs give AAA similarity."}]},{"id":"tr5","title":"All Three Sides Agree","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"The blacksmith hands you two triangular blades of totally different sizes. 'Measure every edge,' he says. 'If all three ratios match, I'll forge them from the same mold pattern — no angle-measuring needed.'","teach":[{"title":"SSS Similarity (Theorem 6.4)","body":"If the sides of one triangle are proportional to the sides of another triangle (all three ratios equal), then their corresponding angles are equal, and the triangles are similar. This is the SSS (Side-Side-Side) similarity criterion.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Sides Only, No Angles","body":"In Activity 5, triangle ABC has AB = 3 cm, BC = 6 cm, CA = 8 cm, and triangle DEF has DE = 4.5 cm, EF = 9 cm, FD = 12 cm. Every ratio (AB/DE, BC/EF, CA/FD) equals 2/3, and measuring confirms all corresponding angles are indeed equal.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Not the Same as SSS Congruence","body":"SSS congruence requires the sides to be exactly equal in length; SSS similarity only requires the sides to be in the same ratio. A triangle and its enlargement satisfy SSS similarity but never SSS congruence unless the scale factor is exactly 1.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The SSS similarity criterion says two triangles are similar when:","options":["all three pairs of corresponding sides are in the same ratio","any one pair of sides is equal","all three angles are equal but sides are unrelated","the perimeters are equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Proportional sides (same ratio for all three pairs) is exactly the SSS similarity condition."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Triangle ABC has AB = 3 cm and BC = 6 cm. It is similar to triangle DEF with DE = 4.5 cm. Using AB/DE = BC/EF, what is EF (in cm)?","answer":9,"explain":"AB/DE = 3/4.5 = 2/3, so EF = BC ÷ (2/3) = 6 × 1.5 = 9 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is SSS similarity different from SSS congruence?","options":["Similarity needs proportional sides; congruence needs equal sides","They are identical conditions","Similarity needs equal sides; congruence needs proportional sides","Congruence ignores side lengths entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Congruent triangles must have exactly equal corresponding sides, while similar triangles only need the sides in the same ratio."}]},{"id":"tr6","title":"One Angle, Two Sides","icon":"📊","kind":"lesson","story":"A cartographer sketches two triangular plots sharing one identical 50° corner angle, with the two sides meeting that angle in matching proportion. 'That's enough to prove they're similar,' she says — 'no need to check the third side or the other two angles.'","teach":[{"title":"SAS Similarity (Theorem 6.5)","body":"If one angle of a triangle equals one angle of another triangle, and the sides including those angles are proportional, then the two triangles are similar. This is the SAS (Side-Angle-Side) similarity criterion.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Seeing It In Numbers","body":"In Activity 6, triangle ABC has AB = 2 cm, AC = 4 cm, angle A = 50°; triangle DEF has DE = 3 cm, DF = 6 cm, angle D = 50°. AB/DE = AC/DF = 2/3, and angle A = angle D, so by SAS similarity, triangle ABC ~ triangle DEF.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Using a Product Relation (Example 6)","body":"If OA . OB = OC . OD, this rearranges to OA/OC = OD/OB. Combined with angle AOD = angle COB (vertically opposite angles), triangle AOD ~ triangle COB by SAS similarity, giving angle A = angle C and angle D = angle B.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The SAS similarity criterion requires:","options":["one equal angle, with the two sides including it proportional","two equal angles only","three proportional sides","one proportional side and one equal angle anywhere in the triangle"],"answer":0,"explain":"SAS similarity needs the included angle to match and the two sides around it to be in the same ratio."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Activity 6, triangle ABC has AB = 2 cm, AC = 4 cm, angle A = 50°. Triangle DEF has angle D = 50° and DE = 3 cm, with AB/DE = AC/DF. What is DF (in cm)?","answer":6,"explain":"AB/DE = 2/3, so DF = AC ÷ (2/3) = 4 × 1.5 = 6 cm, matching the SAS similarity ratio."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Example 6, given OA . OB = OC . OD and angle AOD = angle COB (vertically opposite), triangles AOD and COB are similar by:","options":["SAS similarity criterion","AAA similarity criterion","SSS similarity criterion","They are not similar"],"answer":0,"explain":"OA.OB = OC.OD rearranges to OA/OC = OD/OB; one included angle pair equal plus proportional sides gives SAS similarity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"From Example 6 (triangle AOD ~ triangle COB), which angle equality follows?","options":["angle A = angle C","angle A = angle B","angle O = angle O only, nothing else","angle D = angle O"],"answer":0,"explain":"Corresponding angles of similar triangles AOD and COB give angle A = angle C and angle D = angle B."}]},{"id":"tr7","title":"Shadows That Measure the World","icon":"🏮","kind":"lesson","story":"At dusk, a lamp-post throws a long shadow across the courtyard. A fellow traveler jokes that this is exactly how ancient surveyors measured mountains and the distance to the moon — using nothing but similar triangles and a shadow.","teach":[{"title":"Indirect Measurement (Example 7)","body":"A 90 cm (0.9 m) tall girl walks away from a 3.6 m lamp-post at 1.2 m/s. After 4 seconds she has walked BD = 1.2 × 4 = 4.8 m. Since the lamp-post and the girl both stand vertical, triangle ABE ~ triangle CDE by AA similarity (equal right angles, shared angle E).","visual":"🏮"},{"title":"Solving for the Shadow","body":"From triangle ABE ~ triangle CDE, BE/DE = AB/CD, giving (4.8 + x)/x = 3.6/0.9 = 4. Solving: 4.8 + x = 4x, so 3x = 4.8, and x = 1.6 m — the girl's shadow after 4 seconds.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Medians of Similar Triangles (Example 8)","body":"If triangle ABC ~ triangle PQR with CM and RN as their respective medians, then triangle AMC ~ triangle PNR (SAS) and triangle CMB ~ triangle RNQ (SSS), giving CM/RN = AB/PQ — the medians are in the very same ratio as the corresponding sides.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"A Bonus Case: RHS Similarity","body":"For right triangles specifically, if the hypotenuse and one side of one triangle are proportional to the hypotenuse and one side of another, the triangles are similar — this is called the RHS similarity criterion.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 0.9 m tall girl walks away from a 3.6 m lamp-post at 1.2 m/s. Using similar triangles, find her shadow length x (in metres) after 4 seconds, given (4.8 + x)/x = 4.","answer":1.6,"explain":"4.8 + x = 4x gives 3x = 4.8, so x = 1.6 m, exactly as in Example 7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same problem, how far has the girl walked from the lamp-post after 4 seconds, at 1.2 m/s (in metres)?","answer":4.8,"explain":"Distance = speed × time = 1.2 m/s × 4 s = 4.8 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Example 8, CM and RN are medians of similar triangles ABC and PQR. What does the example prove about CM/RN?","options":["CM/RN equals the same ratio as the corresponding sides (e.g. AB/PQ)","CM/RN is always 1","CM and RN are always equal in length","CM/RN cannot be determined from similarity alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Example 8 shows CM/RN = AB/PQ = BC/QR = CA/PR — medians scale exactly like the sides."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The RHS similarity criterion applies specifically to:","options":["right triangles, comparing hypotenuse and one side","any two triangles with one equal angle","isosceles triangles only","triangles with equal perimeters"],"answer":0,"explain":"RHS similarity compares the hypotenuse and one other side of two right triangles, proportionally."}]},{"id":"tr8","title":"The Proportion Phantom","icon":"👻","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the ruins, a shifting apparition made of overlapping triangles blocks the exit — its shape endlessly rescaling, daring you to prove exactly how similarity, proportion, and parallel lines hold it together.","boss":{"name":"The Proportion Phantom","taunt":"Every shadow you've measured, every ratio you've matched — now prove you can see through ALL of it at once, traveler!"},"teach":[{"title":"Recap: Three Similarity Criteria","body":"AAA/AA needs equal angles, SSS needs proportional sides, and SAS needs one equal angle with the two including sides proportional. Any one of these three is enough to prove two triangles similar.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Recap: Proportional Lines","body":"The Basic Proportionality Theorem and its converse connect parallel lines to equal ratios on the other two sides of a triangle — go one way (parallel implies ratio) or the other (ratio implies parallel).","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In triangle ABC, DE || BC with D on AB, E on AC. If AD = 4 cm, DB = 2 cm, and AC = 9 cm, find AE (in cm).","answer":6,"explain":"AD/DB = AE/EC = 2, so AE = 2×EC and AE+EC = 9, giving EC = 3 and AE = 6 cm — the Basic Proportionality Theorem."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which similarity criterion needs only two pairs of equal angles?","options":["AA similarity criterion","SSS similarity criterion","SAS similarity criterion","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"AA similarity criterion: two equal angle pairs force the third pair to match too."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which similarity criterion compares all three pairs of sides only, with no angles given?","options":["SSS similarity criterion","AA similarity criterion","SAS similarity criterion","RHS similarity criterion"],"answer":0,"explain":"SSS similarity uses three proportional side pairs and no given angles."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which similarity criterion needs exactly one equal angle plus the two sides including it in proportion?","options":["SAS similarity criterion","AA similarity criterion","SSS similarity criterion","AAA similarity criterion"],"answer":0,"explain":"SAS similarity: one included angle equal, plus the two sides around it proportional."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two similar triangles have angles A = 70° and B = 50° in the first triangle. What is the third angle, C (in degrees)?","answer":60,"explain":"By the angle sum property, C = 180 - 70 - 50 = 60°."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A girl's shadow is 1.6 m long and she has walked 4.8 m from a lamp-post. What is the total distance from the lamp-post's base to the tip of her shadow (in metres)?","answer":6.4,"explain":"Total distance = distance walked + shadow length = 4.8 + 1.6 = 6.4 m, matching Example 7's figures."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two figures having the same shape but not necessarily the same size are called:","options":["similar figures","congruent figures","equal figures","parallel figures"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is the definition of similar figures given at the start of the chapter."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement is correct?","options":["All congruent figures are similar, but similar figures need not be congruent","All similar figures are congruent","Congruent and similar mean exactly the same thing","No congruent figure can ever be similar to another"],"answer":0,"explain":"Congruence requires equal size and shape; similarity only requires equal shape, so congruent implies similar but not vice versa."}]}]},{"id":"coordinategeometry10","index":6,"name":"Coordinate Geometry","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 7","emoji":"🗺️","palette":{"from":"#2dd4bf","to":"#134e4a"},"lessons":[{"id":"cg1","title":"The Cartographer's Puzzle","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"The Study Atlas hands you your first commission: two frontier towns need a road, but no one has measured the distance between them. Grab your grid paper and Pythagoras's oldest trick — you're about to become the map's official distance-finder.","teach":[{"title":"Abscissa and Ordinate","body":"Every point on the grid has a x-coordinate (abscissa), its distance from the y-axis, and a y-coordinate (ordinate), its distance from the x-axis. Points on the x-axis look like (x, 0); points on the y-axis look like (0, y).","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Building the Distance Formula","body":"To find the distance between P(x1, y1) and Q(x2, y2), draw perpendiculars to the x-axis and apply the Pythagoras theorem to the right triangle formed. This gives PQ = the square root of (x2 - x1) squared plus (y2 - y1) squared — the distance formula.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Distance from the Origin","body":"A useful special case: the distance of a point P(x, y) from the origin O(0, 0) is simply the square root of x squared plus y squared.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Points A(4, 0) and B(6, 0) lie on the x-axis. What is the distance AB?","answer":2,"explain":"Both points lie on the x-axis, so AB = OB - OA = 6 - 4 = 2 units."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Points C(0, 3) and D(0, 8) lie on the y-axis. What is the distance CD?","answer":5,"explain":"Both points lie on the y-axis, so CD = 8 - 3 = 5 units."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the distance formula, find the distance AC where A(4, 0) and C(0, 3).","answer":5,"explain":"AC = sqrt((4-0)^2 + (0-3)^2) = sqrt(16 + 9) = sqrt(25) = 5 units."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Town B is 36 km east and 15 km north of Town A. Taking A as the origin, how far apart are the towns (in km)?","answer":39,"explain":"Distance = sqrt(36^2 + 15^2) = sqrt(1296 + 225) = sqrt(1521) = 39 km."}]},{"id":"cg2","title":"Shapes in the Wild","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in uncharted terrain you find three ancient stone markers — do they form a triangle, and what kind? Further along, four crumbling ruins might be the corners of a perfect square. Your compass and the distance formula will tell the truth.","teach":[{"title":"Identifying a Triangle","body":"Given three points, compute the three side lengths with the distance formula. If the sum of any two distances is greater than the third, the points form a triangle. If the square of one side equals the sum of squares of the other two, the converse of the Pythagoras theorem tells you it's a right triangle.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Spotting a Square","body":"To check if four points form a square, find all four side lengths and both diagonals. If all four sides are equal and both diagonals are equal, the quadrilateral is a square.","visual":"⬜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For P(3, 2), Q(-2, -3), R(2, 3): PQ = sqrt(50), QR = sqrt(52), PR = sqrt(2). Since PQ^2 + PR^2 = QR^2, what type of triangle is PQR?","options":["Right triangle (right angle at P)","Equilateral triangle","Obtuse triangle","Not a triangle at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"PQ^2 + PR^2 = 50 + 2 = 52 = QR^2, so by the converse of the Pythagoras theorem, angle P = 90 degrees."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is PR, the distance between P(3, 2) and R(2, 3)?","options":["sqrt(2) units","sqrt(50) units","sqrt(52) units","2 units"],"answer":0,"explain":"PR = sqrt((3-2)^2 + (2-3)^2) = sqrt(1+1) = sqrt(2) units."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For A(1,7), B(4,2), C(-1,-1), D(-4,4): all four sides equal sqrt(34), and both diagonals AC and BD equal sqrt(68). What shape is ABCD?","options":["Square","Rectangle that is not a square","Rhombus that is not a square","Parallelogram only"],"answer":0,"explain":"All four sides are equal AND both diagonals are equal, so ABCD is a square."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For points (5, -2), (6, 4), (7, -2): find the distance between (5, -2) and (7, -2).","answer":2,"explain":"Distance = sqrt((7-5)^2 + (-2-(-2))^2) = sqrt(4+0) = 2 units."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Are (5, -2), (6, 4) and (7, -2) the vertices of an isosceles triangle?","options":["Yes, since two sides both equal sqrt(37)","No, all three sides are different lengths","No, it's an equilateral triangle instead","Yes, since all three sides are equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"The distances from (5,-2) to (6,4) and from (6,4) to (7,-2) both equal sqrt(37), so the triangle is isosceles."}]},{"id":"cg3","title":"Straight Roads and Equal Ground","icon":"🛤️","kind":"lesson","story":"The village council wants to know if three seats in the classroom line up perfectly straight, and later needs a meeting spot exactly as far from two rival villages. Time to put the distance formula to civic use.","teach":[{"title":"Checking Collinearity","body":"To check if three points lie on a straight line, compute all three pairwise distances. If the sum of the two smaller distances equals the largest distance, the points are collinear.","visual":"🛣️"},{"title":"Finding Equidistant Points","body":"To find a point equidistant from two given points, set their squared distances from the unknown point equal to each other and simplify. The resulting equation describes the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two points.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ashima A(3,1), Bharti B(6,4) and Camella C(8,6) have AB = 3*sqrt(2), BC = 2*sqrt(2), AC = 5*sqrt(2). Since AB + BC = AC, what can you conclude?","options":["A, B and C are collinear — they are seated in a line","They form a right triangle","They form an equilateral triangle","Not enough information to decide"],"answer":0,"explain":"When the sum of two distances equals the third, the three points lie on a straight line."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the point on the x-axis equidistant from (2, -5) and (-2, 9). What is its x-coordinate?","answer":-7,"explain":"Setting (x-2)^2+25 = (x+2)^2+81 and solving gives x = -7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find a point on the y-axis equidistant from A(6, 5) and B(-4, 3). What is its y-coordinate?","answer":9,"explain":"Setting (6-0)^2+(5-y)^2 = (-4-0)^2+(3-y)^2 and solving gives y = 9, so the point is (0, 9)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Are the points (1, 5), (2, 3) and (-2, -11) collinear?","options":["No, they do not lie on the same straight line","Yes, they are collinear","Yes, and they form a right angle at the middle point","Cannot be determined without a graph"],"answer":0,"explain":"The slope from (1,5) to (2,3) is -2, but the slope from (2,3) to (-2,-11) is 3.5 — different slopes mean the points are not collinear."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"P(x, y) is equidistant from A(7, 1) and B(3, 5). What relation must x and y satisfy?","options":["x - y = 2","x + y = 2","2x - y = 7","x - 2y = 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Setting AP^2 = BP^2 and simplifying (x-7)^2+(y-1)^2 = (x-3)^2+(y-5)^2 gives x - y = 2."}]},{"id":"cg4","title":"The Relay Tower","icon":"📡","kind":"lesson","story":"A telephone company needs a relay tower placed between two towns so its distance from one town is exactly twice its distance from the other. The section formula is about to become your most powerful surveying tool.","teach":[{"title":"The Section Formula","body":"If P(x, y) divides the segment joining A(x1, y1) and B(x2, y2) internally in the ratio m1 : m2, then x = (m1*x2 + m2*x1)/(m1+m2) and y = (m1*y2 + m2*y1)/(m1+m2). This comes from similar triangles formed by dropping perpendiculars to the x-axis.","visual":"📡"},{"title":"The Midpoint Formula","body":"A midpoint divides a segment in the ratio 1 : 1. Plugging m1 = m2 into the section formula gives the midpoint of A(x1, y1) and B(x2, y2) as ((x1+x2)/2, (y1+y2)/2).","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Parallelogram Diagonals","body":"The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. So if ABCD is a parallelogram, the midpoint of diagonal AC must equal the midpoint of diagonal BD — a handy shortcut for finding an unknown vertex.","visual":"🧩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Tower P divides AB in ratio 1:2, where A is the origin (0,0) and B is (36, 15). What is the x-coordinate of P?","answer":12,"explain":"x = (1*36 + 2*0)/(1+2) = 36/3 = 12."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same tower problem, what is the y-coordinate of P?","answer":5,"explain":"y = (1*15 + 2*0)/(1+2) = 15/3 = 5, so P(12, 5)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the point dividing the segment joining (4, -3) and (8, 5) in the ratio 3:1 internally. What is its x-coordinate?","answer":7,"explain":"x = (3*8 + 1*4)/(3+1) = 28/4 = 7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same problem, what is the y-coordinate of the dividing point?","answer":3,"explain":"y = (3*5 + 1*(-3))/(3+1) = 12/4 = 3, so the point is (7, 3)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A(6,1), B(8,2), C(9,4) and D(p,3) are vertices of a parallelogram taken in order. Find p.","answer":7,"explain":"Midpoint of AC equals midpoint of BD: (15/2, 5/2) = ((8+p)/2, 5/2), so 8+p = 15, giving p = 7."}]},{"id":"cg5","title":"Splitting the Trail","icon":"✂️","kind":"lesson","story":"A treasure map shows a trail that must be cut into equal stretches for waypoint markers, and a mysterious dividing point whose exact ratio you must uncover. Sharpen the section formula — it's time to cut the map down to size.","teach":[{"title":"Finding an Unknown Ratio","body":"If you know a dividing point but not the ratio, let the ratio be k : 1, apply the section formula, and solve for k by comparing coordinates — the same equation works for both x and y as a check.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Points of Trisection","body":"Two points split a segment into three equal parts. The first trisection point divides the segment in the ratio 1:2 from the start, and the second divides it in the ratio 2:1.","visual":"✂️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The point (-4, 6) divides the segment joining A(-6, 10) and B(3, -8). In what ratio?","options":["2 : 7","1 : 2","7 : 2","3 : 5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Setting up -4 = (3k-6)/(k+1) and solving gives k:1 = 2:7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the point dividing the segment joining (-1, 7) and (4, -3) in the ratio 2:3. What is its x-coordinate?","answer":1,"explain":"x = (2*4 + 3*(-1))/(2+3) = (8-3)/5 = 1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same problem, what is the y-coordinate of the dividing point?","answer":3,"explain":"y = (2*(-3) + 3*7)/(2+3) = (-6+21)/5 = 3, so the point is (1, 3)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A(2, -2) and B(-7, 4) are trisected by points P and Q. Find the x-coordinate of P, which divides AB in the ratio 1:2.","answer":-1,"explain":"x = (1*(-7) + 2*2)/(1+2) = (-7+4)/3 = -1, so P is (-1, 0)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the y-coordinate of Q, which divides AB in the ratio 2:1.","answer":2,"explain":"y = (2*4 + 1*(-2))/(2+1) = (8-2)/3 = 2, so Q is (-4, 2)."}]},{"id":"cg6","title":"The Grand Cartographer's Trial","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"The Distortion has scrambled the Atlas's grid, twisting towns, triangles and trails out of place. Only a cartographer who has mastered both the distance formula and the section formula can redraw the map and restore order.","boss":{"name":"The Distortion","taunt":"Your grid means nothing to me — every point I touch drifts from where it belongs!"},"teach":[{"title":"Your Toolkit","body":"Distance formula: PQ = sqrt((x2-x1)^2 + (y2-y1)^2). Section formula: a point dividing A(x1,y1) and B(x2,y2) in ratio m1:m2 is ((m1x2+m2x1)/(m1+m2), (m1y2+m2y1)/(m1+m2)). Use them together to measure, divide, and even find area from coordinates.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Area from a Diagonal Split","body":"For a rhombus, the area equals half the product of its diagonals. Since the diagonal endpoints are just coordinate pairs, the distance formula finds each diagonal's length before you multiply.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Boss recap: Town A is the origin and Town B is (36, 15). What is the distance between them, in km?","answer":39,"explain":"sqrt(36^2+15^2) = sqrt(1521) = 39 km."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Are (5, -2), (6, 4) and (7, -2) the vertices of an isosceles triangle?","options":["Yes, since two sides both equal sqrt(37)","No, all sides are different","No, they are collinear","Yes, it is equilateral"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two of the three side lengths both equal sqrt(37), making the triangle isosceles."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the point on the x-axis equidistant from (2, -5) and (-2, 9). What is its x-coordinate?","answer":-7,"explain":"Solving (x-2)^2+25 = (x+2)^2+81 gives x = -7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A(6,1), B(8,2), C(9,4), D(p,3) form a parallelogram taken in order. Find p.","answer":7,"explain":"The midpoints of diagonals AC and BD must match, giving p = 7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"(1,2), (4,y), (x,6), (3,5) are vertices of a parallelogram taken in order. Find x.","answer":6,"explain":"Midpoint of (1,2)-(x,6) equals midpoint of (4,y)-(3,5): (1+x)/2 = 7/2, so x = 6."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same parallelogram, find y.","answer":3,"explain":"4 = (y+5)/2, so y+5 = 8, giving y = 3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A rhombus has vertices (3,0), (4,5), (-1,4), (-2,-1) taken in order. Its diagonals are 4*sqrt(2) and 6*sqrt(2) units. Using area = half the product of the diagonals, what is the rhombus's area in square units?","answer":24,"explain":"Area = 1/2 * (4*sqrt(2)) * (6*sqrt(2)) = 1/2 * 48 = 24 square units."}]}]},{"id":"trigonometry10","index":7,"name":"Introduction to Trigonometry","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 8","emoji":"📐","palette":{"from":"#818cf8","to":"#312e81"},"lessons":[{"id":"tg1","title":"Shadows Across the River","icon":"🗼","kind":"lesson","story":"A message arrives from the Trigonometry Guild: a mysterious minar has appeared on the horizon, and only right triangles can reveal its true height. Step into the world of trigonometry, where sides and angles hide answers that no ruler alone can reach.","teach":[{"title":"Right Triangles Hide the Answer","body":"Picture looking up at the top of a tall minar, looking down from a balcony at a flower pot across a river, or spotting a hot air balloon in the sky — each scene forms an imaginary right triangle between the observer, the object, and the ground. Trigonometry (from Greek 'tri' = three, 'gon' = sides, 'metron' = measure) studies how the sides and angles of such triangles relate.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Naming the Sides","body":"In right triangle ABC, right-angled at B, look at angle A: BC (the side facing A) is the side opposite to A, AC (facing the right angle) is the hypotenuse, and AB is the side adjacent to A. Switch your attention to angle C instead, and the opposite and adjacent labels swap.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In right triangle ABC (right angle at B), which side is the hypotenuse?","options":["AC, the side facing the right angle","AB, the side adjacent to angle A","BC, the side opposite angle A","There is no hypotenuse in this triangle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The hypotenuse is always the side opposite the right angle, here AC."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For angle A in the same triangle, which side is called the side 'opposite' to A?","options":["BC","AB","AC","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"BC faces angle A directly, so it is the side opposite A."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If you instead focus on angle C, what happens to the 'opposite' and 'adjacent' labels?","options":["They swap — AB becomes opposite and BC becomes adjacent","They stay exactly the same","Both sides become hypotenuses","The triangle stops being right-angled"],"answer":0,"explain":"The position of the sides relative to the chosen angle changes, so opposite and adjacent swap when you move from angle A to angle C."}]},{"id":"tg2","title":"The Six Ratios","icon":"⚖","kind":"lesson","story":"The Guild hands you a strange instrument with six dials — sine, cosine, tangent, and their reciprocals. To calibrate it, you must learn exactly how each ratio is built from the sides of a right triangle.","teach":[{"title":"Sine, Cosine, Tangent","body":"For acute angle A in right triangle ABC: sin A = (side opposite A) / hypotenuse = BC/AC, cos A = (side adjacent A) / hypotenuse = AB/AC, and tan A = (side opposite A) / (side adjacent A) = BC/AB.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Their Reciprocals","body":"cosec A, sec A and cot A are the reciprocals of sin A, cos A and tan A: cosec A = 1/sin A = AC/BC, sec A = 1/cos A = AC/AB, cot A = 1/tan A = AB/BC.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"A Useful Link","body":"Since sin A = BC/AC and cos A = AB/AC, dividing one by the other gives tan A = sin A / cos A. In the same way, cot A = cos A / sin A.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"cosec A is defined as the reciprocal of which ratio?","options":["sin A","cos A","tan A","cot A"],"answer":0,"explain":"cosec A = 1/sin A by definition."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"tan A can be rewritten as:","options":["sin A / cos A","cos A / sin A","1 / sin A","sin A × cos A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since sin A = BC/AC and cos A = AB/AC, dividing them gives tan A = sin A / cos A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a right triangle, tan A = 4/3, so BC = 4k, AB = 3k, AC = 5k. What is sin A as a decimal?","answer":0.8,"explain":"sin A = BC/AC = 4k/5k = 4/5 = 0.8."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"With the same triangle (tan A = 4/3), what is cos A as a decimal?","answer":0.6,"explain":"cos A = AB/AC = 3k/5k = 3/5 = 0.6."}]},{"id":"tg3","title":"One Ratio Unlocks Them All","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Guild archive, an old scroll shows that the ratios never change size with the triangle — only with the angle. Master this, and a single known ratio will let you unlock every other one.","teach":[{"title":"Size Doesn't Matter, Angle Does","body":"If you shrink or stretch a right triangle but keep angle A the same, all its trigonometric ratios stay identical. This is because any two such triangles are similar (AA similarity), so their corresponding sides stay in the same proportion.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Finding the Rest with Pythagoras","body":"If one trigonometric ratio of an acute angle is known, the Pythagoras theorem can be used to find the third side of the triangle, and then every other ratio can be written down. Since the hypotenuse is always the longest side, sin A and cos A can never exceed 1.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If two right triangles both have an acute angle equal to A, how do their trigonometric ratios for A compare?","options":["They are exactly the same, regardless of the triangles' sizes","They are always different","They are equal only if the triangles are congruent","They cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because the triangles are similar (AA), corresponding side ratios — and so the trigonometric ratios — are equal."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In right triangle ABC, right-angled at B, tan A = 1, so AB = BC = k. What is the value of 2 sin A cos A?","answer":1,"explain":"Here AC = √2 k, so sin A = cos A = 1/√2, and 2 sin A cos A = 2 × (1/√2) × (1/√2) = 1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In triangle OPQ, right-angled at P, OP = 7 cm and OQ − PQ = 1 cm. Using OQ² = OP² + PQ², what is sin Q as a decimal?","answer":0.28,"explain":"Solving gives PQ = 24 cm and OQ = 25 cm, so sin Q = OP/OQ = 7/25 = 0.28."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same triangle OPQ, what is cos Q as a decimal?","answer":0.96,"explain":"cos Q = PQ/OQ = 24/25 = 0.96."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If angles B and Q are acute and sin B = sin Q, what must be true?","options":["Angle B = Angle Q","Angle B is always double Angle Q","No conclusion can be drawn","Angle B + Angle Q = 90°"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal sines for acute angles force the triangles to be similar, so the angles themselves must be equal."}]},{"id":"tg4","title":"The 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 Twins","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"Two ancient triangle statues guard the Guild gate — one an isosceles right triangle, one sliced from an equilateral triangle. Their proportions never change, and knowing them by heart lets you answer instantly without measuring anything.","teach":[{"title":"Ratios of 45°","body":"In a right triangle with angles 45°-45°-90°, the two legs are equal (say BC = AB = a), so the hypotenuse AC = a√2. This gives sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2 and tan 45° = 1.","visual":"🔹"},{"title":"Ratios of 30° and 60°","body":"Drop a perpendicular AD from vertex A to side BC of an equilateral triangle ABC with side 2a. This splits it into two right triangles where BD = a and AD = a√3, giving sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2, tan 30° = 1/√3, and sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 60° = √3.","visual":"🔻"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"sin 45° equals:","options":["1/√2","1","√3/2","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the 45-45-90 triangle, BC = AB, so sin 45° = BC/AC = 1/√2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is sin 30° as a decimal?","answer":0.5,"explain":"sin 30° = BD/AB = a/2a = 1/2 = 0.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is cos 60° as a decimal?","answer":0.5,"explain":"cos 60° = 1/2 = 0.5, from the same 30-60-90 triangle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"tan 60° equals:","options":["√3","1/√3","1","2"],"answer":0,"explain":"tan 60° = AD/BD = a√3/a = √3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is tan 45°?","answer":1,"explain":"tan 45° = BC/AB = a/a = 1."}]},{"id":"tg5","title":"The Edges of the Table — 0° and 90°","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"As angle A shrinks toward the ground or rises toward the sky, the Guild's triangle nearly collapses. You must learn what the ratios become at these two extreme edges — and read the full table before the vault seals.","teach":[{"title":"When A Shrinks to 0°","body":"As angle A gets closer and closer to 0°, side BC shrinks toward 0 while AC becomes almost equal to AB. This gives sin 0° = 0 and cos 0° = 1, so tan 0° = 0, while cot 0° and cosec 0° are not defined.","visual":"🌅"},{"title":"When A Grows to 90°","body":"As angle A grows toward 90°, side AB shrinks toward 0 while AC becomes almost equal to BC. This gives sin 90° = 1 and cos 90° = 0, so cot 90° = 0, while tan 90° and sec 90° are not defined.","visual":"🌇"},{"title":"The Full Table","body":"As A increases from 0° to 90°, sin A steadily increases from 0 to 1, while cos A steadily decreases from 1 to 0. The values for 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90° form Table 8.1, the reference every trigonometry problem is built on.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"sin 0° equals:","options":["0","1","1/2","Not defined"],"answer":0,"explain":"As angle A shrinks to 0°, side BC shrinks to 0, so sin A = BC/AC → 0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"cos 90° equals:","options":["0","1","1/2","Not defined"],"answer":0,"explain":"As angle A grows to 90°, side AB shrinks to 0, so cos A = AB/AC → 0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which ratio is NOT defined at A = 90°?","options":["tan A","sin A","cos A","cot A"],"answer":0,"explain":"tan 90° = sin 90°/cos 90° = 1/0, which is not defined."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As angle A increases from 0° to 90°, sin A and cos A:","options":["sin A increases from 0 to 1, cos A decreases from 1 to 0","Both increase together","Both decrease together","sin A decreases, cos A increases"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 8.1 shows sin A rising steadily while cos A falls steadily across this range."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From Table 8.1, what is sec 60°?","answer":2,"explain":"sec 60° = 1/cos 60° = 1/(1/2) = 2."}]},{"id":"tg6","title":"Solving the Guild's Triangles","icon":"🗺","kind":"lesson","story":"With the table memorized, the Guild sends you real puzzles: a wall of known height, a beam of known length, a pair of angles whispered in riddles. Time to put the specific-angle values to work.","teach":[{"title":"Finding a Missing Side","body":"In triangle ABC, right-angled at B, with AB = 5 cm and angle C = 30°: since AB/BC = tan C, BC = 5/tan 30° = 5√3 cm; and since sin 30° = AB/AC, AC = AB/sin 30° = 5/(1/2) = 10 cm.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Finding a Missing Angle","body":"In triangle PQR, right-angled at Q, with PQ = 3 cm and PR = 6 cm: sin R = PQ/PR = 3/6 = 1/2, so angle R = 30°, and therefore angle QPR = 60°.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Solving for Two Angles at Once","body":"If sin(A − B) = 1/2 and cos(A + B) = 1/2, then A − B = 30° and A + B = 60°. Solving these two equations together gives A = 45° and B = 15°.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In triangle ABC (right angle at B), AB = 5 cm and angle C = 30°. What is AC in cm?","answer":10,"explain":"sin 30° = AB/AC = 1/2, so AC = 5 ÷ (1/2) = 10 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In triangle PQR (right angle at Q), PQ = 3 cm and PR = 6 cm. What is angle R in degrees?","answer":30,"explain":"sin R = PQ/PR = 3/6 = 1/2, so angle R = 30°."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same triangle, what is angle QPR in degrees?","answer":60,"explain":"The angles of a right triangle sum to 180°, so angle QPR = 180° − 90° − 30° = 60°."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If sin(A − B) = 1/2 and cos(A + B) = 1/2, with A > B and 0° < A + B ≤ 90°, what is angle A in degrees?","answer":45,"explain":"A − B = 30° and A + B = 60°; adding these equations gives 2A = 90°, so A = 45°."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same pair of equations, what is angle B in degrees?","answer":15,"explain":"Subtracting A − B = 30° from A + B = 60° gives 2B = 30°, so B = 15°."}]},{"id":"tg7","title":"The Three Identities","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"The final vault door is carved with three equations that hold true for every acute angle, without exception. Prove you understand the Pythagorean identity and its two cousins to unlock the boss chamber.","teach":[{"title":"The Pythagorean Identity","body":"Dividing AB² + BC² = AC² by AC² gives (AB/AC)² + (BC/AC)² = 1, i.e. cos²A + sin²A = 1. This holds for every angle A with 0° ≤ A ≤ 90°.","visual":"🟰"},{"title":"Two More Identities","body":"Dividing AB² + BC² = AC² by AB² instead gives 1 + tan²A = sec²A, true for 0° ≤ A < 90° since tan A and sec A aren't defined at 90°. Dividing by BC² gives cot²A + 1 = cosec²A, true for 0° < A ≤ 90° since cot A and cosec A aren't defined at 0°.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Using an Identity","body":"Since cos²A + sin²A = 1, cos A can always be written in terms of sin A as cos A = √(1 − sin²A) — exactly how Example 9 of the chapter expresses cos A, tan A and sec A purely using sin A.","visual":"🔓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"sin²A + cos²A equals:","options":["1","0","2","tan²A"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the Pythagorean identity, derived by dividing AB² + BC² = AC² by AC²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"1 + tan²A equals:","options":["sec²A","cosec²A","cos²A","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dividing AB² + BC² = AC² by AB² gives 1 + tan²A = sec²A."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"cot²A + 1 equals:","options":["cosec²A","sec²A","sin²A","tan²A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dividing AB² + BC² = AC² by BC² gives cot²A + 1 = cosec²A."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If sin A = 0.6, what is cos A (using cos²A = 1 − sin²A)?","answer":0.8,"explain":"cos²A = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, so cos A = 0.8 — the familiar 3-4-5 triangle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The identity 1 + tan²A = sec²A fails to hold only at:","options":["A = 90°, where tan A and sec A are undefined","A = 0°, where tan A and sec A are undefined","A = 45°","It holds at every angle, no exceptions"],"answer":0,"explain":"tan A and sec A are not defined at 90°, so the identity is restricted to 0° ≤ A < 90°."}]},{"id":"tg8","title":"Boss: Trikon, Warden of the Angle Vault","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"Trikon towers before you, all six ratios spinning like blades around it. Every worked example, every table value, every identity you've learned must fire in an instant — miss one, and the vault stays sealed forever.","teach":[{"title":"Everything You've Learned","body":"Six ratios (sin, cos, tan, cosec, sec, cot), their values at 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°, and three identities (sin²A + cos²A = 1, 1 + tan²A = sec²A, cot²A + 1 = cosec²A) — Trikon falls only to a player who can combine all of it.","visual":"⚔"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For angle A in right triangle ABC (right-angled at B), sin A is defined as:","options":["side opposite A ÷ hypotenuse","side adjacent A ÷ hypotenuse","side opposite A ÷ side adjacent A","hypotenuse ÷ side opposite A"],"answer":0,"explain":"sin A = BC/AC, the opposite side over the hypotenuse."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If tan A = 4/3 (so BC = 4k, AB = 3k, AC = 5k), what is cot A as a decimal?","answer":0.75,"explain":"cot A = AB/BC = 3k/4k = 3/4 = 0.75."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"sin 60° equals:","options":["√3/2","1/2","1/√2","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"From the 30-60-90 triangle, sin 60° = AD/AB = √3/2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is cos²45° + sin²45°?","answer":1,"explain":"By the Pythagorean identity, cos²A + sin²A = 1 for every angle, including 45°."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From Table 8.1, what is cosec 90°?","answer":1,"explain":"cosec 90° = 1/sin 90° = 1/1 = 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which ratio is NOT defined at A = 0°?","options":["cot A","sin A","cos A","tan A"],"answer":0,"explain":"cot A = cos A/sin A, and sin 0° = 0, so cot 0° is not defined."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In triangle OPQ (right angle at P), OP = 7 cm and OQ − PQ = 1 cm. What is sin Q as a decimal?","answer":0.28,"explain":"Solving gives PQ = 24 cm, OQ = 25 cm, so sin Q = OP/OQ = 7/25 = 0.28."}],"boss":{"name":"Trikon, Warden of the Angle Vault","taunt":"Feed me one ratio, and I shall demand all six — miss a single decimal, and you start again!"}}]},{"id":"trigapplications10","index":8,"name":"Applications of Trigonometry","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 9","emoji":"🗼","palette":{"from":"#f87171","to":"#7f1d1d"},"lessons":[{"id":"ta1","title":"Line of Sight","icon":"👁️","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the old Ridgewood Watchtower district, where surveyors measure towers, minars, and balconies without ever climbing them. An old surveyor hands you a simple tool: your own eyes, and a horizontal line to measure against.","teach":[{"title":"Line of Sight","body":"The line of sight is the line drawn from the eye of an observer to the point on the object being viewed. Every height-and-distance problem starts by drawing this line.","visual":"👁️"},{"title":"Angle of Elevation","body":"When the object is above the horizontal level, you raise your head to look at it. The angle formed between your line of sight and the horizontal is called the angle of elevation.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Angle of Depression","body":"When the object is below the horizontal level, like a girl on a balcony looking down at a flower pot on a temple stair, you lower your head. The angle formed between the line of sight and the horizontal is called the angle of depression.","visual":"🔽"},{"title":"Solving with Right Triangles","body":"Heights and distances are found by drawing a right triangle from the line of sight, the horizontal, and the vertical object, then picking the trig ratio that connects the sides you know to the side you want.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A girl sitting on a balcony looks down at a flower pot on a temple stair. What is the angle between her line of sight and the horizontal called?","options":["Angle of depression","Angle of elevation","Angle of incidence","Angle of reflection"],"answer":0,"explain":"Looking down at something below the horizontal level gives the angle of depression."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A student looks up at the top of a minar. The angle between the line of sight and the horizontal is called the...","options":["angle of elevation","angle of depression","angle of refraction","tangent angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Raising your head to view something above the horizontal gives the angle of elevation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The line of sight is drawn from...","options":["the observer's eye to the point on the object viewed","the object to the ground","the horizontal line to the vertical line","the foot of the object to the observer's feet"],"answer":0,"explain":"By definition, the line of sight runs from the observer's eye to the point being viewed."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A tower stands vertically on the ground. From a point 15 m from its foot, the angle of elevation of the top is 60°. Using tan 60° = √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the tower in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":25.98,"explain":"tan 60° = height/15, so height = 15 × 1.732 = 25.98 m."}]},{"id":"ta2","title":"Straight-Up: Towers and Poles","icon":"🗼","kind":"lesson","story":"The Watchtower Guild hands you an inclinometer and a measuring tape. Your job: find the heights of towers, poles, and ropes stretched taut across the yard, using only an angle and a distance.","teach":[{"title":"Setting Up the Triangle","body":"For a tower AB with a point C on the ground, the angle ACB is the angle of elevation. Since BC (the ground distance) and AB (the height) are opposite and adjacent to angle C, tan C = AB/BC lets you solve for the unknown height.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"When the Ground Distance is Known","body":"If you know the distance from the foot of the tower and the angle of elevation, height = distance × tan(angle). A tower 15 m away with a 60° angle of elevation has height 15 × tan 60° = 15 × 1.732 ≈ 25.98 m.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"When the Rope or Ladder Length is Known","body":"If instead you know the length of a taut rope or ladder (the hypotenuse) and the angle it makes with the ground, height = length × sin(angle), since sine relates the opposite side to the hypotenuse.","visual":"🩹"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A tower stands 15 m from an observation point, with an angle of elevation of 60° to its top. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the tower in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":25.98,"explain":"tan 60° = height/15, so height = 15 × 1.732 = 25.98 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The angle of elevation of the top of a tower from a point 30 m from its foot is 30°. Using tan 30° = 1/√3 ≈ 0.577, find the height of the tower in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":17.32,"explain":"height = 30 × tan 30° = 30 × 0.577 ≈ 17.32 m (this is 10√3 m)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A circus artist climbs a 20 m long rope tied tightly from the top of a vertical pole to the ground, and the rope makes a 30° angle with the ground. Find the height of the pole in metres.","answer":10,"explain":"height = rope length × sin 30° = 20 × 0.5 = 10 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You know the length of a taut rope (the hypotenuse) and the angle it makes with the ground, and you want the height it reaches. Which trig ratio should you use?","options":["sine","cosine","tangent","cotangent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sine relates the side opposite the angle (height) to the hypotenuse (rope length)."}]},{"id":"ta3","title":"Ladders and Slides","icon":"🪜","kind":"lesson","story":"An electrician needs a ladder to reach a fault on a pole, and a park contractor needs to size two children's slides. Both jobs come down to the same right triangle: a sloped hypotenuse meeting the ground at a known angle.","teach":[{"title":"The Ladder Triangle","body":"A ladder leaning against a pole forms the hypotenuse of a right triangle. If BD is the vertical height it must reach and the ladder makes an angle with the horizontal, then sin(angle) = BD/ladder length, so ladder length = BD ÷ sin(angle).","visual":"🪜"},{"title":"Finding the Foot of the Ladder","body":"To find how far the base of the ladder sits from the pole, use cot(angle) = distance/height, i.e. distance = height × cot(angle) (or equivalently, distance = height ÷ tan(angle)).","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Slides Work the Same Way","body":"A slide's sloped surface is just a ladder in disguise: its length is the hypotenuse, its height above the ground is the opposite side, and its angle with the ground gives length = height ÷ sin(angle).","visual":"🛝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electrician needs to reach a point 3.7 m up a pole. Her ladder is inclined at 60° to the horizontal. Using sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.866, find the length of the ladder in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":4.27,"explain":"Ladder length = 3.7 ÷ 0.866 ≈ 4.27 m (the source gives 4.28 m using a slightly different rounding of √3)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same ladder (reaching 3.7 m up at 60° to the horizontal), how far from the foot of the pole should the ladder's base be placed, in metres? Use cot 60° = 1/√3 ≈ 0.577 (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":2.14,"explain":"Distance = 3.7 × cot 60° = 3.7 × 0.577 ≈ 2.14 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A park slide for young children has its top at a height of 1.5 m and is inclined at 30° to the ground. Find the length of the slide in metres.","answer":3,"explain":"Length = 1.5 ÷ sin 30° = 1.5 ÷ 0.5 = 3 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A steeper slide for older children has its top at a height of 3 m and is inclined at 60° to the ground. Using sin 60° ≈ 0.866, find the length of the slide in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":3.46,"explain":"Length = 3 ÷ sin 60° = 3 ÷ 0.866 ≈ 3.46 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ladder's length is the hypotenuse of a right triangle, and the height it reaches on a wall is the side opposite the angle it makes with the ground. Which ratio connects them directly?","options":["sine","cosine","tangent","cotangent"],"answer":0,"explain":"sine = opposite/hypotenuse = height/ladder length."}]},{"id":"ta4","title":"Don't Forget the Observer","icon":"🧍","kind":"lesson","story":"A short chimney-watcher, a boy walking toward a building, and a girl tracking a drifting balloon all share one trap: the observer's own height changes the answer. Fall for it and your tower will come out too short.","teach":[{"title":"Add the Eye Level Back","body":"When an observer's eyes are above the ground, the angle of elevation is measured from eye level, not ground level. So total height = (height found using the right triangle) + (observer's height). A 1.5 m tall observer 28.5 m from a chimney, sighting its top at 45°, gets an extra 28.5 m of triangle height, plus their own 1.5 m, for a chimney height of 30 m.","visual":"🧍"},{"title":"Two Angles, One Walk","body":"When an observer walks toward an object and the angle of elevation changes from one value to another, find the horizontal distance at each angle separately using tan, then subtract the two distances to get the distance walked.","visual":"🚶"},{"title":"A Drifting Object Works the Same Way","body":"For an object moving horizontally at constant height (like a kite or balloon), the same subtraction trick applies: find the horizontal distance to the object at each angle of elevation, then subtract to get the horizontal distance travelled.","visual":"🎈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An observer 1.5 m tall stands 28.5 m from a chimney. The angle of elevation of the top of the chimney from her eyes is 45°. Find the height of the chimney in metres.","answer":30,"explain":"tan 45° = 1, so the height above eye level equals 28.5 m; adding her 1.5 m eye-level height gives 30 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1.5 m tall boy stands near a 30 m building (28.5 m above his eyes). As he walks towards it, the angle of elevation to the top increases from 30° to 60°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find how far he walked, in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":32.91,"explain":"Distance at 30° is 28.5×1.732≈49.36 m; distance at 60° is 28.5÷1.732≈16.45 m. The difference is about 32.91 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1.2 m tall girl spots a balloon flying at a height of 88.2 m (that's 87 m above her eyes). The angle of elevation is first 60°, then reduces to 30° as the balloon drifts. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the distance travelled by the balloon in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":100.46,"explain":"Height above eye level is 88.2 − 1.2 = 87 m. Distance at 60° ≈ 50.23 m; distance at 30° ≈ 150.68 m. The difference is about 100.46 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why must you add the observer's own height back on at the end of a problem like the chimney example?","options":["Because the angle of elevation is measured from the observer's eye level, not from the ground","Because trigonometric ratios only work for whole numbers","Because the chimney is tilted","Because the horizontal distance changes with height"],"answer":0,"explain":"The right triangle only accounts for the height above the observer's eyes, so the eye-level height must be added back."}]},{"id":"ta5","title":"Two Triangles, One Puzzle","icon":"🚩","kind":"lesson","story":"A flagstaff rises from a rooftop, a tower casts two different shadows at two different times of day, and one building overlooks another. Each puzzle hides two right triangles sharing a common side — solve one to unlock the other.","teach":[{"title":"Flagstaff on a Building","body":"When a flagstaff sits on a building of known height, first use the angle of elevation to the building's top to find the horizontal distance (a right triangle with a known side). Then use the angle of elevation to the flagstaff's top, with that same distance, to find the total height, and subtract the building's height to get the flagstaff's length.","visual":"🚩"},{"title":"Two Shadows, One Height","body":"When a shadow's length is given at two different sun angles, write tan(angle) = height/shadow-length for each case. This gives two equations in two unknowns (height and shadow length), which can be solved together by substitution.","visual":"🌑"},{"title":"Alternate Angles Between Buildings","body":"When looking from the top of one building down at another, the horizontal line of sight is parallel to the ground, so the angle of depression equals the angle of elevation measured from the other building (alternate angles). This lets you set up right triangles from both buildings' perspectives.","visual":"🏙️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From a point P, the angle of elevation of the top of a 10 m building is 30°. Using tan 30° = 1/√3 ≈ 0.577, find the distance of the building from P, in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":17.32,"explain":"distance = 10 ÷ tan 30° = 10 ÷ 0.577 ≈ 17.32 m (this is 10√3 m)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A flag is hoisted on top of that same 10 m building. From the same point P (17.32 m away), the angle of elevation of the top of the flagstaff is 45°. Find the length of the flagstaff in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":7.32,"explain":"tan 45° = 1, so total height = 17.32 m; subtracting the 10 m building gives a flagstaff length of about 7.32 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A tower's shadow is 40 m longer when the sun's altitude is 30° than when it is 60°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the tower in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":34.64,"explain":"Solving tan 60° = h/x and tan 30° = h/(x+40) together gives x = 20 m and h = 20√3 ≈ 34.64 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From the top of a multi-storeyed building, the angles of depression of the top and bottom of a nearby 8 m tall building are 30° and 45°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the multi-storeyed building in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":18.93,"explain":"Using alternate angles and solving the two right triangles together gives a height of 4√3 + 12 ≈ 18.93 m."}]},{"id":"ta6","title":"Bridges, Canals, and Lighthouses","icon":"🌉","kind":"lesson","story":"From a bridge above a river, a lighthouse above the sea, and a bank beside a canal, every sightline points downward. Master the angle of depression here and no waterway will keep its width secret from you.","teach":[{"title":"Splitting the Width in Two","body":"When the angles of depression to two points on opposite sides of a bridge (or river banks) are different, split the total width into two right triangles sharing the bridge's height, solve each separately with tan, then add the two distances together.","visual":"🌉"},{"title":"Same Idea, Opposite Direction","body":"Angles of depression to two ships (or two points) on the same side of an observer, one behind the other, work the same way but with subtraction: find each distance separately, then subtract the nearer from the farther to get the distance between them.","visual":"🚢"},{"title":"Eliminating the Unknown Distance","body":"When a tower's height and the distance to it are both unknown, but you have two angles of elevation from two different points a known distance apart, write two tan equations and solve them together — just like the two-shadow problem — to find both unknowns.","visual":"📡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From a bridge 3 m above the banks of a river, the angles of depression of the two banks on opposite sides are 30° and 45°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the width of the river in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":8.2,"explain":"One bank is 3×√3 ≈ 5.20 m away, the other is 3 m away (tan 45° = 1); the total width is about 8.20 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From the top of a 75 m lighthouse, the angles of depression of two ships in line on the same side are 30° and 45°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the distance between the ships in metres (round to 1 decimal place).","answer":54.9,"explain":"The nearer ship is 75 m away (tan 45° = 1); the farther ship is 75×√3 ≈ 129.9 m away. The difference is about 54.9 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A TV tower stands on one bank of a canal. From a point directly opposite on the other bank, the angle of elevation of the top is 60°. From a point 20 m further back on the same line, the angle of elevation is 30°. Find the width of the canal in metres.","answer":10,"explain":"Solving tan 60° = h/d and tan 30° = h/(d+20) together gives d = 10 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same TV tower and canal, using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the tower in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":17.32,"explain":"h = d × tan 60° = 10 × 1.732 = 17.32 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A man at the top of a tower watches a car approach along a straight highway. The angle of depression rises from 30° to 60° over 6 seconds. What does that tell you about the car's distance from the tower's foot?","options":["It has decreased over those 6 seconds","It has increased over those 6 seconds","It has stayed exactly the same","It cannot be determined from the angles alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"A larger angle of depression for the same height means a shorter horizontal distance, so the car has moved closer."}]},{"id":"ta7","title":"The Surveyor Sphinx","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the top of the Watchtower Guild's tallest spire waits the Surveyor Sphinx, guardian of every unmeasured height in the land. It will not let you pass until you've proven you can measure anything — towers, statues, kites, and broken trees alike — using nothing but angles and a tape measure.","boss":{"name":"The Surveyor Sphinx","taunt":"Measure me without a ruler... if you dare."},"teach":[{"title":"Review: Elevation and Depression","body":"Angle of elevation is measured looking up from the horizontal; angle of depression is measured looking down from the horizontal. Both are always measured against a horizontal line at the observer's eye.","visual":"👁️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You are standing on the ground looking up at the top of a tower. The angle between your line of sight and the horizontal is called the...","options":["angle of elevation","angle of depression","angle of incidence","vertical angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Looking upward from the horizontal gives the angle of elevation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A kite flies at a height of 60 m, and its string (with no slack) makes a 60° angle with the ground. Using sin 60° ≈ 0.866, find the length of the string in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":69.28,"explain":"String length = 60 ÷ sin 60° = 60 ÷ 0.866 ≈ 69.28 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A tree breaks in a storm; the broken top touches the ground 8 m from the foot of the tree, making a 30° angle with the ground. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the original height of the tree in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":13.86,"explain":"The standing stump is 8×tan30° ≈ 4.62 m and the broken part is 8÷cos30° ≈ 9.24 m; together, 8√3 ≈ 13.86 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The angle of elevation of the top of a building from the foot of a 50 m tower is 30°, and the angle of elevation of the top of the tower from the foot of the building is 60°. Find the height of the building in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":16.67,"explain":"Both triangles share the same horizontal distance d, where 50 = d×tan60° and building height = d×tan30°, giving height = 50/3 ≈ 16.67 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1.6 m tall statue stands on a pedestal. From a point on the ground, the angle of elevation of the top of the statue is 60° and of the top of the pedestal is 45°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the pedestal in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":2.19,"explain":"Solving tan45° = h/d and tan60° = (h+1.6)/d together gives h = 0.8(√3+1) ≈ 2.19 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From the top of a 20 m building, the angle of elevation of the top of a cable tower on an adjacent building is 60°, and the angle of depression of its foot is 45°. Using √3 ≈ 1.732, find the height of the cable tower in metres (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":14.64,"explain":"The horizontal distance is 20 m (from the 45° depression); the tower's extra height above the 20 m mark is 20×(√3−1) ≈ 14.64 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do 'two right triangle' problems like the flagstaff, the two-building, or the pedestal-and-statue problems all need two separate trigonometric equations?","options":["Because there are two unknowns, such as an unknown height and an unknown distance, that must be solved together","Because every trigonometry problem requires exactly two equations","Because one of the triangles is always equilateral","Because the angle of elevation must always be checked twice"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two unknowns require two independent equations, one from each right triangle, solved simultaneously."}]}]},{"id":"circles10","index":9,"name":"Circles","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 10","emoji":"⭕","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"ci1","title":"One Touch, No More","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"You find an old astrolabe etched with a circle and stray lines. The map says only a line that 'kisses' the circle at a single point will unlock the next passage — figure out which of your lines actually qualifies.","teach":[{"title":"Three ways a line meets a circle","body":"A line and a circle in a plane can have no common point (non-intersecting line), two common points (a secant), or exactly one common point (a tangent). There is no other possibility.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"The point of contact","body":"The single common point of a tangent and the circle is called the point of contact, and the tangent is said to touch the circle there. The word 'tangent' comes from the Latin 'tangere', meaning 'to touch'.","visual":"✨"},{"title":"Tangent as a limiting secant","body":"A tangent is really a secant in disguise: as the two points where a secant cuts the circle move closer together, they eventually merge into one point, and the secant becomes a tangent.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A line that touches a circle at exactly one point is called a","options":["tangent","secant","chord","diameter"],"answer":0,"explain":"A tangent meets the circle at exactly one point, the point of contact."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A line that cuts a circle at two points is called a","options":["secant","tangent","radius","normal"],"answer":0,"explain":"A secant intersects the circle in two points."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The common point of a tangent and the circle is called the","options":["point of contact","centre","vertex","focus"],"answer":0,"explain":"This shared point is named the point of contact."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"With how many points does a tangent share contact with a circle?","answer":1,"explain":"By definition a tangent touches the circle at exactly one point."}]},{"id":"ci2","title":"The Perpendicular Guard","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"A wheel spins on its axle, and every spoke stands guard at right angles to the ground it rolls on. The gatekeeper of this level demands you prove why the tangent line and the radius always meet like sworn enemies — at 90°.","teach":[{"title":"Theorem 10.1","body":"The tangent at any point of a circle is perpendicular to the radius through the point of contact. This is because every other point on the tangent line lies outside the circle, so OP (the radius) is the shortest distance from centre O to the line — and the shortest distance is always perpendicular.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Only one tangent at a point","body":"Since the tangent must be perpendicular to the radius at that point, there can be one and only one tangent to a circle at any given point on it.","visual":"1️⃣"},{"title":"The 'normal'","body":"The line containing the radius through the point of contact is sometimes also called the normal to the circle at that point.","visual":"🧭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The tangent to a circle at a point is always ____ the radius through the point of contact.","options":["perpendicular to","parallel to","equal in length to","at 45° to"],"answer":0,"explain":"Theorem 10.1: tangent ⊥ radius through the point of contact."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A tangent PQ touches a circle of radius 5 cm at P. The tangent meets a line through centre O at point Q, with OQ = 12 cm. What is the length of PQ?","options":["√119 cm","13 cm","12 cm","8.5 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since OP ⊥ PQ, by Pythagoras PQ² = OQ² − OP² = 144 − 25 = 119, so PQ = √119 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At most how many tangents can be drawn to a circle at one particular point on it?","answer":1,"explain":"There is one and only one tangent at any point of a circle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The line containing the radius through the point of contact is also called the ____ to the circle at that point.","options":["normal","secant","chord","axis"],"answer":0,"explain":"This radius line is sometimes called the normal."}]},{"id":"ci3","title":"Inside, On, or Outside?","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Three explorers stand at three different spots near a circular moat: one inside the walls, one right on the wall, one outside it. Only some of them can fire a single 'tangent arrow' — and only one of them can fire two. Find out who.","teach":[{"title":"Case 1: point inside the circle","body":"Every line drawn through a point lying inside a circle cuts the circle in two points, so no tangent can be drawn from a point inside the circle.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"Case 2: point on the circle","body":"There is one and only one tangent to a circle through a point lying on the circle.","visual":"1️⃣"},{"title":"Case 3: point outside the circle","body":"Exactly two tangents can be drawn to a circle from a point lying outside it. The two points where these tangents touch the circle are their points of contact.","visual":"✌️"},{"title":"Length of a tangent","body":"The length of the segment of a tangent from an external point to its point of contact with the circle is called the length of the tangent from that point.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many tangents can be drawn to a circle from a point lying inside it?","options":["0","1","2","infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every line through an interior point is a secant, never a tangent."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many tangents can be drawn to a circle from a point lying on the circle?","options":["1","0","2","3"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exactly one tangent exists at a point on the circle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many tangents can be drawn to a circle from a point lying outside it?","options":["2","0","1","4"],"answer":0,"explain":"From an external point exactly two tangents can be drawn."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The length of the tangent from a point A at distance 5 cm from the centre of a circle of radius 3 cm is how many cm?","answer":4,"explain":"Tangent length² = 5² − 3² = 25 − 9 = 16, so the tangent length is 4 cm (a 3-4-5 right triangle)."}]},{"id":"ci4","title":"Twin Tangents","icon":"🪞","kind":"lesson","story":"From outside the moat you fire two tangent arrows at the circular wall. The old scroll insists both arrows will always land with exactly the same length, and that the line back to you always splits their angle evenly. Test the legend.","teach":[{"title":"Theorem 10.2","body":"The lengths of the two tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are equal. This follows because the two right triangles formed with the centre (right-angled at the points of contact, by Theorem 10.1) share a common hypotenuse and equal radii, so they are congruent by RHS.","visual":"🪞"},{"title":"The angle bisector","body":"As a bonus from that same congruence, the line joining the external point to the centre bisects the angle between the two tangents — the centre always lies on this angle bisector.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Worked example: concentric circles","body":"For two concentric circles with centre O, if a chord AB of the larger circle touches the smaller circle at P, then OP ⊥ AB (tangent ⊥ radius), and since a perpendicular from the centre bisects a chord, AP = BP — the chord is bisected at the point of contact.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"From an external point, the two tangent segments drawn to a circle are","options":["equal in length","never equal","in the ratio 2:1","always unequal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Theorem 10.2: tangents from an external point are equal in length."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From a point Q, the length of the tangent to a circle is 24 cm and the distance of Q from the centre is 25 cm. What is the radius of the circle (in cm)?","answer":7,"explain":"radius² = 25² − 24² = 625 − 576 = 49, so radius = 7 cm (a 7-24-25 right triangle)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two concentric circles have radii 5 cm and 3 cm. Find the length of the chord of the larger circle that touches the smaller circle (in cm).","answer":8,"explain":"Half-chord = √(5² − 3²) = √16 = 4 cm, so the full chord = 2 × 4 = 8 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If PQ and PR are the two tangents from external point P to a circle with centre O, then OP is the","options":["angle bisector of ∠QPR","perpendicular bisector of QR only","altitude of triangle PQR","median from Q"],"answer":0,"explain":"By the congruence in Theorem 10.2, OP bisects the angle between the two tangents."}]},{"id":"ci5","title":"Angles Around the Arrowhead","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"Two tangent arrows from your bow meet the wall and trace out a kite of angles with the centre. Master archers can read the whole shape from a single angle — can you?","teach":[{"title":"∠PTQ and ∠OPQ","body":"If TP and TQ are tangents from an external point T with ∠PTQ = θ, then triangle TPQ is isosceles (TP = TQ), and since ∠OPT = 90°, working through the angles gives ∠PTQ = 2∠OPQ.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Worked example: chord and tangents","body":"For a chord PQ of length 8 cm in a circle of radius 5 cm, if tangents at P and Q meet at T, then OT bisects PQ perpendicularly, giving half-chord PR = 4 cm and OR = √(5² − 4²) = 3 cm. Using similar right triangles TRP and PRO, TP/OP = RP/RO, so TP = 20/3 cm.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"A circumscribing quadrilateral","body":"If a quadrilateral ABCD has all four sides touching a circle, the equal tangent lengths from each vertex mean AB + CD = AD + BC.","visual":"🟦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"TP and TQ are tangents to a circle with centre O, and ∠POQ = 110°. What is ∠PTQ?","options":["70°","110°","55°","90°"],"answer":0,"explain":"In quadrilateral OPTQ, ∠OPT = ∠OQT = 90°, so ∠PTQ = 360° − 90° − 90° − 110° = 70°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Tangents PA and PB from point P are inclined to each other at 80°. What is ∠POA?","options":["50°","40°","80°","100°"],"answer":0,"explain":"OP bisects ∠APB so ∠APO = 40°; in right triangle OAP, ∠POA = 90° − 40° = 50°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"PQ is a chord of length 8 cm in a circle of radius 5 cm. Tangents at P and Q meet at point T. What is the length TP?","options":["20/3 cm","4 cm","3 cm","5 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Using similar triangles TRP and PRO: TP/OP = RP/RO, i.e. TP/5 = 4/3, so TP = 20/3 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A quadrilateral ABCD is drawn so that all its sides touch a circle. Which relation always holds?","options":["AB + CD = AD + BC","AB × CD = AD × BC","AB − CD = AD − BC","AB + BC = CD + AD"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal tangent lengths from each vertex make the sum of opposite sides equal."}]},{"id":"ci6","title":"The Untouchable Sentinel","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the chapter stands Tangentor, a sentinel who can only ever be struck at a single point. Prove every law of tangents to break its guard for good.","boss":{"name":"Tangentor, the Untouchable Sentinel","taunt":"Touch me at exactly one point... if you dare!"},"teach":[{"title":"Chapter recap","body":"A tangent meets a circle at exactly one point and is perpendicular to the radius at that point (Theorem 10.1). From an external point exactly two tangents can be drawn, and their lengths are always equal (Theorem 10.2); from a point inside the circle, none can be drawn, and from a point on the circle, exactly one.","visual":"⭕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The common point of a tangent to a circle and the circle is called the","options":["point of contact","vertex","focus","midpoint"],"answer":0,"explain":"This shared point is the point of contact."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The length of the tangent from a point at distance 13 cm from the centre of a circle of radius 5 cm is how many cm?","answer":12,"explain":"tangent² = 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144, so tangent length = 12 cm (a 5-12-13 right triangle)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The tangents drawn at the two ends of a diameter of a circle are always","options":["parallel to each other","perpendicular to each other","equal in length only","intersecting at the centre"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both tangents are perpendicular to the same diameter, so they are parallel to each other."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A parallelogram that circumscribes a circle (all four sides touch it) must be a","options":["rhombus","rectangle","square","trapezium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal tangent lengths force all four sides of the parallelogram to be equal, making it a rhombus."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two concentric circles have radii 13 cm and 5 cm. Find the length of the chord of the larger circle that touches the smaller circle (in cm).","answer":24,"explain":"Half-chord = √(13² − 5²) = √144 = 12 cm, so the full chord = 2 × 12 = 24 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a point P outside a circle, the two tangent segments from P to the circle are always","options":["equal in length","different in length","perpendicular to each other","parallel to each other"],"answer":0,"explain":"Theorem 10.2: tangents from the same external point are always equal in length."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From a point 17 cm from the centre of a circle of radius 8 cm, what is the length of the tangent (in cm)?","answer":15,"explain":"tangent² = 17² − 8² = 289 − 64 = 225, so tangent length = 15 cm (an 8-15-17 right triangle)."}]}]},{"id":"areascircles10","index":10,"name":"Areas Related to Circles","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 11","emoji":"🎯","palette":{"from":"#fbbf24","to":"#78350f"},"lessons":[{"id":"ac1","title":"The Sundial's Old Secrets","icon":"⭕","kind":"lesson","story":"The Sundial Sentinel guards the gate to the Circle Kingdom and will not lift the bar until you recite the rim and the space inside a circle. Dust off the old formulas before you march further in.","teach":[{"title":"Circumference of a circle","body":"The distance all the way around a circle of radius r is its circumference, C = 2πr. This is the perimeter of the circular region.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Area of a circle","body":"The flat space enclosed by a circle of radius r is its area, A = πr². You can also picture the whole disc as one giant sector spanning the full 360° at the centre.","visual":"🔵"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the circumference of a circle of radius r?","options":["2πr","πr²","πr","4r²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Circumference is the perimeter of a circle, given by C = 2πr."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the circumference (in cm) of a circle of radius 7 cm. (Use π = 22/7)","answer":44,"explain":"C = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the area (in cm²) of a circle of radius 7 cm. (Use π = 22/7)","answer":154,"explain":"A = πr² = 22/7 × 7² = 154 cm²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A full circular region can itself be thought of as a sector spanning what angle at the centre?","options":["360°","180°","90°","270°"],"answer":0,"explain":"The whole disc is the sector formed when the angle at the centre is the complete 360°."}]},{"id":"ac2","title":"Wedges and Walls","icon":"🍕","kind":"lesson","story":"Inside the Circle Kingdom, two straight roads run from the centre out to the rim, carving off a wedge called a sector, while a lone wall cuts a straight chord across, slicing off a segment. The Cartographer will not hand you her map until you can name every piece.","teach":[{"title":"Sector of a circle","body":"The region enclosed by two radii and the arc between them is a sector. The angle between the two radii, measured at the centre, is called the angle of the sector.","visual":"🍕"},{"title":"Minor and major sector","body":"The smaller wedge is the minor sector and the larger one is the major sector. Since the two together make the full circle, the angle of the major sector is 360° minus the angle of the minor sector.","visual":"🌓"},{"title":"Segment of a circle","body":"The region enclosed between a chord and its corresponding arc is a segment. The smaller piece is the minor segment and the larger piece, on the other side of the chord, is the major segment.","visual":"🌗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of a circle is enclosed by two radii and the arc between them?","options":["Sector","Segment","Chord","Diameter"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two radii plus the arc between their endpoints enclose a sector."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of a circle is enclosed between a chord and its corresponding arc?","options":["Segment","Sector","Radius","Tangent"],"answer":0,"explain":"A chord together with the arc it cuts off encloses a segment."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the angle of the minor sector OAB is 70°, what is the angle of the major sector?","options":["290°","70°","180°","110°"],"answer":0,"explain":"Angle of major sector = 360° − 70° = 290°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Unless stated otherwise, when this chapter says just 'sector' and 'segment', which does it mean?","options":["The minor sector and the minor segment","The major sector and the major segment","The minor sector and the major segment","The major sector and the minor segment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The textbook's remark says 'sector' and 'segment' mean the minor sector and minor segment unless stated otherwise."}]},{"id":"ac3","title":"The Lighthouse Keeper's Sweep","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"The Lighthouse Keeper needs to know how much sea her rotating beam covers, and how far its tip travels along an arc. Give her the sector-area and arc-length formulas so no ship strays into danger.","teach":[{"title":"Area of a sector","body":"For a sector of angle θ in a circle of radius r, the area is (θ/360) × πr². This comes from the unitary method: the full circle (angle 360°) has area πr², so a θ° slice gets that fraction of it.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Length of an arc","body":"For the same sector, the length of its arc is (θ/360) × 2πr, since the full circumference 2πr corresponds to 360°.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Worked Example","body":"For radius 4 cm and angle 30° (π = 3.14), the sector area is (30/360) × 3.14 × 16 ≈ 4.19 cm², and the major sector's area is πr² minus that, about 46.1 cm².","visual":"🕯️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The area of a sector with angle θ (in degrees) and radius r is:","options":["(θ/360) × πr²","(θ/180) × πr²","(θ/360) × 2πr","πr² − θ"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sector's fraction of the full circle's area is θ/360, so area = (θ/360) × πr²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sector has radius 4 cm and angle 30°. Using π = 3.14, find its area in cm² (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":4.19,"explain":"(30/360) × 3.14 × 4² = 12.56/3 ≈ 4.19 cm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same circle (radius 4 cm, minor sector angle 30°, π = 3.14), find the area of the major sector in cm² (round to 1 decimal place).","answer":46.1,"explain":"Major sector = πr² − minor sector = 50.24 − 4.19 ≈ 46.1 cm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a circle of radius 21 cm, an arc subtends 60° at the centre. Find the length of the arc in cm. (Use π = 22/7)","answer":22,"explain":"Arc length = (60/360) × 2 × 22/7 × 21 = 22 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same arc (radius 21 cm, angle 60°, π = 22/7), find the area of the sector it forms, in cm².","answer":231,"explain":"Sector area = (60/360) × 22/7 × 21² = 231 cm²."}]},{"id":"ac4","title":"The Segment Vault","icon":"🌗","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the chord-wall lies a sealed vault. Its lock is a puzzle carved by an old temple architect: take a sector, cut away the triangle inside it, and what remains is the key.","teach":[{"title":"Area of a segment","body":"The segment is what's left of a sector after you remove the triangle formed by the two radii and the chord: Area of segment = Area of sector − Area of the triangle.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Major pieces from minor pieces","body":"Since the minor and major sectors together make the whole circle, Area of major sector = πr² − Area of minor sector. The same relation holds for segments: Area of major segment = πr² − Area of minor segment.","visual":"🌗"},{"title":"Worked Example","body":"For radius 21 cm and ∠AOB = 120° (π = 22/7), the sector OAYB has area 462 cm². Subtracting the area of triangle OAB (found using trigonometry) gives the area of segment AYB.","visual":"🏛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Area of a segment of a circle is found by:","options":["Area of sector − Area of the triangle formed by the two radii and the chord","Area of sector + Area of the triangle","πr² − Area of the sector","2 × Area of the sector"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing the triangle from the sector leaves exactly the segment."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is the area of the major segment related to the minor segment?","options":["Major segment = πr² − minor segment","Major segment = minor segment","Major segment = πr² + minor segment","Major segment = 2 × minor segment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Minor and major segments together make up the whole circle's area."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A circle has radius 21 cm and ∠AOB = 120°. Using π = 22/7, find the area of sector OAYB in cm².","answer":462,"explain":"Sector area = (120/360) × 22/7 × 21² = 462 cm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A chord of a circle of radius 15 cm subtends 60° at the centre. Using π = 3.14 and √3 = 1.73, find the area of the minor segment in cm² (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":20.44,"explain":"Sector area = 117.75 cm²; equilateral triangle area = (√3/4) × 15² = 97.3125 cm²; segment = 117.75 − 97.3125 ≈ 20.44 cm²."}]},{"id":"ac5","title":"Chakra, the Wheel Warden","icon":"🎡","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Circle Kingdom spins Chakra, the Wheel Warden, built from every grazing horse, sweeping wiper, and turning minute hand you've studied. Only mastery of the full circle — rim, wedge, and vault — can still its endless rotation.","boss":{"name":"Chakra, the Wheel Warden","taunt":"Grazing horses, sweeping wipers, spinning minute hands — you think you've mastered every wedge of my domain? Prove it, traveler, or be swept back to the rim!"},"teach":[{"title":"Final review","body":"Remember the three core relations: Arc length = (θ/360) × 2πr, Area of sector = (θ/360) × πr², and Area of segment = Area of sector − Area of triangle.","visual":"🎡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A circle's circumference is 22 cm. Using π = 22/7, find the area of a quadrant (a 90° sector) of the circle, in cm².","answer":9.625,"explain":"Circumference 22 cm gives r = 3.5 cm; quadrant area = (90/360) × 22/7 × 3.5² = 9.625 cm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The minute hand of a clock is 14 cm long. Using π = 22/7, find the area swept by the minute hand in 5 minutes, in cm² (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":51.33,"explain":"5 minutes is 30° of rotation; area = (30/360) × 22/7 × 14² ≈ 51.33 cm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A horse is tied to a peg at a corner of a square field by a 5 m rope. Using π = 3.14, find the area it can graze, in m².","answer":19.625,"explain":"The horse grazes a quarter circle (90°, since a square's corner angle is 90°) of radius 5 m: (90/360) × 3.14 × 5² = 19.625 m²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A lighthouse spreads light over a sector of angle 80° to a distance of 16.5 km. Using π = 3.14, find the area of sea covered, in km² (round to 2 decimal places).","answer":189.97,"explain":"Area = (80/360) × 3.14 × 16.5² ≈ 189.97 km²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Area of a sector of angle p (in degrees) of a circle with radius R is:","options":["(p/720) × 2πR²","(p/180) × 2πR","(p/180) × πR²","(p/360) × 2πR"],"answer":0,"explain":"(p/720) × 2πR² simplifies to (p/360) × πR², the standard sector-area formula."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Area of the major sector OAQB equals:","options":["πr² − Area of the minor sector OAPB","Area of the minor sector OAPB","πr² + Area of the minor sector OAPB","2 × Area of the minor sector OAPB"],"answer":0,"explain":"The minor and major sectors together make up the full circle of area πr²."}]}]},{"id":"surfacevolumes10","index":11,"name":"Surface Areas and Volumes","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 12","emoji":"🧊","palette":{"from":"#c084fc","to":"#581c87"},"lessons":[{"id":"sv1","title":"Two Shapes, One Solid","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"An oil tanker rumbles past the Atlas gate, and you realise its tank isn't one basic shape at all — it's a cylinder with a hemisphere capping each end. The gatekeeper won't let you through until you can explain what happens to a surface when two solids are joined.","teach":[{"title":"Solids join, surfaces hide","body":"Everyday objects — an oil tanker, a test tube, a medicine capsule — are combinations of basic solids like cylinders, cones, spheres and hemispheres. When two solids are glued flat-face to flat-face, that shared flat face is sealed inside and is no longer part of the outer surface.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Total surface area = sum of exposed curved surfaces","body":"For the tanker (cylinder + two hemispheres), you only ever see the curved surface of the cylinder and the curved surfaces of the two hemispheres. So TSA of the new solid = CSA of hemisphere + CSA of cylinder + CSA of other hemisphere.","visual":"🛢️"},{"title":"Volumes don't hide anything","body":"Surface area loses the hidden joining faces, but volume never does — the volume of a combined solid is simply the sum of the volumes of its parts, because every bit of space still counts.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A medicine capsule is a cylinder with a hemisphere stuck on each end. Why is its total surface area NOT the CSA of the cylinder plus the full TSA of each hemisphere?","options":["Because the flat circular faces where the hemispheres meet the cylinder are hidden inside, not on the outside","Because hemispheres have no curved surface at all","Because a cylinder has no curved surface","Because total surface area is always smaller than curved surface area"],"answer":0,"explain":"The flat circle of each hemisphere is glued to the cylinder's flat end, so that circle disappears from the outer surface."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A capsule's cylindrical part plus two hemispherical ends has overall length 14 mm and diameter 5 mm (radius 2.5 mm, so the cylinder's own height is 14 − 5 = 9 mm). Using CSA(cylinder) + 2×CSA(hemisphere), find its surface area in mm² (use π = 22/7).","answer":220,"explain":"2π(2.5)(9) + 4π(2.5)² = 141.43 + 78.57 = 220 mm²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which expression correctly gives the total surface area of a cylinder capped by a hemisphere at each end?","options":["CSA of cylinder + 2 × CSA of one hemisphere","TSA of cylinder + 2 × TSA of one hemisphere","CSA of cylinder only, ignoring the hemispheres","Volume of cylinder + volume of the two hemispheres"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the curved parts are ever exposed; the flat circular joins are hidden inside the solid."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two cubes, each of volume 64 cm³, are joined end to end (so each cube has edge 4 cm, forming an 8 cm × 4 cm × 4 cm cuboid). Find the surface area of the resulting cuboid in cm².","answer":160,"explain":"TSA = 2(lb + bh + hl) = 2(8×4 + 4×4 + 4×8) = 2(32+16+32) = 160 cm². (Joining the cubes hides one 4×4 face from each cube.)"}]},{"id":"sv2","title":"Rasheed's Birthday Top","icon":"🎨","kind":"lesson","story":"Rasheed hands you a wooden spinning top (lattu) shaped like a cone sitting under a smooth hemisphere — but it has no colour on it yet. To know how much paint you'll need, you must work out the exact area of its curved surface.","teach":[{"title":"Curved surface area of a hemisphere","body":"The curved surface area of a hemisphere of radius r is half the surface area of a full sphere: CSA = (1/2)(4πr²) = 2πr².","visual":"🌗"},{"title":"Slant height and CSA of a cone","body":"For a cone of radius r and height h, the slant height is l = √(r² + h²), and its curved surface area is CSA = πrl.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Colour only what you can see","body":"The top's flat circle (where the cone meets the hemisphere) is hidden, so the surface Rasheed must colour is TSA of the top = CSA of hemisphere + CSA of cone — never the sum of their separate total surface areas.","visual":"🪀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Rasheed's top is 5 cm tall in total with diameter 3.5 cm (radius 1.75 cm). The hemisphere's radius equals 1.75 cm, so the cone's height is 5 − 1.75 = 3.25 cm and its slant height works out to about 3.7 cm. Find the area (in cm²) Rasheed has to colour (use π = 22/7). Round to 1 decimal place.","answer":39.6,"explain":"CSA(cone) + CSA(hemisphere) = πrl + 2πr² ≈ 20.35 + 19.25 = 39.6 cm²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a cone of radius r and height h, the slant height l is given by:","options":["l = √(r² + h²)","l = r + h","l = √(r² − h²)","l = 2πr"],"answer":0,"explain":"The slant height is the hypotenuse of the right triangle formed by the radius and the height."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A toy is a cone of radius 3.5 cm mounted on a hemisphere of the same radius; the total height of the toy is 15.5 cm (so the cone's height is 12 cm and its slant height is 12.5 cm). Find the total surface area of the toy in cm² (use π = 22/7).","answer":214.5,"explain":"CSA(cone) + CSA(hemisphere) = πrl + 2πr² = 137.5 + 77 = 214.5 cm²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For Rasheed's top, is the total surface area equal to the sum of the separate total surface areas of the cone and the hemisphere?","options":["No — the flat circular face where they join is hidden and must be left out of both","Yes, always add the two total surface areas directly","No — you must add their volumes instead","Yes, but only if the radii are different"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adding the full TSAs would double-count the two matching flat circles that vanish when the solids are joined."}]},{"id":"sv3","title":"The Decorator's Block","icon":"🧱","kind":"lesson","story":"A craftsman in the Atlas market is finishing a decorative block: a wooden cube with a smooth hemisphere fixed on top. He needs the total surface area to know how much varnish to buy — and warns you that a plain cube-plus-hemisphere addition will give the wrong answer.","teach":[{"title":"A cube loses a patch, not a face","body":"When a hemisphere sits on top of a cube, the circular patch of the cube's top face directly under the hemisphere is covered and no longer exposed, so it must be subtracted from the cube's total surface area.","visual":"⬛"},{"title":"The block's surface area formula","body":"Surface area of the block = TSA of cube − base area of hemisphere + CSA of hemisphere = 6(edge)² − πr² + 2πr² = 6(edge)² + πr².","visual":"🧱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A decorative block has a cube base of edge 5 cm, topped by a hemisphere of diameter 4.2 cm (radius 2.1 cm). Find the total surface area of the block in cm² (use π = 22/7).","answer":163.86,"explain":"TSA = 6(5)² + π(2.1)² = 150 + 13.86 = 163.86 cm²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the base area of the hemisphere subtracted from the cube's total surface area, instead of just adding the hemisphere's full surface?","options":["Because that circular patch of the cube's face is now covered by the hemisphere, so it is no longer part of the outer surface","Because hemispheres do not have a flat circular base","Because the cube's total surface area formula is wrong otherwise","Because volume and surface area are the same thing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only exposed surface counts — the hidden circular patch under the hemisphere is removed from the cube's TSA."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cubical block of side 7 cm is surmounted by the greatest possible hemisphere (so its diameter equals 7 cm, radius 3.5 cm). Find the total surface area of the solid in cm² (use π = 22/7).","answer":332.5,"explain":"TSA = 6(7)² + π(3.5)² = 294 + 38.5 = 332.5 cm²."}]},{"id":"sv4","title":"Painting the Toy Rocket","icon":"🚀","kind":"lesson","story":"A wooden toy rocket needs its cone painted orange and its cylinder painted yellow before launch — but the cone's base is wider than the cylinder's, leaving a stubborn ring exposed at the join that also needs orange paint.","teach":[{"title":"Watch for a mismatched join","body":"In the toy rocket, the cone's base radius (2.5 cm) is larger than the cylinder's base radius (1.5 cm). So a ring-shaped strip of the cone's base — not just its curved surface — is left exposed and must be painted too.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Area to paint orange (the cone)","body":"Orange area = CSA of cone + base area of cone − base area of cylinder = πrl + πr²(cone) − πr²(cylinder), because the ring is the cone's base circle minus the part covered by the cylinder.","visual":"🟠"},{"title":"Area to paint yellow (the cylinder)","body":"Yellow area = CSA of cylinder + area of the cylinder's own bottom base = 2πrh + πr² = πr(2h + r).","visual":"🟡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the toy rocket need a ring painted orange near the join, instead of just the cone's curved surface?","options":["Because the cone's base circle is wider than the cylinder's, leaving an exposed ring around the join","Because cones never have a flat base","Because the cylinder's curved surface is orange","Because the rocket has no cylindrical part"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cone's base (radius 2.5 cm) is bigger than the cylinder's base (radius 1.5 cm), so a ring of the cone's base is left showing."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The rocket's cone has radius 2.5 cm, height 6 cm (slant height 6.5 cm); the cylinder has radius 1.5 cm. Find the area painted orange, in cm² (use π = 3.14): πrl + πr²(cone) − πr²(cylinder).","answer":63.585,"explain":"π[(2.5×6.5) + 2.5² − 1.5²] = 3.14 × 20.25 = 63.585 cm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The rocket's cylindrical part has radius 1.5 cm and height 20 cm. Find the area painted yellow, in cm² (use π = 3.14): πr(2h + r).","answer":195.465,"explain":"3.14 × 1.5 × (40 + 1.5) = 4.71 × 41.5 = 195.465 cm²."}]},{"id":"sv5","title":"Mayank's Bird-Bath","icon":"🐦","kind":"lesson","story":"Mayank is building a bird-bath for his garden: a cylinder with a hemispherical dip scooped into one end so birds have a shallow place to land. Whether a hemisphere bulges out or is scooped in, its curved surface is still there to measure.","teach":[{"title":"A depression is still a curved surface","body":"Scooping a hemispherical dent into a cylinder doesn't remove surface area — it just curves it inward. The bird-bath's total surface area is still CSA of cylinder + CSA of hemisphere = 2πrh + 2πr² = 2πr(h + r).","visual":"🕳️"},{"title":"Keep your units consistent","body":"Always convert every length to the same unit before calculating — for example, 1.45 m must become 145 cm if the radius is given in centimetres.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Mayank's bird-bath is a cylinder of height 1.45 m (145 cm) and radius 30 cm, with a hemispherical depression of the same radius at one end. Find its total surface area in cm² (use π = 22/7): 2πr(h + r).","answer":33000,"explain":"2×(22/7)×30×(145+30) = 2×22×30×175/7 = 33000 cm² (= 3.3 m²)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"1.45 m is equal to how many centimetres?","options":["145 cm","14.5 cm","1450 cm","1.45 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 m = 100 cm, so 1.45 m = 145 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A vessel is a hollow hemisphere of diameter 14 cm (radius 7 cm) mounted by a hollow cylinder, with the total height of the vessel being 13 cm (so the cylinder's height is 13 − 7 = 6 cm). Find the inner surface area of the vessel in cm² (use π = 22/7): 2πr(h + r).","answer":572,"explain":"2×(22/7)×7×(6+7) = 44×13 = 572 cm²."}]},{"id":"sv6","title":"Where Volumes Simply Add","icon":"🥤","kind":"lesson","story":"Shanta's workshop is shaped like a cuboid topped with a half-cylinder roof, and a juice-seller's glass has a hidden hemispherical bump at its base that cheats customers out of a little juice. Time to see how volume behaves differently from surface area.","teach":[{"title":"Volumes just add up","body":"Unlike surface area, nothing is 'hidden' when you compute volume — the volume of a solid built by attaching parts together is simply the sum of the volumes of each part.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Hollowed-out solids: subtract instead","body":"When a shape is scooped or carved out of another (like a hemisphere raised into the base of a glass), the actual capacity is the outer volume minus the volume of the removed/occupied piece.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"Circumscribing solids: find the gap","body":"If one solid tightly surrounds another (like a cylinder circumscribing a cone-and-hemisphere toy), the leftover space equals volume of the surrounding solid minus volume of the solid inside.","visual":"🎁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Shanta's shed has a cuboidal base 15 m × 7 m with height 8 m, topped by a half-cylinder of diameter 7 m and length 15 m. Find the total volume of air the shed can hold, in m³ (use π = 22/7): volume of cuboid + (1/2) × volume of cylinder.","answer":1128.75,"explain":"15×7×8 + (1/2)×(22/7)×3.5²×15 = 840 + 288.75 = 1128.75 m³."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In that same shed, machinery occupies 300 m³ and 20 workers occupy 0.08 m³ each (1.6 m³ total). Find the remaining volume of air, in m³.","answer":827.15,"explain":"1128.75 − (300 + 1.6) = 827.15 m³."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A juice glass has inner diameter 5 cm (radius 2.5 cm) and height 10 cm, but a hemispherical bump of the same radius rises from its base. The apparent capacity (as if it were a plain cylinder) is 196.25 cm³, and the hemisphere's volume is about 32.71 cm³. Find the glass's actual capacity in cm³.","answer":163.54,"explain":"Actual capacity = apparent capacity − volume of hemisphere = 196.25 − 32.71 = 163.54 cm³."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A solid toy is a hemisphere (radius 2 cm) topped by a cone of the same radius and height 2 cm. A right circular cylinder of radius 2 cm and height 4 cm exactly circumscribes this toy. Find the difference between the cylinder's volume and the toy's volume, in cm³ (use π = 3.14).","answer":25.12,"explain":"Volume of toy = (2/3)πr³ + (1/3)πr²h = 25.12 cm³; cylinder volume = πr²(4) = 50.24 cm³; difference = 50.24 − 25.12 = 25.12 cm³."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the juice glass's actual capacity have to be LESS than a plain cylinder of the same outer size?","options":["Because the hemispherical bump at the base takes up space that liquid would otherwise fill","Because hemispheres always increase volume","Because the radius changes partway up the glass","Because π is smaller for curved solids"],"answer":0,"explain":"The raised hemisphere occupies part of the cylinder's interior, so liquid can only fill the rest."}]},{"id":"sv7","title":"The Volume Keeper's Vault","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Atlas vault, the Volume Keeper guards a treasury of combined solids — capsules, poles, pens, and a vessel of lead shots — and will only lower the gate for someone who can measure every hidden surface and every combined volume without a single mistake.","boss":{"name":"The Volume Keeper","taunt":"Every join hides a face, every hollow steals a volume — prove you never miss either, apprentice!"},"teach":[{"title":"Surface area: only what's exposed","body":"When solids combine, add only the curved (exposed) surfaces and drop any flat faces that got glued together or covered — total surface area is rarely just the sum of the parts' own TSAs.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Volume: everything still counts","body":"Volumes of joined solids simply add; volumes of hollowed-out or displaced solids simply subtract — no space ever vanishes the way surface does.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A capsule (cylinder of radius 2.5 mm, height 9 mm, capped by two hemispheres of the same radius) has surface area = CSA(cylinder) + 2×CSA(hemisphere). Find it in mm² (use π = 22/7).","answer":220,"explain":"2π(2.5)(9) + 4π(2.5)² = 141.43 + 78.57 = 220 mm²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A solid iron pole is a cylinder of height 220 cm, base diameter 24 cm (radius 12 cm), surmounted by a second cylinder of height 60 cm and radius 8 cm. Its total volume is 111532.8 cm³. Given 1 cm³ of iron has a mass of about 8 g, find the mass of the pole in kilograms (use π = 3.14). Round to 2 decimal places.","answer":892.26,"explain":"Mass = 111532.8 × 8 g = 892262.4 g = 892.26 kg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An inverted conical vessel (height 8 cm, top radius 5 cm) is filled to the brim with water. Spherical lead shots of radius 0.5 cm are dropped in until one-fourth of the water overflows. How many lead shots were dropped (use π = 22/7)?","answer":100,"explain":"Cone volume = 209.52 cm³, one-fourth = 52.38 cm³; each shot's volume = 0.524 cm³; 52.38 ÷ 0.524 = 100 shots."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A wooden pen stand is a cuboid 15 cm × 10 cm × 3.5 cm with 4 conical depressions of radius 0.5 cm and depth 1.4 cm drilled in for pens. Find the volume of wood remaining, in cm³ (use π = 22/7). Round to 2 decimal places.","answer":523.53,"explain":"Cuboid volume = 525 cm³; each conical depression = (1/3)π(0.5)²(1.4) ≈ 0.3667 cm³, so 4 depressions ≈ 1.47 cm³; wood left = 525 − 1.47 = 523.53 cm³."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which principle explains why the total surface area of a combined solid is usually LESS than the sum of the total surface areas of its separate parts?","options":["The surfaces where the parts join are hidden inside the new solid and are not counted","Curved surfaces are always smaller than flat surfaces","Total surface area cannot be calculated for combined solids","Every combined solid is actually hollow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Joining two solids seals away the matching flat faces at the join, so those areas are dropped from the total."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When solids are attached to each other (not hollowed out), how do you find the volume of the combined solid?","options":["Add the volumes of each part directly — nothing is hidden in volume the way it is in surface area","Subtract the smaller volume from the larger one","Multiply the volumes of each part together","Average the volumes of each part"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every bit of space in each part still occupies space in the combined solid, so volumes simply add."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Back at the rocket workshop: the cone (radius 2.5 cm, height 6 cm, slant height 6.5 cm) sits on a cylinder of radius 1.5 cm. Find the area painted orange in cm² — CSA(cone) + base area of cone − base area of cylinder (use π = 3.14).","answer":63.585,"explain":"π[(2.5×6.5) + 2.5² − 1.5²] = 3.14 × 20.25 = 63.585 cm²."}]}]},{"id":"statistics10","index":12,"name":"Statistics","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 13","emoji":"📊","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"st1","title":"The Class Mark Compass","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"A wind-torn scroll lists the marks of 30 students, too scattered to read at a glance. To cross the Archivist's Gate you must condense the scroll into class intervals and cast the Direct Method to reveal their true average.","teach":[{"title":"Mean of Ungrouped Data","body":"For observations x1...xn with frequencies f1...fn, the mean is x̄ = Σfixi / Σfi, the sum of each value times its frequency, divided by the total frequency. For the 30 students' exact marks, this gives a mean of 59.3.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Class Mark","body":"When data is grouped into class intervals, each interval is represented by its class mark, found as (upper limit + lower limit) / 2. The class 10-25 has class mark (10+25)/2 = 17.5.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Direct Method for Grouped Data","body":"Treat each class mark xi as the representative value and apply x̄ = Σfixi/Σfi. For the grouped marks data, Σfixi = 1860 and Σfi = 30, giving mean = 62 — close to but not exactly the ungrouped mean of 59.3, because of the mid-point assumption.","visual":"➗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the grouped marks table, the class 70-85 has what class mark?","answer":77.5,"explain":"(70+85)/2 = 77.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the grouped marks table, Σfixi = 1860 and Σfi = 30. What is the mean marks by the direct method?","answer":62,"explain":"1860/30 = 62."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the grouped-data mean (62) only an approximate value, unlike the exact ungrouped mean (59.3)?","options":["Because grouping assumes every observation in a class is located exactly at the class mark","Because grouped data always has fewer observations than ungrouped data","Because the direct method only works for odd numbers of classes","Because class marks must always be rounded to whole numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The class-mark assumption centres all values of a class at its mid-point, which is only an approximation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the class interval 55-70, what is the class mark?","answer":62.5,"explain":"(55+70)/2 = 62.5."}]},{"id":"st2","title":"The Assumed Anchor","icon":"⚓","kind":"lesson","story":"The gate's numbers grow larger and the direct spell grows heavy to cast. An old inscription hints at a shortcut: drop an anchor near the centre of the data and measure everything as a small deviation from it.","teach":[{"title":"Why Assume a Mean","body":"When xi and fi are large, computing fixi directly becomes tedious. Instead, pick any class mark as the assumed mean 'a' (often near the centre) and work with deviations di = xi - a.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"The Formula","body":"The mean is x̄ = a + Σfidi/Σfi. For the marks data, taking a = 47.5, Σfidi = 435 and Σfi = 30 gives x̄ = 47.5 + 435/30 = 47.5 + 14.5 = 62, the same answer as the direct method.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Any Anchor Works","body":"An activity in the chapter shows that choosing any xi as 'a' still gives the same mean, 62. The value of the mean does not depend on the choice of assumed mean.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the deviation table, di for the class 25-40 (xi=32.5, a=47.5) equals?","answer":-15,"explain":"di = xi - a = 32.5 - 47.5 = -15."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using a=47.5, Σfidi=435, Σfi=30, find the mean by the assumed mean method.","answer":62,"explain":"x̄ = 47.5 + 435/30 = 47.5 + 14.5 = 62."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the chapter's activity of trying different values of 'a' demonstrate?","options":["The mean stays the same (62) no matter which class mark is chosen as the assumed mean","The mean changes every time a different assumed mean is chosen","The assumed mean method only works for even numbers of classes","The assumed mean must always equal the mode"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter shows the mean obtained does not depend on the choice of 'a'."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the class 85-100 (xi=92.5), what is di if a=47.5?","answer":45,"explain":"di = 92.5 - 47.5 = 45."}]},{"id":"st3","title":"The Step-Down Shortcut","icon":"🪜","kind":"lesson","story":"The deviations before you all share a hidden factor of 15. A step-carved shortcut lets you shrink every number before you multiply, so even the heaviest tables become light to carry.","teach":[{"title":"Step-Deviation Formula","body":"When all di share a common factor h (the class size), define ui = (xi - a)/h. Then x̄ = a + h × (Σfiui/Σfi). This shrinks the numbers you must multiply.","visual":"🔽"},{"title":"Worked Example","body":"For the marks data, a=47.5, h=15, Σfiui=29, Σfi=30, giving x̄ = 47.5 + 15×(29/30) = 47.5 + 14.5 = 62, matching both earlier methods.","visual":"✅"},{"title":"Choosing a Method","body":"If xi and fi are small, use the direct method. If they are large, use the assumed mean or step-deviation method. Even with unequal class sizes, step-deviation works if h is a suitable divisor of the di's, as in a cricket-wickets example where a=200, h=20 gives mean=152.89.","visual":"🏏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For class 70-85 (xi=77.5, a=47.5, h=15), what is ui?","answer":2,"explain":"ui = (77.5-47.5)/15 = 30/15 = 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using a=47.5, h=15, Σfiui=29, Σfi=30, compute the mean by step-deviation.","answer":62,"explain":"x̄ = 47.5 + 15×(29/30) = 47.5 + 14.5 = 62."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the percentage-of-female-teachers data, Σfixi=1390 and Σfi=35. Find the mean by the direct method (2 decimals).","answer":39.71,"explain":"1390/35 = 39.71 (rounded)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the cricket-wickets data, a=200, h=20, Σfiui=-106, Σfi=45. The mean number of wickets, rounded to 2 decimals, is?","answer":152.89,"explain":"x̄ = 200 + 20×(-106/45) = 200 - 47.11 = 152.89."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the cricket-wickets example, why can the step-deviation method still be used even though the class widths vary (20-60, 60-100, 100-150...)?","options":["Because h can still be chosen as a suitable divisor of the deviations even when class widths differ","Because unequal class sizes always force the use of the direct method","Because the mode formula replaces the mean formula in such cases","Because only the assumed mean method works when widths are unequal"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter notes h can be any suitable divisor of the di's, even with unequal class sizes."}]},{"id":"st4","title":"The Modal Class Trial","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"The guardian of the Hall of Frequency demands to know which chamber is most crowded, not on average, but by sheer popularity. You must find the modal class and the value that represents it best.","teach":[{"title":"Mode Recap","body":"The mode is the value that occurs most often. For wickets taken in 10 matches (2,6,4,5,0,2,1,3,2,3), the value 2 occurs most (3 times), so the mode is 2.","visual":"🔂"},{"title":"Modal Class Formula","body":"For grouped data, first find the modal class, the class with the highest frequency. Then Mode = l + [(f1-f0)/(2f1-f0-f2)] × h, where l=lower limit, h=class size, f1=modal class frequency, f0=frequency of the class before it, f2=frequency of the class after it.","visual":"🏆"},{"title":"Worked Example","body":"For a household family-size survey, the modal class is 3-5 (frequency 8) with l=3, h=2, f1=8, f0=7, f2=2. Mode = 3 + [(8-7)/(16-7-2)]×2 = 3.286 family members.","visual":"🏠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the wickets data (2,6,4,5,0,2,1,3,2,3), what is the mode?","answer":2,"explain":"2 occurs 3 times, more than any other value."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the household family-size data, the modal class is 3-5 with f1=8, f0=7, f2=2, l=3, h=2. What is the mode (3 decimals)?","answer":3.286,"explain":"Mode = 3 + [(8-7)/(2×8-7-2)]×2 = 3 + (1/7)×2 = 3.286."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For a marks distribution, the modal class is 40-55 with f1=7, f0=3, f2=6, l=40, h=15. What is the mode?","answer":52,"explain":"Mode = 40 + [(7-3)/(14-3-6)]×15 = 40 + (4/5)×15 = 52."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In that marks example, the mode is 52 while the mean is 62. What does this comparison tell us?","options":["Most students scored around 52, but a few higher scorers pulled the average up to 62","The data must contain an error since mode and mean can never differ","The mode is always greater than the mean for any grouped data","52 and 62 must be the same class mark"],"answer":0,"explain":"The most frequent score cluster (mode) can differ from the overall average (mean)."}]},{"id":"st5","title":"The Running Tally","icon":"📈","kind":"lesson","story":"A locked gate only opens to a running tally: how many are 'less than' a mark, and how many are 'more than or equal to' it. You must keep both counts as you march down the scroll.","teach":[{"title":"Less Than Type","body":"A cumulative frequency (less than type) table adds up frequencies from the start. For 53 students' marks, 'less than 20' = 5+3 = 8, 'less than 30' = 8+4 = 12, and so on up to 'less than 100' = 53.","visual":"⬇️"},{"title":"More Than Type","body":"A more than (or equal to) type table subtracts frequencies from the total instead. Starting at 53 (more than or equal to 0), 'more than or equal to 10' = 53-5 = 48, 'more than or equal to 20' = 48-3 = 45, and so on.","visual":"⬆️"},{"title":"Why It Matters","body":"Cumulative frequency tables let us locate where the middle of the data lies, essential for finding the median of grouped data, and for plotting cumulative frequency curves called ogives (for which the class intervals must first be made continuous).","visual":"🗂️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For marks data with classes 0-10:5, 10-20:3, 20-30:4, 30-40:3, what is the cumulative frequency for 'less than 40'?","answer":15,"explain":"5+3+4+3 = 15."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same 53-student data, 'more than or equal to 30' is 41. Given the 30-40 class has frequency 3, what is 'more than or equal to 40'?","answer":38,"explain":"41 - 3 = 38."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the cumulative frequency of a class, as defined in the chapter?","options":["The frequency obtained by adding the frequencies of all the classes preceding the given class","The average of all frequencies in the table","The highest frequency among all classes","The class mark multiplied by the frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the chapter's exact definition of cumulative frequency."}]},{"id":"st6","title":"The Halfway Ward","icon":"⚗️","kind":"lesson","story":"In the Halfway Ward, only the value that splits 51 guardians exactly in two may pass: half shorter, half taller. You must find the median class and the precise height that divides them.","teach":[{"title":"Median Class","body":"Find n/2, then locate the class whose cumulative frequency is just greater than (and nearest to) n/2. This is the median class.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Median Formula","body":"Median = l + [(n/2 - cf)/f] × h, where l = lower limit of the median class, cf = cumulative frequency of the class preceding it, f = frequency of the median class, h = class size.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Worked Example","body":"For 51 girls' heights, n/2 = 25.5 falls in class 145-150 (l=145, cf=11, f=18, h=5). Median = 145 + [(25.5-11)/18]×5 = 149.03 cm — about half the girls are shorter, half taller.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 53 students' marks, n=53 so n/2=26.5, which lies in class 60-70 (l=60, cf=22, f=7, h=10). What is the median?","answer":66.4,"explain":"Median = 60 + [(26.5-22)/7]×10 = 60 + 6.4 = 66.4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the 51 girls' heights, n/2=25.5, median class 145-150 with l=145, cf=11, f=18, h=5. What is the median height (2 decimals)?","answer":149.03,"explain":"Median = 145 + [(25.5-11)/18]×5 = 149.03."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does a median height of 149.03 cm mean for the 51 girls?","options":["About half the girls are shorter than 149.03 cm and half are taller","Every girl is exactly 149.03 cm tall","149.03 cm is the most common height among the girls","149.03 cm is the average of the tallest and shortest girl only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The median splits the ordered data into two equal halves."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 100 students' ungrouped marks, n=100 is even, so the median is the average of the 50th and 51st observations, which are 28 and 29. What is the median?","answer":28.5,"explain":"(28+29)/2 = 28.5."}]},{"id":"st7","title":"The Vanished Numbers","icon":"🔮","kind":"lesson","story":"Two frequencies, x and y, have been erased from a damaged scroll. Only the total count and a known median remain. Rebuild the missing numbers using the median formula in reverse.","teach":[{"title":"Setting Up Equations","body":"When frequencies x and y are missing but the total n and median are known, use n = Σfi for one equation, then substitute into the median formula for a second equation.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Worked Example","body":"With total frequency 100 and median 525 (falling in class 500-600, l=500, f=20, h=100, cf=36+x), the total gives x+y=24, and solving the median equation gives x=9.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Solving Together","body":"Once x=9 is found, substituting into x+y=24 gives y=15 — both missing frequencies recovered using only the median and the total.","visual":"🔓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If the total frequency is 100 and the known frequencies (excluding x and y) sum to 76, what is x+y?","answer":24,"explain":"100 - 76 = 24."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Solving the median equation (median=525, l=500, cf=36+x, f=20, h=100) gives x = ?","answer":9,"explain":"525 = 500 + [(50-(36+x))/20]×100 leads to x = 9."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Given x+y=24 and x=9, what is y?","answer":15,"explain":"24 - 9 = 15."}]},{"id":"st8","title":"The Three Measures United","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"At the final threshold, three keys must align: the Mean, the Median, and the Mode. An old note on the gate reveals that these three are never fully independent, and a curve traced from the running tally can also tell their story.","teach":[{"title":"Choosing the Right Measure","body":"The mean uses every observation and is best for comparing overall performance, but extreme values distort it. The median suits 'typical' values like wages or productivity. The mode is best for finding the most popular or most frequent item.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Empirical Relationship","body":"There is an approximate empirical relationship between the three measures of central tendency: 3 × Median = Mode + 2 × Mean.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Cumulative Frequency Curves (Ogives)","body":"Plotting a cumulative frequency table as a graph produces a curve called an ogive. Before drawing one (or computing median and mode), the class intervals must be made continuous, and the scale need not be the same on both axes.","visual":"📉"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which measure of central tendency is most affected by a few extremely large or small values in the data?","options":["Mean","Median","Mode","Cumulative frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mean uses every value, so extreme values pull it away from the bulk of the data."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which measure would best identify the single most popular TV programme being watched?","options":["Mode","Mean","Median","Class mark"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mode identifies the most frequently occurring value or category."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If the mode of a data set is 52 and the mean is 62, using 3×Median = Mode + 2×Mean, what is the median (2 decimals)?","answer":58.67,"explain":"Median = (52 + 2×62)/3 = 176/3 = 58.67."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What must be true of the class intervals before drawing an ogive or computing the median or mode?","options":["They must be continuous, with no gaps between class limits","They must all have exactly the same frequency","There must be an even number of classes","Every class mark must be a whole number"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter's note states class intervals must be continuous before applying these formulae or constructing an ogive."}]},{"id":"st9","title":"Boss: The Grand Tabulator","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"All three measures of central tendency converge at the final gate, guarded by an entity built from every scroll you have tallied. Only mastery of mean, mode, median, and cumulative frequency together will bring it down.","boss":{"name":"The Grand Tabulator","taunt":"Your numbers are but scattered dust until I demand the mean, the median, and the mode — all in one breath!"},"teach":[{"title":"Final Review","body":"Direct, assumed-mean, and step-deviation methods all give the same mean. The modal class formula finds the mode, the median formula finds the middle value from cumulative frequency, and 3×Median = Mode + 2×Mean links all three.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the direct method with Σfixi=1860 and Σfi=30, the mean marks is?","answer":62,"explain":"1860/30 = 62."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the assumed mean method with a=50, Σfidi=-360, Σfi=35 (female-teacher data), the mean percentage, rounded to 2 decimals, is?","answer":39.71,"explain":"39.71 = 50 + (-360/35)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For household family-size data, modal class 3-5 with f1=8, f0=7, f2=2, l=3, h=2, the mode is?","answer":3.286,"explain":"Mode = 3 + [(8-7)/(16-7-2)]×2 = 3.286."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 51 girls' heights, n/2=25.5, median class 145-150 (l=145, cf=11, f=18, h=5), the median is?","answer":149.03,"explain":"Median = 145 + [(25.5-11)/18]×5 = 149.03."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a marks distribution, mode=52 and mean=62. What does comparing them reveal?","options":["Most students scored near 52, but higher scorers pulled the average up to 62","The data must be invalid because mode and mean disagree","The median must equal 52","The modal class has the lowest frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"The most common score cluster (mode) can differ from the overall average (mean)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For cricket-wickets data, a=200, h=20, Σfiui=-106, Σfi=45, the mean wickets (2 decimals) is?","answer":152.89,"explain":"x̄ = 200 + 20×(-106/45) = 152.89."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If mode=52 and mean=62, using 3×Median = Mode + 2×Mean, the median (2 decimals) is?","answer":58.67,"explain":"(52 + 124)/3 = 58.67."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct formula for the mode of grouped data?","options":["l + [(f1-f0)/(2f1-f0-f2)] × h","l + [(n/2 - cf)/f] × h","a + Σfidi/Σfi","(upper limit + lower limit) / 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the modal-class formula; the other options are the median formula, assumed-mean formula, and class-mark formula respectively."}]}]},{"id":"probability10","index":13,"name":"Probability","subtitle":"Mathematics Chapter 14","emoji":"🎲","palette":{"from":"#fb7185","to":"#881337"},"lessons":[{"id":"pr1","title":"The Coin of Fate","icon":"🪙","kind":"lesson","story":"You arrive at a shrine where a coin never lands the same way twice by choice. The Keeper explains that in the realm of Chance, every unbiased outcome has a fair claim, and to unlock the shrine gate you must learn to weigh possibility itself.","teach":[{"title":"Equally Likely Outcomes","body":"A coin toss has two possible outcomes, head or tail. Since the coin is unbiased and tossed randomly, both outcomes have the same chance of occurring — they are equally likely. A fair die similarly has six equally likely outcomes: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.","visual":"🪙"},{"title":"Theoretical Probability","body":"The theoretical (classical) probability of an event E is P(E) = (number of outcomes favourable to E) / (number of all possible outcomes), assuming the outcomes are equally likely. This definition was given by Pierre-Simon Laplace in 1795.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Heads or Tails","body":"Tossing a coin once gives 2 possible outcomes. Only 1 outcome favours ‘getting a head’, so P(head) = 1/2. By the same reasoning, P(tail) = 1/2.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A coin is tossed once. What is P(getting a head)?","options":["1/2","1","0","1/4"],"answer":0,"explain":"There are 2 equally likely outcomes (H, T) and 1 favours head, so P = 1/2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A fair die is thrown once. How many equally likely outcomes are there in total?","answer":6,"explain":"The outcomes are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who gave the definition of theoretical (classical) probability, in 1795?","options":["Pierre-Simon Laplace","J. Cardan","James Bernoulli","A. de Moivre"],"answer":0,"explain":"Laplace defined theoretical probability in 1795."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A coin is tossed once. What is P(getting a tail), expressed as a decimal?","answer":0.5,"explain":"1 favourable outcome (tail) out of 2 equally likely outcomes = 1/2 = 0.5."}]},{"id":"pr2","title":"Three Balls, Three Fates","icon":"🎒","kind":"lesson","story":"In the Chamber of Equal Chances, Kritika's bag holds a red ball, a blue ball and a yellow ball. Reach in blind, and the shrine will only open once you can name the odds of every colour.","teach":[{"title":"One Bag, Equally Likely Draws","body":"A bag contains a red ball, a blue ball and a yellow ball, all the same size, so drawing any one of them without looking is equally likely. With 3 possible outcomes and 1 outcome favourable to each colour, P(yellow) = P(red) = P(blue) = 1/3.","visual":"🎒"},{"title":"Elementary Events","body":"An event with only one outcome of the experiment, like ‘the ball drawn is yellow’, is called an elementary event. Getting a head in a single coin toss is also an elementary event.","visual":"🔹"},{"title":"The Probabilities Add to One","body":"The sum of the probabilities of all the elementary events of an experiment is always 1. For the three-ball bag: P(Y) + P(R) + P(B) = 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3 = 1.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A bag has one red, one blue and one yellow ball. What is P(drawing the red ball)?","options":["1/3","1/2","1","2/3"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 favourable outcome out of 3 equally likely outcomes gives 1/3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the red/blue/yellow bag, what is P(Yellow) + P(Red) + P(Blue)?","answer":1,"explain":"The probabilities of all elementary events of an experiment always sum to 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an elementary event?","options":["Getting a head when tossing one coin","Getting a number greater than 4 on a die","Drawing a face card from a deck","Getting at least one head from two coins"],"answer":0,"explain":"An elementary event has only one outcome of the experiment; getting a head is one of just two outcomes of a single coin toss."}]},{"id":"pr3","title":"The Mirror of Not-E","icon":"🪞","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the shrine lies the Hall of Mirrors, where every event E has a shadow-twin, ‘not E’. The die-throwing sentinel guards the passage, and only by proving P(E) + P(not E) = 1 will the mirror let you through.","teach":[{"title":"Complementary Events","body":"For any event E, the event ‘not E’ is called the complement of E. Since every outcome must favour either E or not-E, P(E) + P(not E) = 1, so P(not E) = 1 − P(E).","visual":"🪞"},{"title":"Sure and Impossible Events","body":"Throwing a die, the event ‘getting a number less than 7’ always happens, so its probability is 1 — a sure (certain) event. ‘Getting an 8’ can never happen, so its probability is 0 — an impossible event.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"The Range of Probability","body":"The number of favourable outcomes is always between 0 and the total number of outcomes, so for any event E, 0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1. For a die: P(number > 4) = 2/6 = 1/3 and P(number ≤ 4) = 4/6 = 2/3, and 1/3 + 2/3 = 1.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A die is thrown once. What is P(getting a number greater than 4)?","options":["1/3","2/3","1/2","1/6"],"answer":0,"explain":"Favourable outcomes are 5 and 6, so P = 2/6 = 1/3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A die is thrown once. What is P(getting an 8)?","answer":0,"explain":"No face of the die is marked 8, so this is an impossible event with probability 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A die is thrown once. What is P(getting a number less than 7)?","answer":1,"explain":"Every face is marked with a number less than 7, so this is a sure (certain) event with probability 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If P(E) = 0.3, what is P(not E)?","options":["0.7","0.3","1.3","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"P(not E) = 1 − P(E) = 1 − 0.3 = 0.7."}]},{"id":"pr4","title":"The Deck of Fifty-Two","icon":"🃏","kind":"lesson","story":"The Cartomancer's tent smells of old paper and secrets. She fans out a well-shuffled deck of 52 cards and dares you to calculate the odds before she deals the first hand of the tournament.","teach":[{"title":"Anatomy of a Deck","body":"A standard deck has 52 cards in 4 suits of 13 each — spades and clubs (black), hearts and diamonds (red). Each suit has an ace, king, queen, jack, and cards 2 through 10; kings, queens and jacks are called face cards.","visual":"🃏"},{"title":"Drawing an Ace","body":"There are 4 aces in the deck. Drawing one card at random from a well-shuffled deck gives 52 equally likely outcomes, so P(ace) = 4/52 = 1/13.","visual":"🂡"},{"title":"Not an Ace","body":"The event ‘not an ace’ is the complement of ‘ace’, so its favourable outcomes are 52 − 4 = 48, giving P(not ace) = 48/52 = 12/13. This matches 1 − P(ace) = 1 − 1/13 = 12/13.","visual":"❌"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One card is drawn from a well-shuffled deck of 52 cards. What is P(drawing an ace)?","options":["1/13","1/4","4/13","1/52"],"answer":0,"explain":"There are 4 aces among 52 cards, so P = 4/52 = 1/13."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is P(the card drawn is NOT an ace)?","options":["12/13","1/13","4/13","1/4"],"answer":0,"explain":"P(not ace) = 1 − P(ace) = 1 − 1/13 = 12/13."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many face cards (kings, queens, jacks) are there in a standard 52-card deck?","answer":12,"explain":"There are 3 face cards (K, Q, J) in each of the 4 suits, giving 3 × 4 = 12."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many cards in a standard deck belong to the red suits (hearts and diamonds) combined?","answer":26,"explain":"Hearts and diamonds each have 13 cards, so 13 + 13 = 26."}]},{"id":"pr5","title":"Odds Across the Realm","icon":"🎾","kind":"lesson","story":"The Oracle's ledger records real bets from across the kingdom — a tennis champion's winning streak, two friends comparing birthdays. To pass her chamber, translate each story into a probability.","teach":[{"title":"Complements in the Real World","body":"If Sangeeta's probability of winning a match is P(S) = 0.62, then since winning and losing are complementary events, Reshma's probability of winning is P(R) = 1 − 0.62 = 0.38.","visual":"🎾"},{"title":"Different or Same Birthday?","body":"Ignoring leap years, a friend's birthday can fall on any of 365 days. If Savita's birthday is fixed, Hamida's birthday differs from it on 364 of the 365 equally likely days, so P(different birthdays) = 364/365.","visual":"🎂"},{"title":"Same Birthday, by Complement","body":"The event ‘same birthday’ is the complement of ‘different birthdays’, so P(same birthday) = 1 − 364/365 = 1/365 — rare, but real.","visual":"🎁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The probability that Sangeeta wins her match is 0.62. What is the probability that Reshma wins?","answer":0.38,"explain":"Winning is complementary between the two players, so P(Reshma) = 1 − 0.62 = 0.38."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Savita and Hamida are friends (ignoring leap years). What is P(their birthdays are different)?","options":["364/365","1/365","1/2","364/366"],"answer":0,"explain":"Out of 365 equally likely days, 364 of them differ from Savita's birthday."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is P(Savita and Hamida have the same birthday)?","options":["1/365","364/365","1/2","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the complement of ‘different birthdays’: 1 − 364/365 = 1/365."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a group of 3 students, the probability that 2 of them do NOT share a birthday is 0.992. What is the probability that 2 of them DO share a birthday?","answer":0.008,"explain":"These are complementary events, so P(same birthday) = 1 − 0.992 = 0.008."}]},{"id":"pr6","title":"Counting the Crowd","icon":"🎓","kind":"lesson","story":"The Census Keeper of the Study Atlas tallies everything — students in a classroom, marbles in a box, shirts in a carton — and she'll only stamp your passport if your probabilities match her exact counts.","teach":[{"title":"Picking a Class Representative","body":"In a class of 40 students — 25 girls and 15 boys — one name card is drawn at random from 40 equally likely outcomes. So P(girl) = 25/40 = 5/8, and P(boy) = 15/40 = 3/8, which also equals 1 − 5/8.","visual":"🎓"},{"title":"Marbles in a Box","body":"A box has 3 blue, 2 white and 4 red marbles — 9 marbles total. Drawing one at random gives P(white) = 2/9, P(blue) = 3/9 = 1/3, and P(red) = 4/9; these three probabilities add up to 1.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Sorting a Carton of Shirts","body":"A carton of 100 shirts has 88 good, 8 with minor defects, and 4 with major defects. Jimmy only accepts good shirts, so P(acceptable to Jimmy) = 88/100 = 0.88. Sujatha rejects only major-defect shirts, so she accepts good and minor-defect ones: P(acceptable to Sujatha) = 96/100 = 0.96.","visual":"👕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a class of 40 students with 25 girls and 15 boys, what is P(the card drawn names a girl), as a decimal?","answer":0.625,"explain":"25 favourable outcomes out of 40 total gives 25/40 = 0.625."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A box has 3 blue, 2 white and 4 red marbles. What is P(drawing a blue marble)?","options":["1/3","2/9","4/9","1/2"],"answer":0,"explain":"3 blue marbles out of 9 total gives 3/9 = 1/3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A carton has 100 shirts: 88 good, 8 minor-defect, 4 major-defect. What is P(shirt acceptable to Jimmy, who only takes good shirts), as a decimal?","answer":0.88,"explain":"88 good shirts out of 100 total gives 88/100 = 0.88."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same carton, what is P(shirt acceptable to Sujatha, who rejects only major-defect shirts), as a decimal?","answer":0.96,"explain":"Sujatha accepts good plus minor-defect shirts: 88 + 8 = 96 out of 100."}]},{"id":"pr7","title":"Two of Everything","icon":"🎲","kind":"lesson","story":"The final gate before the Oracle's inner sanctum is guarded by twin dice and a pair of coins — every combined toss must be counted before the gate recognises your claim.","teach":[{"title":"Two Coins Together","body":"Tossing two coins at once gives 4 equally likely outcomes: (H,H), (H,T), (T,H), (T,T). ‘At least one head’ is favoured by 3 of these outcomes, so P(at least one head) = 3/4.","visual":"🪙"},{"title":"The 36 Outcomes of Two Dice","body":"Throwing two dice together gives 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely ordered outcomes, since (1,4) and (4,1) are counted separately. Listing them in a 6-by-6 table lets you count how many pairs give any particular sum.","visual":"🎲"},{"title":"Sums on Two Dice","body":"Exactly 5 of the 36 outcomes give a sum of 8 — (2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2) — so P(sum = 8) = 5/36. No outcome sums to 13, so P(sum = 13) = 0, while every outcome sums to 12 or less, so P(sum ≤ 12) = 1.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two coins are tossed together. How many equally likely outcomes are there in total?","answer":4,"explain":"The outcomes are (H,H), (H,T), (T,H) and (T,T)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two coins are tossed together. What is P(at least one head)?","options":["3/4","1/2","1/4","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"3 of the 4 outcomes — (H,H), (H,T), (T,H) — include at least one head."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two dice are thrown together. How many total equally likely outcomes are there?","answer":36,"explain":"Each die has 6 outcomes, giving 6 × 6 = 36 ordered pairs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two dice are thrown together. What is P(the sum of the two numbers is 8)?","options":["5/36","1/6","6/36","4/36"],"answer":0,"explain":"Exactly 5 of the 36 outcomes — (2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2) — sum to 8."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two dice are thrown together. What is P(the sum is 13)?","answer":0,"explain":"No pair of numbers from 1-6 on each die can sum to 13, so this is an impossible event."}]},{"id":"pr8","title":"The Oracle of Chance","icon":"🔮","kind":"boss","story":"Every equation you've conquered across the Study Atlas has led here — the Oracle of Chance, keeper of the final gate, who deals in nothing but odds. Answer her run of trials correctly, and the entire Mathematics Atlas bows to you.","teach":[{"title":"The Final Formula","body":"Everything the Oracle asks reduces to one idea: P(E) = (favourable outcomes) / (all equally likely outcomes), with 0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1, and P(E) + P(not E) = 1 for every complementary pair.","visual":"🔮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A coin is tossed once. What is P(getting a tail)?","options":["1/2","1","0","1/4"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 favourable outcome out of 2 equally likely outcomes gives 1/2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A die is thrown once. What is P(getting a number less than or equal to 4)?","options":["2/3","1/3","1/2","5/6"],"answer":0,"explain":"Favourable outcomes are 1, 2, 3, 4 giving 4/6 = 2/3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One card is drawn from a well-shuffled deck of 52 cards. What is P(drawing an ace)?","options":["1/13","1/4","4/13","1/52"],"answer":0,"explain":"There are 4 aces among 52 cards, so P = 4/52 = 1/13."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A box has 3 blue, 2 white and 4 red marbles. What is P(drawing a red marble)?","options":["4/9","2/9","1/3","5/9"],"answer":0,"explain":"4 red marbles out of 9 total gives 4/9."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two coins are tossed together. What is the total number of equally likely outcomes?","answer":4,"explain":"The outcomes are (H,H), (H,T), (T,H) and (T,T)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two dice are thrown together. What is P(the sum is 13)?","options":["0","1/36","5/36","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"No pair of numbers from 1-6 on each die can sum to 13, so this is an impossible event."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If P(E) = 0.62, what is P(not E)?","answer":0.38,"explain":"Complementary events sum to 1, so P(not E) = 1 − 0.62 = 0.38."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A carton has 100 shirts: 88 good, 8 minor-defect, 4 major-defect. What is P(shirt acceptable to Sujatha, who rejects only major-defect shirts), as a decimal?","answer":0.96,"explain":"Sujatha accepts good plus minor-defect shirts: 88 + 8 = 96 out of 100."}],"boss":{"name":"The Oracle of Chance","taunt":"You've solved every equation the Atlas could throw at you — now let's see if you can call the odds before I deal the cards."}}]}];